#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/artists/artist_bairei.tres
msgid "Kōno Bairei"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/artists/artist_bairei.tres
msgid ""
"Kōno Bairei was a painter, book illustrator, and art educator who became a central figure in the Kyōto art scene during the Meiji period.\n"
"\n"
"He specialized in [url=art_style:kachō-e]kachō-e[/url], with a refined and highly observational approach to traditional nature subjects. He is best known for his masterful woodblock-printed books, which featured incredibly detailed and lifelike studies of wildlife.\n"
"\n"
"Beyond his own artwork, he established the Kyōto Prefectural School of Painting in 1880."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/artists/artist_bartlett.tres
msgid "Charles Bartlett"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/artists/artist_bartlett.tres
msgid ""
"Charles Bartlett was an English painter and one of the few Western artists who created ukiyo-e prints. He created over 30 [url=art_style:shin-hanga]shin-hanga[/url] designs while working with [url=artist:Shōzaburō Watanabe]Shōzaburō Watanabe[/url].\n"
"\n"
"Bartlett studied at the Royal Academy of Arts in London as well as the Académie Julian in Paris. He embarked on a tour of Asia in 1913, and arrived in Japan in 1915, where he met Shōzaburō, learned to design ukiyo-e, and produced prints depicting scenes from India and Japan.\n"
"\n"
"He settled in Hawaii in 1917, but visited Japan again in 1919 to produce additional prints. He passed away in Honolulu in 1940."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/artists/artist_chikanobu.tres
msgid "Toyohara Chikanobu"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/artists/artist_chikanobu.tres
msgid ""
"Toyohara Chikanobu was a printmaker of the Meiji period who began his career depicting battle scenes, but is best known for his detailed illustrations of the Imperial family and the changing fashions of Japanese society.\n"
"\n"
"His works documented traditional aristocratic customs that were slowly fading, while simultaneously capturing the introduction of Western clothing and modern hairstyles, often in a triptych format."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/artists/artist_eisen.tres
msgid "Keisai Eisen"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/artists/artist_eisen.tres
msgid ""
"Keisai Eisen was an ukiyo-e artist specializing in [url=art_style:bijin-ga]bijin-ga[/url], although he produced landscape prints, and [url=art_style:surimono]surimono[/url] as well.\n"
"\n"
"Eisen is most renowned for his [url=art_style:bijin-ga]bijin-ga[/url], which portrayed the subjects as more worldly than those depicted by earlier artists, replacing their grace and elegance with a less studied sensuality. He produced many portraits and full-length studies depicting the fashions of the time.\n"
"\n"
"He was also known as Ikeda Eisen, and wrote under the name of Ippitsuan."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/artists/artist_eishou.tres
msgid "Narazaki Eishō"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/artists/artist_eishou.tres
msgid ""
"Narazaki Eishō was a printmaker active at the turn of the 20th century. He studied under Kobayashi Eitaku and worked at the Printing Bureau of the Ministry of Finance in Tōkyō.\n"
"\n"
"He published several [url=art_style:shin-hanga]shin-hanga[/url] prints with [url=artist:Shōzaburō Watanabe]Shōzaburō Watanabe[/url], including his best-known work, Inside Asakusa Kannon Temple."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/artists/artist_eizan.tres
msgid "Kikugawa Eizan"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/artists/artist_eizan.tres
msgid ""
"Kikugawa Eizan was a woodblock printmaker famous for his [url=art_style:bijin-ga]bijin-ga[/url] prints, following in the footsteps of the great master [url=artist:Kitagawa Utamaro]Kitagawa Utamaro[/url].\n"
"\n"
"He first studied with his father, a member of the [url=art_school:Kanō School]Kanō school[/url], and subsequently with Suzuki Nanrei, of the [url=art_school:Shijō School]Shijō school[/url]. He is known for his masterful triptychs and his detailed depictions of women engaged in everyday cultural activities, fashion, and seasonal events.\n"
"\n"
"His prolific output helped sustain the immense popularity of beauty prints in the early 19th century and set the aesthetic stage for the next generation of artists."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/artists/artist_gakutei.tres
msgid "Yashima Gakutei"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/artists/artist_gakutei.tres
msgid ""
"Yashima Gakutei was an ukiyo-e artist and poet. He studied under both [url=artist:Katsushika Hokusai]Hokusai[/url] and [url=artist:Totoya Hokkei]Totoya Hokkei[/url].\n"
"\n"
"He was the illegitimate son of a samurai known as Hirata who served under the Tokugawa shogunate. Gakutei's mother later married into the Yashima clan, explaining the artist's name.\n"
"\n"
"Gakutei is best known for his [url=art_style:surimono]surimono[/url] works and humorous kyōka poetry. The former often featured extreme precision and elaborate embossing. He was also responsible for a Japanese translation of the famous Chinese novel Journey to the West, for which he also completed illustrations."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#. Property: text
#: art/artists/artist_gekkou.tres
#: art/quiz/art_quiz.tscn
msgid "Ogata Gekkō"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/artists/artist_gekkou.tres
msgid ""
"Ogata Gekkō was a self-taught artist who helped transition Japanese woodblock printing from the traditional ukiyo-e of the Edo period into the modern era.\n"
"\n"
"Unlike most printmakers of his time, he did not train under an established school but instead developed a unique style that closely mimicked the soft brushstrokes and wash effects of traditional Japanese painting.\n"
"\n"
"He is known for blending painterly techniques with classic subjects like landscapes, historical epics, and scenes of the Sino-Japanese War.\n"
"\n"
"His work won multiple international awards, and helped elevate the global status of Japanese printmaking."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/artists/artist_ginkou.tres
msgid "Adachi Ginkō"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/artists/artist_ginkou.tres
msgid ""
"Adachi Ginkō was an ukiyo-e artist of the Utagawa school. He worked in a variety of genres, including [url=art_style:bijin-ga]bijin-ga[/url], [url=art_style:yakusha-e]yakusha-e[/url], landscapes, book illustrations, and satirical works.\n"
"\n"
"He produced many prints of contemporary events such as the Satsuma Rebellion and the First Sino-Japanese War. His most popular work is the series of triptychs titled Pictorial Outline of Japanese History, published from 1885 to 1889.\n"
"\n"
"He is also famous for printing a satirical cartoon that portrayed the Meiji Emperor as a skeleton (as a pun on the name of the publisher), for which he was imprisoned for a year."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/artists/artist_goyou.tres
msgid "Hashiguchi Goyō"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/artists/artist_goyou.tres
msgid ""
"Hashiguchi Goyō was a painter and [url=art_style:shin-hanga]shin-hanga[/url] artist active in the early 20th century.\n"
"\n"
"He studied at the Tōkyō School of Fine Arts, graduating best in his class in 1905, and published many articles about the history and techniques of ukiyo-e. His first commission was designing the layout and illustrations for Natsume Sōseki's famous novel I Am a Cat.\n"
"\n"
"He only released 14 woodblock prints during his lifetime, but they are often regarded as masterpieces of the genre."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/artists/artist_hanko.tres
msgid "Kajita Hanko"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/artists/artist_hanko.tres
msgid ""
"Kajita Hanko was a Meiji-period painter and print artist. He specialized in novel and newspaper illustrations, and most of his known work is in the [url=art_style:bijin-ga]bijin-ga[/url] genre.\n"
"\n"
"He was born the son of a metal engraver and studied under Nabeta Gyokuei of the [url=art_school:Shijō School]Shijō School[/url], as well as [url=artist:Ohara Koson]Ohara Koson[/url]. Later in life he participated in forming several arts organizations and taught at several art schools.\n"
"\n"
"Hanko married the writer Kitada Usurai and died of tuberculosis at age 47."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/artists/artist_harunobu.tres
msgid "Suzuki Harunobu"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/artists/artist_harunobu.tres
msgid ""
"Suzuki Harunobu was a prolific ukiyo-e artist and innovator, the first to produce full-color prints in 1765, rendering obsolete the former modes of two- and three-color prints. He left behind over a thousand different prints.\n"
"\n"
"He is primarily known for his [url=art_style:bijin-ga]bijin-ga[/url] prints, often depicting women as unusually youthful. When asked why he does not paint actors, as was common at the time, he allegedly responded \"Why should I paint pictures of such trash as Kabuki actors?\".\n"
"\n"
"Unlike most ukiyo-e artists, Harunobu used his real name rather than an artist name."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/artists/artist_hasui.tres
msgid "Kawase Hasui"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/artists/artist_hasui.tres
msgid ""
"Kawase Hasui was a leading figure in [url=art_style:shin-hanga]shin-hanga[/url], the 20th-century movement to revitalize traditional woodblock printing by incorporating Western realism.\n"
"\n"
"He is known for his elaborate detailing and atmospheric depictions of seasons, weather, and light, often capturing tranquil landscapes and townscapes bathed in snow, rain, or evening glow.\n"
"\n"
"In addition to over 600 prints, he left behind many watercolor paintings, as well as some oil paintings, hanging scrolls, and painted folding screens.\n"
"\n"
"You can watch the master at work in [url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BQmF3HHyWwI]this YouTube video[/url]."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/artists/artist_hirokage.tres
msgid "Utagawa Hirokage"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/artists/artist_hirokage.tres
msgid ""
"Utagawa Hirokage was a printmaker active in the mid-19th century and a notable student of the great landscape master [url=artist:Utagawa Hiroshige]Utagawa Hiroshige[/url].\n"
"\n"
"While he studied the techniques of his mentor, Hirokage is best known for injecting the traditional landscape genre with slapstick humor and comic chaos. Many of his works are affectionate parodies of the famous scenes designed by his teacher.\n"
"\n"
"He is also known as Ichiyūsai Hirokage."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: custom_date
#: art/artists/artist_hirokage.tres
#: art/artists/artist_yoshitsugu.tres
msgid "mid-19th century"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/artists/artist_hirosada.tres
msgid "Konishi Hirosada"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/artists/artist_hirosada.tres
msgid ""
"Konishi Hirosada was the most prolific Ōsaka-based designer of ukiyo-e woodblock prints during the late Edo period. He specialized in [url=art_style:yakusha-e]yakusha-e[/url] and is particularly known for his diptychs and triptychs, as well as many ōkubi-e portraits of the leading actors on the Ōsaka kabuki stage during his day.\n"
"\n"
"He is also known as Gosōtei Hirosada, Gorakutei Hirosada and Utagawa Hirosada. Some scholars suspect he was also Utagawa Sadahiro."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#. Property: text
#: art/artists/artist_hiroshige.tres
#: art/quiz/art_quiz.tscn
msgid "Utagawa Hiroshige"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/artists/artist_hiroshige.tres
msgid ""
"Utagawa Hiroshige was a master printmaker renowned for his atmospheric approach to the ukiyo-e landscape. He captured the beauty of everyday travel, changing seasons, and distinct weather conditions like rain, mist, and snow.\n"
"\n"
"He is best known for the many editions of his series [url=art_series:53 Stations of the Tōkaidō]53 Stations of the Tōkaidō[/url] and [url=art_series:100 Famous Views of Edo]100 Famous Views of Edo[/url]. Hiroshige's approach was more poetic and ambient than most of his contemporaries, and featured subtle use of color and extensive use of bokashi (color gradation).\n"
"\n"
"His works were widely exported and profoundly influenced European artists, with Vincent van Gogh famously creating direct oil painting copies of his prints."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/artists/artist_hiroshige_2.tres
msgid "Utagawa Hiroshige II"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/artists/artist_hiroshige_2.tres
msgid ""
"Utagawa Hiroshige II was a prominent printmaker who studied under [url=artist:Utagawa Hiroshige]the original Hiroshige[/url], eventually marrying his teacher's daughter, inheriting the name, and continuing his legacy in landscape art.\n"
"\n"
"He was so adept at mirroring Hiroshige's style that he was trusted to complete some of the final works of his predecessor, and early scholars frequently confused their designs.\n"
"\n"
"Later in his career, he moved to the port city of Yokohama and became an early pioneer of [url=art_style:Yokohama-e]Yokohama-e[/url], a genre documenting the influx of foreign cultures, modern architecture, and international trade that marked the opening of Japan."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/artists/artist_hiroshige_3.tres
msgid "Utagawa Hiroshige III"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/artists/artist_hiroshige_3.tres
msgid ""
"Utagawa Hiroshige III was a prominent printmaker who documented the rapid modernization of Japan during the Meiji period. He studied under [url=artist:Utagawa Hiroshige]the original Hiroshige[/url], but shifted his focus toward [url=art_style:kaika-e]kaika-e[/url], which captured the technological and cultural transformation of the era.\n"
"\n"
"Although he never achieved widespread popularity, his works like the [url=art_series:Products of Greater Japan]Products of Greater Japan[/url] series provide a vivid record of a society undergoing rapid industrialization."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/artists/artist_hokkei.tres
msgid "Totoya Hokkei"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/artists/artist_hokkei.tres
msgid ""
"Totoya Hokkei was a Japanese artist best known for his ukiyo-e prints. He was one of [url=artist:Katsushika Hokusai]Hokusai[/url]'s first and best-known students, and worked in a variety of styles and genres.\n"
"\n"
"In addition to mass-produced prints, he produced over 800 [url=art_style:surimono]surimono[/url] and illustrated about a hundred books.\n"
"\n"
"His work also appeared under the art names Aoigazono, Aoigaoka, and Kyōsai."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/artists/artist_hokuju.tres
msgid "Shotei Hokuju"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/artists/artist_hokuju.tres
msgid ""
"Shotei Hokuju was an ukiyo-e designer active roughly between 1790 and 1820. His birth and death dates are unknown.\n"
"\n"
"Hokuju was a student of [url=artist:Katsushika Hokusai]Hokusai[/url], and focused mainly on landscapes. Some of his works show an unconventional style likely inspired by Western techniques."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: custom_date
#: art/artists/artist_hokuju.tres
msgid "early 19th century"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#. Property: text
#: art/artists/artist_hokusai.tres
#: art/quiz/art_quiz.tscn
msgid "Katsushika Hokusai"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/artists/artist_hokusai.tres
msgid ""
"Katsushika Hokusai was a master printmaker who fundamentally transformed the Japanese art of ukiyo-e, from a focus on portraits of actors and courtesans to include landscapes, plants, animals, and scenes of daily life.\n"
"\n"
"He is known for integrating traditional Japanese techniques with Western approaches to perspective and imported pigments like Prussian blue. His best known series is [url=art_series:36 Views of Mount Fuji]36 Views of Mount Fuji[/url], which includes the famous [url=art_piece:great wave off kanagawa]Great Wave off Kanagawa[/url].\n"
"\n"
"His works went on to influence Western artists like Vincent van Gogh and Claude Monet, and he is arguably the best known ukiyo-e artist today."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/artists/artist_ikkei.tres
msgid "Shōsai Ikkei"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/artists/artist_ikkei.tres
msgid ""
"Shōsai Ikkei was a Meiji-era woodblock print artist and student of [url=artist:Hiroshige III]Hiroshige III[/url].\n"
"\n"
"Active in the 1860s–1870s, he is best known for [url=art_style:kaika-e]kaika-e[/url] depicting Tōkyō's rapid modernization, blending traditional ukiyo-e style with scenes of railways, city life, and cultural change.\n"
"\n"
"His work includes the 48 Views of Famous Places in Tōkyō series and the Comic Scenes of 36 Famous Places in Tōkyō series."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: custom_date
#: art/artists/artist_ikkei.tres
msgid "Meiji era"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#. Property: text
#: art/artists/artist_keith.tres
#: art/quiz/art_quiz.tscn
msgid "Elizabeth Keith"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/artists/artist_keith.tres
msgid ""
"Elizabeth Keith was a Scottish woodblock print artist and writer. She has the rare distinction among ukiyo-e artists of being both Western and a woman.\n"
"\n"
"She traveled to Tōkyō at the age of 28 and remained there for nine years, meeting [url=artist:Shōzaburō Watanabe]Shōzaburō Watanabe[/url] who convinced her to publish her paintings as [url=art_style:shin-hanga]shin-hanga[/url] prints.\n"
"\n"
"Keith's work is centered around Asian life and culture, based on her travels around Japan, China, Korea, and the Philippines. It often features landscapes, cultural rituals, and people in traditional and common dress.\n"
"\n"
"She was elected a Fellow of the Royal Society of Arts in London in the 1930s."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/artists/artist_kiyochika.tres
msgid "Kobayashi Kiyochika"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/artists/artist_kiyochika.tres
msgid ""
"Kobayashi Kiyochika was a printmaker best known for his newspaper illustrations. He was active in the Meiji period, and documented the rapid modernization and Westernization of Japan, depicting the influx of new technologies and shifting urban landscapes.\n"
"\n"
"His specialty was night scenes illuminated by sources within the composition, but his best known works are propagandistic caricature prints, often depicting foreigners as foolish, and modern products as aesthetically inferior.\n"
"\n"
"Some consider his work to be the last significant example of traditional ukiyo-e before the [url=art_style:shin-hanga]shin-hanga[/url] revival."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/artists/artist_kiyonaga.tres
msgid "Torii Kiyonaga"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/artists/artist_kiyonaga.tres
msgid ""
"Torii Kiyonaga was a master printmaker active in the 18th century. Although not biologically related to the Torii family, he was adopted and eventually became the head of the [url=art_school:Torii School]Torii school[/url].\n"
"\n"
"While his school had traditionally focused on producing promotional materials and signboards for the kabuki theater, Kiyonaga expanded his focus and achieved his greatest acclaim in the genre of [url=art_style:bijin-ga]bijin-ga[/url], or pictures of beautiful women. He is known for depicting tall, more mature women than his predecessor Harunobu, whose prints often depict women who seem younger and thinner."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/artists/artist_kogyo.tres
msgid "Tsukioka Kōgyo"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/artists/artist_kogyo.tres
msgid ""
"Tsukioka Kōgyo, sometimes called Sakamaki Kōgyo, was a Japanese artist of the Meiji era. He was a student and adopted son of [url=artist:Tsukioka Yoshitoshi]Tsukioka Yoshitoshi[/url], and also studied with [url=artist:Ogata Gekkō]Ogata Gekkō[/url].\n"
"\n"
"Although Kōgyo sometimes painted other subjects, for most of his career he made pictures of Japanese Noh theatre, either as large-scale paintings or colored woodblock prints. Many of the latter were published in series and sold as multi-volume sets."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/artists/artist_koitsu.tres
msgid "Tsuchiya Kōitsu"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/artists/artist_koitsu.tres
msgid ""
"Tsuchiya Kōitsu was a prominent artist of the [url=art_style:shin-hanga]shin-hanga[/url] movement. He trained under [url=artist:Kobayashi Kiyochika]Kobayashi Kiyochika[/url] for 19 years, and initially focused on works depicting scenes from the First Sino-Japanese War. Later in life, he specialized in highly atmospheric landscape prints.\n"
"\n"
"He is best known for his evocative evening and night scenes, masterfully capturing the warm glow of lanterns against snow, rain, and mist. His most celebrated works focus on serene temples, shrines, and rural settings.\n"
"\n"
"By combining Western approaches to lighting with classic Japanese subjects, his prints became highly popular both domestically and abroad, capturing the beauty of historic sites during a period of rapid modernization."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/artists/artist_kokunimasa.tres
msgid "Utagawa Kokunimasa"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/artists/artist_kokunimasa.tres
msgid ""
"Utagawa Kokunimasa, son of Kunimasa IV, was a prolific woodblock print artist who worked across the Meiji, Taishō and Shōwa periods. Many of his prints are sensō-e, or \"war prints\", that depicted the Sino-Japanese (1894-1895) and Russo-Japanese (1904-1905) wars, and were used as propaganda.\n"
"\n"
"He also published works under the names Baido, Ryukei, and Kunimasa V, although another [url=art_school:Utagawa School]Utagawa school[/url] member, Takeuchi Yanagi, used the Kunimasa V name as well."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/artists/artist_koson.tres
msgid "Ohara Koson"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/artists/artist_koson.tres
msgid ""
"Ohara Koson was a printmaker and painter who became the leading master of [url=art_style:kachō-e]kachō-e[/url], or bird-and-flower pictures, during the 20th century [url=art_style:shin-hanga]shin-hanga[/url] movement. He also used the names Ohara Hōson and Ohara Shōson.\n"
"\n"
"Koson revitalized the traditional nature print by combining acute biological observation with soft, painterly woodblock techniques. He is best known for his highly detailed depictions of wildlife, such as eagles in the snow, leaping koi, and delicate songbirds among blossoms.\n"
"\n"
"His works were heavily exported and achieved commercial success in the United States and Europe, playing a major role in shaping Western appreciation for Japanese naturalism."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/artists/artist_koto.tres
msgid "Ōkura Kōtō"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/artists/artist_koto.tres
msgid ""
"Ōkura Kōtō was a student of [url=artist:Ogata Gekkō]Ogata Gekkō[/url] and the son of the well-known woodblock carver Ōkura Hanbei. He created a number of Sino-Japanese and Russo-Japanese sensō-e (war prints), including a double triptych depicting a panoramic view of the victory at Port Arthur.\n"
"\n"
"In 1906, he is mentioned as a member of the art society Edokko-kai, which promoted \"the tastes of the Edokko\". Edokko was a slang term for Edo (present-day Tōkyō) natives, thought to be assertive, straightforward, cheerful, and mercantile."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/artists/artist_kouhou.tres
msgid "Shōda Kōhō"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/artists/artist_kouhou.tres
msgid ""
"Shōda Kōhō was an artist in the [url=art_style:shin-hanga]shin-hanga[/url] movement of the early 20th century. He was a pupil of [url=artist:Ogata Gekkō]Ogata Gekkō[/url] and became known both for his paintings and shin-hanga woodblock prints, as well as later for his satirical poetry.\n"
"\n"
"Kōhō's most celebrated work is his Night Scenes series, which depicts evening landscapes in subdued sepia and blue tones. In addition to the Night Scenes, Shōda also contributed to the production of tanzaku-format prints. These narrow, vertical formats reflected the architectural spaces of traditional Japanese houses, where posts often provided the only surfaces for hanging pictures.\n"
"\n"
"Around the age of thirty, he withdrew from the world of visual arts and was mainly active as a poet of humorous Kyōka and Kyōshi verse under the pen names Unnoya Hachisuke and Renji Kōshi."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/artists/artist_kunichika.tres
msgid "Toyohara Kunichika"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/artists/artist_kunichika.tres
msgid ""
"Toyohara Kunichika was an ukiyo-e artist, primarily known for his [url=art_style:yakusha-e]yakusha-e[/url] prints.\n"
"\n"
"He became an apprentice of [url=artist:Utagawa Kunisada]Utagawa Kunisada[/url] at 13 years old, and later counted [url=artist:Toyohara Chikanobu]Toyohara Chikanobu[/url] among his pupils.\n"
"\n"
"Kunichika led a colorful life, frequently partying at the pleasure district and even appearing in burlesque shows. At one point he published a parody print depicting a fight at a theater, for which the subjects ransacked his house. After the death of his daughter, his alcoholism intensified and likely led to his death."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/artists/artist_kunisada.tres
msgid "Utagawa Kunisada"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/artists/artist_kunisada.tres
msgid ""
"Utagawa Kunisada was the most popular, prolific and commercially successful designer of ukiyo-e woodblock prints in 19th-century Japan. In his own time, his reputation far exceeded that of his contemporaries [url=artist:Katsushika Hokusai]Hokusai[/url], [url=artist:Utagawa Hiroshige]Hiroshige[/url] and [url=artist:Kuniyoshi]Kuniyoshi[/url], who are much more famous today.\n"
"\n"
"He was also known as Utagawa Toyokuni III."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/artists/artist_kuniyoshi.tres
msgid "Utagawa Kuniyoshi"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/artists/artist_kuniyoshi.tres
msgid ""
"Utagawa Kuniyoshi was a leading master of the [url=art_school:Utagawa school]Utagawa school[/url], best known for his dramatic depictions of legendary heroes ([url=art_style:musha-e]musha-e[/url]), mythical beasts, and spectral encounters, most notably in his breakthrough series [url=art_series:The 108 Heroes of the Popular Water Margin]The 108 Heroes of the Popular Water Margin[/url].\n"
"\n"
"Beyond legendary subjects, he produced satirical caricature prints, and many affectionate studies of cats.\n"
"\n"
"His bold, action-oriented compositions went on to heavily influence modern Japanese tattoo art and manga."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/artists/artist_kyosai.tres
msgid "Kawanabe Kyōsai"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/artists/artist_kyosai.tres
msgid ""
"Kawanabe Kyōsai was a highly individualistic artist who bridged the gap between traditional Japanese painting and modern caricature during the tumultuous transition from the Edo to the Meiji period.\n"
"\n"
"After training in both the [url=art_school:Kanō School]Kanō school[/url] and the [url=art_school:Utagawa School]Utagawa school[/url], he developed a fiercely independent and often satirical style.\n"
"\n"
"He is best known for his vivid, energetic, and sometimes grotesque depictions of folklore, particularly his chaotic scenes of ghosts, demons, and animals acting like humans.\n"
"\n"
"He earned a reputation as a master of kyōga, or crazy pictures, with his humorous and exaggerated figures serving as a direct precursor to modern manga."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#. Property: text
#: art/artists/artist_max.tres
#: main_menu/credits_list.tscn
msgid "Max Shawabkeh"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/artists/artist_max.tres
msgid ""
"Yep, that's me. You're probably wondering how I got here.\n"
"\n"
"I made this game, but I'm an amateur artist at best. I made a few of the illustrations, mostly by repainting existing ukiyo-e pieces which were still under copyright."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: custom_date
#: art/artists/artist_max.tres
msgid "1989 - Present"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/artists/artist_moronobu.tres
msgid "Hishikawa Moronobu"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/artists/artist_moronobu.tres
msgid ""
"Hishikawa Moronobu is widely considered the first great master and principal founder of ukiyo-e in the late 17th century. He transformed woodblock printing from simple text illustrations into highly prized standalone artworks.\n"
"\n"
"He is known for popularizing the foundational themes of the floating world, particularly [url=art_style:bijin-ga]bijin-ga[/url] and vibrant scenes of the bustling pleasure quarters in Edo."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/artists/artist_mredraccoon.tres
msgid "M. Red Raccoon"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/artists/artist_mredraccoon.tres
msgid ""
"M. Red Raccoon is a Spanish illustrator based in Japan whose work focuses on historical art styles, particularly printmaking aesthetics. Enthusiast of art history, she draws inspiration from traditional techniques, visual conventions, and the ways artists of the past interpreted the world around them.\n"
"\n"
"Ukiyo-e and sumi-e became particularly important influences on her artistic language, eventually playing a part in her decision to leave Spain and settle in Japan. Living there has allowed her to continue exploring these traditions more closely while developing her own approach to them from the eyes of a Westerner.\n"
"\n"
"Rather than simply reproducing historical works, she enjoys applying their visual language to unexpected subjects, from folklore and landscapes to fantasy and fictional worlds. Her work combines historical references with contemporary illustration, imagining how modern or imaginary scenes might have been interpreted through the aesthetics of another time."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: custom_date
#: art/artists/artist_mredraccoon.tres
msgid "1994 - Present"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/artists/artist_oukyo.tres
msgid "Maruyama Ōkyo"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/artists/artist_oukyo.tres
msgid ""
"Maruyama Ōkyo was an artist active in the late 18th century. He studied artworks from Chinese, Japanese and Western sources while living in Kyōto.\n"
"\n"
"He apprenticed under masters from the [url=art_school:Kanō school]Kanō school[/url], but later founded the Maruyama school which subsequently evolved into the [url=art_school:Shijō school]Shijō school[/url].\n"
"\n"
"He is known for mixing Western naturalism with Eastern decorative design. He followed the Eastern tradition in depicting objects with very little setting, often featuring a single subject on a plain background. His style, despite its popularity, was criticized by contemporary artists for being too realistic."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/artists/artist_raijirou.tres
msgid "Ichikawa Raijirō"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/artists/artist_raijirou.tres
msgid ""
"Ichikawa Raijirō was an editor, cartographer, and publisher of the early Meiji period based in Tōkyō.\n"
"\n"
"He is mainly known for an impressive world map, but he also published several illustrated compendiums."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: custom_date
#: art/artists/artist_raijirou.tres
msgid "active 1876-1893"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/artists/artist_sadahide.tres
msgid "Utagawa Sadahide"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/artists/artist_sadahide.tres
msgid ""
"Utagawa Sadahide was a member of the [url=art_school:Utagawa School]Utagawa school[/url] best known for his [url=art_style:Yokohama-e]Yokohama-e[/url] prints, pictures of foreigners in Yokohama in the 1860s, a period when he was a best-selling artist. His work was chosen by the Tokugawa shogunate's delegation to be displayed at the International Exposition of 1867 in Paris.\n"
"\n"
"His earlier works included [url=art_style:bijin-ga]bijin-ga[/url] and [url=art_style:musha-e]musha-e[/url] prints, as well as book illustrations, maps, and depictions of Ainu people, an ethnic minority inhabiting Northern Japan."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/artists/artist_shigenobu.tres
msgid "Kawashima Shigenobu"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/artists/artist_shigenobu.tres
msgid ""
"Little is known for certain about Kawashima Shigenobu, other than that he was an Edo period artist active during the first half of the 18th century, and that he produced book illustrations and painted scrolls. He may have used the names Ryukado and Itchido, and may have been a pupil of Nishikawa Sukenobu. He allegedly lived in Kyōto, on Kawaramachi street.\n"
"\n"
"His most recognized work is a pair of huge, intricately painted scrolls, [url=art_piece:Pictures of Excursions Through the Famous Sites of the Capital]Pictures of Excursions Through the Famous Sites of the Capital[/url], fragments of which were used as the basis for the Haven in Worlds Upon The Wind (as well as the game's narrative trailer)."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: custom_date
#: art/artists/artist_shigenobu.tres
msgid "mid 18th century"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/artists/artist_shinsai.tres
msgid "Ryūryūkyo Shinsai"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/artists/artist_shinsai.tres
msgid ""
"Ryūryūkyo Shinsai was a printmaker and an alleged pupil of [url=artist:Katsushika Hokusai]Katsushika Hokusai[/url].\n"
"\n"
"While Shinsai illustrated books and designed single-sheet prints throughout his career, he is best known for his [url=art_style:surimono]surimono[/url]."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/artists/artist_shousou.tres
msgid "Mishima Shōsō"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/artists/artist_shousou.tres
msgid ""
"Mishima Shōsō was a painter and ukiyo-e artist from Edo, a member of the [url=art_school:Shijō School]Shijō school[/url].\n"
"\n"
"He started his career with historical scenes and [url=art_style:kachō-e]kachō-e[/url], but is better known for his later book illustrations."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/artists/artist_shoutei.tres
msgid "Takahashi Shōtei"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/artists/artist_shoutei.tres
msgid ""
"Takahashi Shōtei, also known as Hiroaki, was a pioneering printmaker and the first artist recruited to launch the [url=art_style:shin-hanga]shin-hanga[/url] movement in the early 20th century.\n"
"\n"
"He is known for his serene depictions of rural landscapes and traditional village life, and excelled at capturing distinct weather conditions and nighttime scenes.\n"
"\n"
"By 1923, he had produced roughly 500 designs, but the devastating Great Kanto earthquake and subsequent fires destroyed the workshop of his publisher and all his original woodblocks. Undeterred, he spent the rest of his career meticulously recreating lost masterpieces and designing hundreds of new prints."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/artists/artist_shouun.tres
msgid "Yamamoto Shōun"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/artists/artist_shouun.tres
msgid ""
"Yamamoto Shōun, also known as Matsutani Shōun, was a print designer, painter, and illustrator. He was born in Kōchi into a family of retainers of the shōgun and was given the name Mosaburō.\n"
"\n"
"As a teenager, he studied with the [url=art_school:Kanō School]Kanō school[/url]. At age 17 he moved to Tōkyō, and by age 20 was employed as an illustrator for Fugoku Gahō, a pictorial magazine dealing with the sights in and around Tōkyō.\n"
"\n"
"He is known for [url=art_style:bijin-ga]bijin-ga[/url] woodblock prints and his contributions to [url=art_style:shin-hanga]shin-hanga[/url], though in his later career he primarily produced paintings."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/artists/artist_shunkei.tres
msgid "Mori Shunkei"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/artists/artist_shunkei.tres
msgid ""
"Mori Shunkei was an Ōsaka-based woodblock print artist active in the early 19th century. Little is known about his life, other than that he trained under Mori Sosen (1747-1821) of the [url=art_school:Shijō School]Shijō school[/url].\n"
"\n"
"Shunkei is best-known for the e-hon (\"illustrated book\") Chuka Senzen (\"Selection of Insects at My Elbow\"), published in 1820, which contains 12 detailed illustrations of insects and plants, alternating with 12 Chinese verses."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: custom_date
#: art/artists/artist_shunkei.tres
msgid "active early 19th century"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/artists/artist_shunshou.tres
msgid "Katsukawa Shunshō"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/artists/artist_shunshou.tres
msgid ""
"Katsukawa Shunshō was a painter and printmaker. During his lifetime he was the leading artist of the [url=art_school:Katsukawa school]Katsukawa school[/url]. He studied under the school's founder Miyagawa Shunsui, and among his pupils counted [url=artist:Katsushika Hokusai]Katsushika Hokusai[/url].\n"
"\n"
"Shunshō is best known for introducing a new form of [url=art_style:yakusha-e]yakusha-e[/url], which depicted large, portrait-style heads and the insides of actors' dressing rooms. He was also one of the first to pioneer realistic depictions of actors; in Shunshō's prints, unlike in the works of the [url=art_school:Torii school]Torii school[/url], it was possible to distinguish not only the role, but also the actor portraying it."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/artists/artist_shuntei.tres
msgid "Katsukawa Shuntei"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/artists/artist_shuntei.tres
msgid ""
"Katsukawa Shuntei was a printmaker whose works span [url=art_style:yakushe-e]yakushe-e[/url], [url=art_style:musha-e]musha-e[/url], [url=art_style:bijin-ga]bijin-ga[/url], [url=art_style:surimono]surimono[/url], landscapes, and book illustrations.\n"
"\n"
"He is best known for battle scene triptychs, which were unlike the single-sheet warriror prints of the time.\n"
"\n"
"Relatively few of his works survive."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/artists/artist_souzan.tres
msgid "Itō Sōzan"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/artists/artist_souzan.tres
msgid ""
"Itō Sōzan was a [url=art_style:shin-hanga]shin-hanga[/url] artist active around the turn of the 20th century, though little of his biography is known.\n"
"\n"
"In his early career he designed reproductions of [url=artist:Hiroshige]Hiroshige[/url] and [url=artist:Katsushika Hokusai]Hokusai[/url] prints for foreign markets, while later he was known mainly for his [url=art_style:kachō-e]kachō-e[/url] prints.\n"
"\n"
"He is also known to have collaborated with [url=artist:Takahashi Shōtei]Takahashi Shōtei[/url] on several prints, which was very uncommon for ukiyo-e artists."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: custom_date
#: art/artists/artist_souzan.tres
msgid "late 19th century - early 20th century"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/artists/artist_tankei.tres
msgid "Inoue Tankei"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/artists/artist_tankei.tres
msgid ""
"Inoue Tankei, also known as Inoue Yasuji, was an ukiyo-e artist active during the Meiji era.\n"
"\n"
"Born in Asakusa, Edo (present-day Tōkyō), he briefly apprenticed under [url=artist:Tsukioka Yoshitoshi]Tsukioka Yoshitoshi[/url] before becoming the main disciple of [url=artist:Kobayashi Kiyochika]Kobayashi Kiyochika[/url] at age 15.\n"
"\n"
"Most of his works deal with Tōkyō, and specifically his home neighborhood of Asakusa, but he also depicted sumo wrestlers and designed illustrations for comic novels. His style is based on Kiyochika's, defined by subdued color, the use of white space, and eschewing the traditional thick black outlines of ukiyo-e.\n"
"\n"
"He died of beriberi at age 26."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/artists/artist_toshikata.tres
msgid "Mizuno Toshikata"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/artists/artist_toshikata.tres
msgid ""
"Mizuno Toshikata was an ukiyo-e artist and painter active during the Meiji period. He apprenticed from age 13 under [url=artist:Tsukioka Yoshitoshi]Tsukioka Yoshitoshi[/url], and also studied traditional and ceramic painting.\n"
"\n"
"His works include [url=art_style:rekishi-ga]rekishi-ga[/url], [url=art_style:bijin-ga]bijin-ga[/url], newspaper illustrations, and sensō-e (war pictures) depicting the First Sino-Japanese War.\n"
"\n"
"Toshikata worked during the decline of traditional ukiyo-e, and passed away at age 43, before the [url=art_style:shin-hanga]shin-hanga[/url] revival. Nonetheless, it is easy to see a connection between the two in his style."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/artists/artist_toukou.tres
msgid "Oide Tōkō"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/artists/artist_toukou.tres
msgid ""
"Oide Tōkō was a painter in the Nanga style from Edo, a pupil of Todo Ryo'un.\n"
"\n"
"He received a silver medal at the Domestic Painting Competition in 1896 and exhibited his works at the National Industrial Exhibition and the Japan Art Association. Some of his works are in the Imperial Household collection.\n"
"\n"
"Unlike most of the artists featured here, he did not work in woodblock prints, but rather painted on silk."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/artists/artist_toyokuni.tres
msgid "Utagawa Toyokuni"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/artists/artist_toyokuni.tres
msgid ""
"Utagawa Toyokuni was a master printmaker, known in particular for his [url=art_style:yakusha-e]yakusha-e[/url] prints, which depicted kabuki actors in action, rather than as neutral portraits.\n"
"\n"
"He apprenticed under Utagawa Toyoharu, the founder of the [url=art_school:Utagawa School]Utagawa school[/url], and became the second head of the school. He is considered to be responsible for elevating it to the position of great fame and power it occupied for the rest of the 19th century.\n"
"\n"
"Among his many pupils were the great masters [url=artist:Utagawa Kunisada]Kunisada[/url] and [url=artist:Utagawa Kuniyoshi]Kuniyoshi[/url]."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/artists/artist_utamaro.tres
msgid "Kitagawa Utamaro"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/artists/artist_utamaro.tres
msgid ""
"Kitagawa Utamaro was one of the most highly regarded masters of ukiyo-e, fundamentally defining the golden age of [url=art_style:bijin-ga]bijin-ga[/url].\n"
"\n"
"He is best known for popularizing ōkubi-e, or large-headed portraits, which focused tightly on the faces and upper bodies of his subjects. Instead of relying on generic facial types, his close-up compositions captured subtle psychological states, distinct personalities, and the intimate daily routines of women from various social classes.\n"
"\n"
"When his prints reached Europe in the late 19th century, his masterful linework and domestic subject matter profoundly influenced Western artists, particularly the Impressionist painter Mary Cassatt."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/artists/artist_watanabe.tres
msgid "Shōzaburō Watanabe"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/artists/artist_watanabe.tres
msgid ""
"Shōzaburō Watanabe was a publisher who launched the [url=art_style:shin-hanga]shin-hanga[/url] movement that revitalized ukiyo-e in the early 20th century. Although he only created 2 prints himself, he hired and managed most of the best-known [url=art_style:shin-hanga]shin-hanga[/url] artists, including [url=artist:Kawase Hasui]Kawase Hasui[/url], [url=artist:Ohara Koson]Ohara Koson[/url], [url=artist:Tsuchiya Kōitsu]Tsuchiya Kōitsu[/url], [url=artist:Yoshida Hiroshi]Yoshida Hiroshi[/url], and [url=artist:Charles Bartlett]Charles Bartlett[/url].\n"
"\n"
"Watanabe employed expert carvers and printers, and commissioned artists to design prints that combined traditional Japanese techniques with elements of contemporary Western painting, such as perspective and shadows, exporting most of the prints to the United States and Europe.\n"
"\n"
"Much of his company's stockpile of both prints and their original woodblocks was destroyed in the Great Kantō earthquake of 1923. In the following years, new versions of many of these prints were created, using re-carved blocks. Typically, the re-issued \"post-quake\" prints included changes and revisions in the designs. After the end of World War II, his heirs continued the business, [url=https://hangasw.com/]which still operates[/url]."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/artists/artist_yoshida.tres
msgid "Yoshida Hiroshi"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/artists/artist_yoshida.tres
msgid ""
"Yoshida Hiroshi was a leading figure of the [url=art_style:shin-hanga]shin-hanga[/url] movement who blended Western oil painting techniques with traditional Japanese woodblock printing. He is distinct among his peers for his intense involvement in the entire production process, often supervising the carving and printing himself to achieve his exact vision.\n"
"\n"
"He was known as a mountain painter and spent about half of the year on sketching travels. As a mountaineer, he climbed the mountains of the Japanese Alps every summer and created his large paintings and woodblock prints after returning home.\n"
"\n"
"As a world traveler, Yoshida expanded the traditional subjects of Japanese prints to include global landmarks, creating renowned series depicting the Swiss Alps, the Grand Canyon, and the Taj Mahal."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/artists/artist_yoshimune.tres
msgid "Arai Yoshimune"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/artists/artist_yoshimune.tres
msgid ""
"Arai Yoshimune was a woodblock illustrator and print designer in the [url=art_style:shin-hanga]shin-hanga[/url] movement. He was the youngest son of Utagawa Yoshimune I, and after his father's death assumed the name Utagawa Yoshimune II. He was also a disciple of [url=artist:Tsukioka Yoshitoshi]Tsukioka Yoshitoshi[/url] under the name Tosetsu.\n"
"\n"
"In 1882, together with [url=artist:Tsukioka Yoshitoshi]Tsukioka Yoshitoshi[/url], he joined the staff of the Illustrated Free Newspaper, and later drew many illustrations for newspapers and magazines."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/artists/artist_yoshitoshi.tres
msgid "Tsukioka Yoshitoshi"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/artists/artist_yoshitoshi.tres
msgid ""
"Tsukioka Yoshitoshi was a printmaker working during the end of the Edo and the beginning of the Meiji period. He was a holdout of the traditional style when woodblock printing was rapidly being replaced by Western technologies like photography.\n"
"\n"
"In his early career he focused on extremely graphic and shocking depictions of violence, but later in life he transitioned to depicting historical subjects, folklore, and ghosts.\n"
"\n"
"Yoshitoshi suffered from severe depression, and despite being widely recognized as the foremost ukiyo-e master of the time, spent much of his short life destitute."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/artists/artist_yoshitsugu.tres
msgid "Yoshitsugu"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/artists/artist_yoshitsugu.tres
msgid "I was unable to find anything about this artist other than that he was from Ōsaka and made kabuki advertisement prints. The featured print appears to be his only surviving work."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/artists/artist_yuhan.tres
msgid "Itō Yūhan"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/artists/artist_yuhan.tres
msgid ""
"Itō Yūhan was a distinctive landscape artist of the [url=art_style:shin-hanga]shin-hanga[/url] movement who was active primarily in the 1930s.\n"
"\n"
"He is known for a unique printing technique that deliberately omitted the traditional black key block, which was normally used to create sharp outlines. By removing these rigid lines and relying on vivid pigments, delicate gradients, and soft shading, he achieved a luminous, atmospheric effect that closely resembled watercolor painting."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/artists/artist_zaisei.tres
msgid "Hara Zaisei"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/artists/artist_zaisei.tres
msgid ""
"Hara Zaisei was a Kyoto-based painter of the Hara school during the Edo period.\n"
"\n"
"Although he was said to have surpassed his father, Hara Zaichū, in skill, he passed away at the young age of 33, leaving behind few works."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/artists/artist_zeshin.tres
msgid "Shibata Zeshin"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/artists/artist_zeshin.tres
msgid ""
"Shibata Zeshin was arguably Japan's greatest lacquer painter, who also worked in printmaking. He is notable for his masterful use of urushi-e, a technique where lacquer is applied directly to paper.\n"
"\n"
"By mixing lacquer with traditional pigments, he gave his woodblock prints a stylish glossy finish and rich textures that mimicked expensive lacquerware. He applied this technique to nature studies, still lifes, and depictions of folklore.\n"
"\n"
"Although he was eventually appointed as an official Imperial Household Artist, and was celebrated internationally, his work was criticized in Japan for being too traditional during a time of immense innovation."
msgstr ""

