#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/pieces/piece_bakemono_akaguchi.tres
msgid "Depicts Akaguchi (\"red mouth\"), also known as Akashita (\"red tongue\"), a yōkai from the northwestern region of Aomori prefecture. It lives in a black cloud, visibly baring its komainu (Japanese lion-dog) face and large claws. Its face and claws are covered in thick blue hair. It is distinguished by its large red mouth and tongue."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: piece_name
#: art/pieces/piece_bakemono_doumo_koumo.tres
msgid "Dōmo Kōmo"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/pieces/piece_bakemono_doumo_koumo.tres
msgid "Depicts Dōmo Kōmo (\"both right and left\"), a yōkai with no clear origin story or behavior description."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: piece_name
#. Property: name
#: art/pieces/piece_bakemono_furaribi.tres
#: stage/hauntings/types/furaribi/haunting_furaribi.tres
msgid "Furaribi"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/pieces/piece_bakemono_furaribi.tres
msgid "Depicts Furaribi (\"aimless fire\"), a bird yōkai with a dog-like face enveloped in fire. They are said to be souls that have undergone suffering due to mistakes in burial rites, and are now unable to move on to the next life."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: piece_name
#: art/pieces/piece_bakemono_hyousube_shoukira.tres
msgid "Hyōsube and Shōkira"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: edits_description
#: art/pieces/piece_bakemono_hyousube_shoukira.tres
msgid "Only Shōkira was used, it was flipped, rotated, and a tongue was added to turn it into an Akaname."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/pieces/piece_bakemono_hyousube_shoukira.tres
msgid ""
"Depicts two yōkai, Hyōsube (left) and Shōkira (\"mole cricket spirit\", right).\n"
"\n"
"Hyōsube are child-sized river monsters from Kyūshū, similar to kappa, but living in underwater caves. They are said to come out at night and to love eating eggplants.\n"
"\n"
"Shōkira are nighttime demons that lurk on rooftops, spying through windows, doorways, or skylights to look for impious behavior."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: piece_name
#. Property: name
#: art/pieces/piece_bakemono_kamikiri.tres
#: stage/hauntings/types/kamikiri/haunting_kamikiri.tres
msgid "Kamikiri"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: edits_description
#: art/pieces/piece_bakemono_kamikiri.tres
msgid "The background was edited out and the white spots were toned down."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/pieces/piece_bakemono_kamikiri.tres
msgid ""
"Depicts Kamikiri (\"hair cutter\"), an insect-like yōkai with a long beak and scissor-like hands, used to cut unsuspecting victims' hair off without warning or motive. They were said to infest dark alleys, bedrooms, and, most commonly, bathrooms.\n"
"\n"
"Hair in the Edo period was an important status symbol, and cutting it was symbolic of becoming a monk or nun, so to have one's hair cut against one's will would have been a major ordeal."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: piece_name
#. Property: name
#: art/pieces/piece_bakemono_kappa.tres
#: stage/hauntings/types/kappa/haunting_kappa.tres
msgid "Kappa"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: edits_description
#: art/pieces/piece_bakemono_kappa.tres
msgid "The head was edited to remove the indentation, the face was edited to express suffering, and the background was removed."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/pieces/piece_bakemono_kappa.tres
msgid ""
"Depicts Kappa (\"river child\"), one of the most well-known yōkai. It is said to be an amphibian, about the size of a child, living in the lakes and rivers of Japan.\n"
"\n"
"The kappa has webbed feet and hands, scaly or slippery skin, often with a turtle-like shell on its back. Most depictions of kappa have a dish-like structure on the top of its head, filled with some kind of liquid. Kappa enjoy sumo wrestling, eating cucumbers, and causing trouble in various ways."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: piece_name
#: art/pieces/piece_bakemono_nukekubi.tres
msgid "Nukekubi and Inugami"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: edits_description
#: art/pieces/piece_bakemono_nukekubi.tres
msgid "The head was reattached, the image was cropped, the background was edited out, and an extra mouth was attached."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/pieces/piece_bakemono_nukekubi.tres
msgid ""
"Depicts two yōkai, Nukekubi (\"removable neck\") and Inugami (\"dog spirit\").\n"
"\n"
"Nukekubi are women whose heads detach at night and attack people, usually draining their blood. According to legend, if the body is moved, the head cannot reattach itself.\n"
"\n"
"Inugami are dog spirits from western Japan. They were related to a ritual of burying dog heads at a crossroads to inflict curses, which was banned during the Heian period (794-1185)."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: piece_name
#: art/pieces/piece_bakemono_nuppeppo.tres
msgid "Nuppeppō"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: edits_description
#: art/pieces/piece_bakemono_nuppeppo.tres
msgid "The background was edited out and the image was used to illustrate a related yōkai, Nopperabō."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/pieces/piece_bakemono_nuppeppo.tres
msgid "Depicts Nuppeppō, a yōkai with indistinguishable wrinkles on its face and body, appearing as a blob of flesh. The name comes from nupperi, an old term that meant \"smear\", and refers to the creature smearing its face with white powder. This was used idiomatically to mean feigning ignorance."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: piece_name
#. Property: name
#: art/pieces/piece_bakemono_nurarihyon.tres
#: stage/hauntings/types/nurarihyon/haunting_nurarihyon.tres
msgid "Nurarihyon"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/pieces/piece_bakemono_nurarihyon.tres
msgid "Depicts Nurarihyon (\"slippery gourd\"), a yōkai with a gourd-shaped head said to sneak into wealthy houses and indulge in a household's fine goods. Later interpretations relabeled him as the leader of the yōkai, which was likely the result of a depiction in a 1929 book by Morihiko Fujisawa."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: piece_name
#: art/pieces/piece_bakemono_nureonna.tres
msgid "Nure Onna"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: edits_description
#: art/pieces/piece_bakemono_nureonna.tres
msgid "The background was edited out and the image was used to illustrate a different yōkai, Uwabami."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/pieces/piece_bakemono_nureonna.tres
msgid "Depicts Nure-onna (\"wet woman\"), a yōkai with the head of a woman and the body of a snake. It is said that Nure-onna is associated with another yōkai, the Ushi-oni. Nure-onna hands her baby off to innocent strangers, then walks into the sea and disappears. The baby becomes incredibly heavy so that the victim cannot move. Ushi-oni then comes out of the water to attack and they feed on the prey together."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: piece_name
#: art/pieces/piece_bakemono_nuribotoke.tres
msgid "Nuribotoke"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: edits_description
#: art/pieces/piece_bakemono_nuribotoke.tres
msgid "The background was edited out, the eyes made less creepy, and the image was used to illustrate a different yōkai, Kuro Bōzu."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/pieces/piece_bakemono_nuribotoke.tres
msgid "Depicts Nuribotoke, a yōkai that resembles an animated corpse. Distinguishing attributes in most depictions are its pitch black color and its droopy eyeballs. Its name means \"lacquered Buddha\", and it is supposed to appear at night out of poorly maintained Buddhist shrines."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: piece_name
#: art/pieces/piece_bakemono_oonyuudou.tres
msgid "Daisō"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/pieces/piece_bakemono_oonyuudou.tres
msgid "Depicts Daisō, or Ōnyūdō, a yōkai that appears as a giant monk. Sometimes they are depicted as unusually large humans, and sometimes as the size of mountains. There are many legends of these yōkai, some claiming them to be helpful, but most attributing various kinds of malice."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: piece_name
#. Property: name
#: art/pieces/piece_bakemono_tsuchigumo.tres
#: stage/hauntings/types/tsuchigumo/haunting_tsuchigumo.tres
msgid "Tsuchigumo"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/pieces/piece_bakemono_tsuchigumo.tres
msgid ""
"Depicts Tsuchigumo (\"earth spider\"), a giant spider yōkai that makes a home out of silk tubes and preys on humans and animals.\n"
"\n"
"The name of this yōkai was a derogatory term for renegade local clans, primarily during the Asuka, Nara, and early Heian periods."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: piece_name
#. Property: name
#: art/pieces/piece_bakemono_umibouzu.tres
#: stage/hauntings/types/umibouzu/haunting_umibouzu.tres
msgid "Umibōzu"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: edits_description
#: art/pieces/piece_bakemono_umibouzu.tres
msgid "In one use the background was edited out. In another use, only the background is kept."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/pieces/piece_bakemono_umibouzu.tres
msgid "Depicts Umibōzu (\"sea priest\"), a yōkai that appears to sailors on calm seas which quickly turn tumultuous. It demands a barrel from the sailors and proceeds to drown them. According to legend, the only safe way to escape an umibōzu is to give it a bottomless barrel and sail away while it is confused. It often appears as a humanoid shape with a shaved head and piercing eyes."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: piece_name
#: art/pieces/piece_bakemono_ushi_oni.tres
msgid "Ushi-oni"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: edits_description
#: art/pieces/piece_bakemono_ushi_oni.tres
msgid "The head was used as part of a composite image."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/pieces/piece_bakemono_ushi_oni.tres
msgid "Depicts Ushi-oni (\"ox demon\"), a yōkai that was said to reside near bodies of water and terrorizing the locals. It is usually depicted as a six-legged spider with a demonic fanged ox head, and is rumored to cooperate with another yōkai, the Nure-onna, to ambush and eat people."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: piece_name
#. Property: name
#: art/pieces/piece_bakemono_uwan.tres
#: stage/hauntings/types/uwan/haunting_uwan.tres
msgid "Uwan"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: edits_description
#: art/pieces/piece_bakemono_uwan.tres
#: art/pieces/piece_gekkou_priest_saigyo.tres
#: art/pieces/piece_gekkou_temple_bell.tres
msgid "The background was edited out and the image was flipped."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/pieces/piece_bakemono_uwan.tres
msgid "Depicts Uwan, a yōkai that was first considered to be a disembodied voice shouting \"uwan!\" at people in the dark, especially in old homes and abandoned temples. Edo-period interpretations give this disembodied voice as physical form. Mentions of this yōkai are believed to have originated in Aomori prefecture."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: piece_name
#. Property: name
#: art/pieces/piece_bakemono_yamabiko.tres
#: stage/hauntings/types/yamabiko/haunting_yamabiko.tres
msgid "Yamabiko"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/pieces/piece_bakemono_yamabiko.tres
msgid "Depicts Yamabiko (\"mountain boy\", homophone for \"echo\"), a yōkai that appears in the mountains. When echoes appear to be coming from a mysterious origin or come back sounding different, they are attributed to a yamabiko. It is a relatively harmless yōkai, causing confusion rather than any serious injury. They are usually depicted as a hybrid between a dog and a monkey in a shrugging pose."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: piece_name
#: art/pieces/piece_bakemono_yamawarou.tres
msgid "Yamawarō"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: edits_description
#: art/pieces/piece_bakemono_yamawarou.tres
msgid "The body was used as part of a composite image."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/pieces/piece_bakemono_yamawarou.tres
msgid "Depicts Yamawarō (\"ox demon\"), a yōkai from the mountains in the west of Japan. They are one-eyed and child-sized, and are said to mimic both natural sounds and human speech. They offer woodcutting services if presented with an appropriate amount of food at an appropriate time."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: piece_name
#: art/pieces/piece_bakemono_yuki_onna.tres
msgid "Yuki Onna"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: edits_description
#: art/pieces/piece_bakemono_yuki_onna.tres
msgid "The mouth expression was changed and the background was edited out."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/pieces/piece_bakemono_yuki_onna.tres
msgid "Depicts Yuki Onna (\"snow woman\"), a yōkai that appears on snowy winter nights. Legends describe her as beautiful, with pale bluish skin, cold to the touch, and dressed in white. Some believe she is simply an embodiment of snow, both dangerous and serene. Others believe that she is a spirit who died because of snow, a fallen heavenly being, or a form of some other shape-shifting yōkai."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: piece_name
#. Property: name
#: art/pieces/piece_bakemono_yume_seirei.tres
#: stage/hauntings/types/yume_no_seirei/haunting_yume_no_seirei.tres
msgid "Yume no Seirei"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/pieces/piece_bakemono_yume_seirei.tres
msgid "Depicts Yume no Seirei (\"dream ghost\"), a yōkai appearing as an emaciated elderly man, believed to cause nightmares."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: piece_name
#: art/pieces/piece_bakemono_yuurei.tres
msgid "Yūrei"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/pieces/piece_bakemono_yuurei.tres
msgid ""
"Depicts Yūrei (\"faint spirit\"), a generic term for ghosts, usually referring to spirits barred from a peaceful afterlife.\n"
"\n"
"According to traditional Japanese beliefs, humans have a soul which leaves the body when the person dies. It stays in a purgatory waiting for funeral rites to join its ancestors, and then to return yearly in August during the Obon Festival to receive thanks.\n"
"\n"
"However, a violent death or the lack of funeral rites can cause the soul to become a yūrei which stays in the physical world to take care of its unfinished business."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: piece_name
#: art/pieces/piece_bartlett_shoji.tres
msgid "Shōji, Mount Fuji Amid Clouds"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: edits_description
#: art/pieces/piece_bartlett_shoji.tres
msgid "The image was resized and the text was removed."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/pieces/piece_bartlett_shoji.tres
msgid "Depicts the peak of Mount Fuji appearing to float above the cloud cover near Lake Shōji, Yamanashi Prefecture."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: piece_name
#: art/pieces/piece_bartlett_taj_mahal.tres
msgid "Taj Mahal from the Desert"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: edits_description
#: art/pieces/piece_bartlett_taj_mahal.tres
msgid "Everything but the desert was removed."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/pieces/piece_bartlett_taj_mahal.tres
msgid "Depicts a camel caravan making its way through the desert near the Taj Mahal in India. At the time of Bartlett's visit, the sole means of accessing this remote location was via camel caravan, which took days, if not weeks, depending on the onset of one's journey."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: piece_name
#: art/pieces/piece_bartlett_taj_mahal_moonlight.tres
msgid "Taj Mahal by Moonlight"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: edits_description
#: art/pieces/piece_bartlett_taj_mahal_moonlight.tres
msgid "The people were edited out."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/pieces/piece_bartlett_taj_mahal_moonlight.tres
msgid ""
"Depicts a view of the Taj Mahal, in Agra, India, seen from its garden. A reflecting pool runs through the center of the image.\n"
"\n"
"The building, a mausoleum for Mumtaz Mahal, the wife of Shah Jahan, was finished in 1648 and is currently open to visitors."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: piece_name
#: art/pieces/piece_bartlett_tartar_wall.tres
msgid "Tartar Wall, Peking"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: edits_description
#: art/pieces/piece_bartlett_tartar_wall.tres
msgid "The camel, its handler, and the distance houses with their greenery were removed."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/pieces/piece_bartlett_tartar_wall.tres
msgid ""
"Depicts the outside of the Tartar Wall, the southern boundary of the inner-city wall of old Peking, at dusk. Under an indigo blue sky, a lone traveler and his camel stop for a drink. The pool reflects the dark sky and the silhouette of the wall.\n"
"\n"
"This wall has been demolished since, today the 2nd Ring Road in Beijing cuts over where it once stood."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: piece_name
#: art/pieces/piece_chikanobu_cats.tres
msgid "Cats on the Roof"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: edits_description
#: art/pieces/piece_chikanobu_cats.tres
msgid "The text overlays were removed and all the colors were manually repainted at a higher resolution."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: piece_name
#: art/pieces/piece_chikanobu_hisamatsuza.tres
msgid "Opening Ceremony at the Hisamatsuza Theater"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: edits_description
#: art/pieces/piece_chikanobu_hisamatsuza.tres
msgid "The text overlays were removed and the triptych was stitched together."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/pieces/piece_chikanobu_hisamatsuza.tres
msgid ""
"Depicts the opening ceremony of Hisamatsuza, a prominent kabuki theater in Tōkyō.\n"
"\n"
"The text labels show the name of each of the depicted actors.\n"
"\n"
"The theater burned down and was rebuilt multiple times, eventually reopening as the Meijiza theater, still operating today."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: piece_name
#: art/pieces/piece_chikanobu_ikebana.tres
msgid "Flower Arranging and Tea Ceremony"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: edits_description
#: art/pieces/piece_chikanobu_ikebana.tres
msgid "Only the left panel was used, and the text was removed."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/pieces/piece_chikanobu_ikebana.tres
msgid ""
"Depicts court ladies preparing a flower arrangement for a tea ceremony. From the series Ladies in Waiting in Chiyoda Palace.\n"
"\n"
"This takes place during the Edo period in the Chiyoda Palace, which to this day serves as the main residence of the Japanese Emperor, though it is now simply called the Imperial Palace."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/pieces/piece_composed_amanojaku.tres
#: stage/hauntings/types/amanojaku/haunting_amanojaku.tres
msgid "Amanojaku"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: edits_description
#: art/pieces/piece_composed_amanojaku.tres
msgid "This image was composed by combining the body from the Yamawarō painting with the head from the Ushi-oni one."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/pieces/piece_composed_amanojaku.tres
msgid ""
"This composite attempts to approximate the legendary appearance of Amanojaku, a yōkai with origins in both Buddhist iconography and Shinto mythology.\n"
"\n"
"In the Buddhist origin, they are said to be demons trampled by the Four Heavenly Kings, gods resposible for the cardinal directions.\n"
"\n"
"In the Shinto origin, they are the disgraced young servants of the sun goddess Amaterasu. One of these servants, Amenosagume (\"heavenly spy woman\"), was said to be able to read the human heart and use that knowledge to play contrarian pranks, which became a major theme associated with the yōkai."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/pieces/piece_composed_calendar.tres
msgid "Calendar"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/pieces/piece_composed_calendar.tres
msgid "The scroll was taken from one Shinsai print, the flower from another, and the writing was added."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/pieces/piece_composed_cloudtop_temple.tres
msgid "Cloudtop Temple"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/pieces/piece_composed_hanging_monastery.tres
msgid "Canyon Monastery"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/pieces/piece_composed_hanging_monastery.tres
msgid "The main temple image was taken from the Nigatsudo Temple print, while the background was edited out and replaced by a rock wall texture created by sampling the Sphinx print."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#. Property: long_term_name
#: art/pieces/piece_composed_haven.tres
#: glossary/terms/standalone/term_hub.tres
msgid "The Haven"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/pieces/piece_composed_haven.tres
msgid ""
"The Haven in Worlds Upon The Wind is composed of many elements directly taken or inspired by that artwork.\n"
"\n"
"Although not an ukiyo-e print but rather an e-maki (painted scroll), this artwork does an amazing job of illustrating life in the Edo period and the themes presented by ukiyo-e and Worlds Upon The Wind."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#. Property: text
#: art/pieces/piece_composed_kodama.tres
#: stage/hauntings/haunting_spot.tscn
#: stage/hauntings/types/kodama/haunting_kodama.tres
msgid "Kodama"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/pieces/piece_composed_kodama.tres
msgid "The image was composed using the texture from the sun, superimposing the flames, and recoloring."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/pieces/piece_composed_main_menu.tres
msgid "Worlds Upon The Wind Title Screen"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/pieces/piece_composed_main_menu.tres
msgid ""
"The game's title screen is composed from 5 different ukiyo-e prints:\n"
"[ul]\n"
"The framing window where the cat is sitting is based on [url=artist:Hiroshige]Hiroshige[/url]'s [url=art_piece:Asakusa Ricefields and Torinomachi Festival]Asakusa Ricefields and Torinomachi Festival[/url].\n"
"The teahouse on the left is from [url=artist:Hiroshige]Hiroshige[/url]'s [url=art_piece:Fuji-view Teahouse at Zōshigaya]Fuji-view Teahouse at Zōshigaya[/url].\n"
"The temple hall on the right is from [url=artist:Hiroshige]Hiroshige[/url]'s [url=art_piece:The Kiyomizu Hall and Shinobazu Pond at Ueno]The Kiyomizu Hall and Shinobazu Pond at Ueno[/url].\n"
"The clouds are based on [url=artist:Katsushika Hokusai]Hokusai[/url]'s famous [url=art_piece:Fine Wind, Clear Morning]Red Fuji[/url].\n"
"Finally, the sky and the floating shards are based on [url=art_piece:Production of Bogwood Carvings in Matsushima]Production of Bogwood Carvings in Matsushima[/url] by [url=artist:Hiroshige III]Hiroshige III[/url].\n"
"[/ul]"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#. Property: event_name
#. Property: text
#. Property: title
#. Property: translation
#: art/pieces/piece_composed_mirage_gate.tres
#: events/landmark/mirage_gate/event_mirage_gate.tres
