#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/schools/school_kano.tres
msgid "Kanō School"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/schools/school_kano.tres
msgid ""
"The Kanō school was the longest-lived and most influential lineage of painters in Japanese history, serving as the official artists of the shogunate for over 4 centuries.\n"
"\n"
"Founded in the 15th century, the school established itself by blending the strong, expressive brushwork of Chinese ink painting with the colorful, highly decorative elements of traditional Japanese aesthetics.\n"
"\n"
"They are renowned for their monumental works on sliding doors and folding screens, frequently using lavish gold leaf backgrounds to project the power of their samurai patrons. Through a strict system of workshops and hereditary succession, they defined the official visual style of the ruling class and shaped the foundations of early modern Japanese art."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/schools/school_katsukawa.tres
msgid "Katsukawa School"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/schools/school_katsukawa.tres
msgid ""
"The Katsukawa school was a major lineage of ukiyo-e artists that revolutionized the depiction of kabuki actors in the late 18th century.\n"
"\n"
"Founded by Katsukawa Shunshō, the school introduced a new level of realism to actor prints. Rather than relying on generic, idealized faces to represent specific roles, Katsukawa artists captured the distinct facial characteristics, personalities, and dramatic expressions of individual performers.\n"
"\n"
"The Katsukawa workshop also served as the early training ground for several prominent printmakers, including a young [url=artist:Katsushika Hokusai]Katsushika Hokusai[/url], before its influence was eclipsed by the rising [url=art_school:Utagawa school]Utagawa school[/url]."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/schools/school_shijo.tres
msgid "Shijō School"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/schools/school_shijo.tres
msgid ""
"The Shijō school was a lineage of Japanese painters that emerged in Kyōto during the late 18th century. Founded by Matsumura Goshun, the movement successfully blended the precise, objective realism of the Maruyama school with the expressive, poetic brushwork of traditional literati painters.\n"
"\n"
"Shijō artists favored approachable, everyday subjects. They are known for their atmospheric landscapes, delicate nature studies, and lively depictions of birds and animals."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/schools/school_torii.tres
msgid "Torii School"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/schools/school_torii.tres
msgid ""
"The Torii school is a long-running school of ukiyo-e painting and printing founded in Edo, specializing in kabuki theater signboards and other promotional materials.\n"
"\n"
"The Torii were among those whose work led to the development of ukiyo-e. Their style was one of the primary influences in the ukiyo-e depiction of actors and kabuki scenes for much of the 18th century.\n"
"\n"
"Still today, kabuki signboards are sometimes painted by members of the Torii family."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/schools/school_utagawa.tres
msgid "Utagawa School"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/schools/school_utagawa.tres
msgid ""
"The Utagawa school was the largest and most influential lineage of printmakers in Japanese history, dominating the ukiyo-e market during the 19th century.\n"
"\n"
"Founded by Utagawa Toyoharu, the school expanded under master artists like Toyokuni, Kunisada, Kuniyoshi, and Hiroshige, who collectively produced the vast majority of all surviving woodblock prints.\n"
"\n"
"Works by this school include a wide array of genres, from dramatic portraits of kabuki actors and dynamic depictions of historical warriors to sweeping landscapes and scenes of everyday life.\n"
"\n"
"By organizing a highly efficient workshop system, the Utagawa school transformed printmaking into a widely accessible mass medium, and their exported works later became the primary lens through which European artists first encountered and studied Japanese aesthetics."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/series/series_bakemono_e.tres
msgid "Illustrations of Supernatural Creatures"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/series/series_bakemono_e.tres
msgid ""
"This is a Japanese picture scroll (e-maki) of the Edo period depicting 35 bakemono (monsters) from Japanese folklore. The figures are hand-painted on paper in vivid color with accents in gold and silver pigments.\n"
"\n"
"Each bakemono is labeled with its name in hand-brushed ink. There is no other writing on the scroll, no colophon, and no artist's signature or seal. Several versions of the scroll exist.\n"
"\n"
"This is a prime example of [url=art_style:Yūrei-zu]Yūrei-zu[/url]."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/series/series_eisen_hiroshige_69_stations_kisokaidou.tres
msgid "69 Stations of the Kisokaidō"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/series/series_eisen_hiroshige_69_stations_kisokaidou.tres
msgid ""
"A series of 71 prints depicting the towns and rest stops along the Kisokaidō, one of the centrally administered five routes of the Edo period, connecting Edo and Kyōto.\n"
"\n"
"There is one print for each of the 69 post stations, except Nakatsugawa-juku has two prints, and an extra print depicts Nihonbashi, the starting point in Edo.\n"
"\n"
"The set was started by Eisen and published by Hōeidō in 1835, but in 1837 [url=artist:Hiroshige]Hiroshige[/url] took over and completed the series with the publisher Iseiri.\n"
"\n"
"Later reprints removed Eisen's signature, presumably because the publishers wanted the public to think the designs were by Hiroshige, the better known landscape artist of the two."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/series/series_gekkou_47_ronin.tres
msgid "Illustrations of the 47 Loyal Retainers"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/series/series_gekkou_47_ronin.tres
msgid "This series depicts scenes from the famous puppet (and later kabuki) play about 47 Ronin (masterless samurai) avenging their master, Asano Naganori, a fictionalized account of a 1703 Akō incident."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/series/series_gekkou_essays.tres
msgid "Gekkō's Essays"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/series/series_gekkou_essays.tres
msgid "This series depicts figures from history and mythology, including famous beauties, poets, scholars, warriors, and supernatural creatures. The designs are rendered in Gekkō's distinctive painterly style, with soft washes of color and flowing calligraphic line work."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/series/series_gekkou_flowers.tres
msgid "Flowers of Japan"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/series/series_gekkou_flowers.tres
msgid "This series of 36 prints, also known as Scenery from Japanese Gardens, depicts historical figures, cultural events, pastimes, and famous natural landmarks like an 800-year-old cherry tree. Many prints still lack a fully documented interpretation."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/series/series_gekkou_fuji.tres
#: art/series/series_hokusai_100_view_of_fuji.tres
msgid "100 Views of Mount Fuji"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/series/series_gekkou_fuji.tres
msgid "This set of 95 known prints continues the long ukiyo-e tradition of depicting mount Fuji from various scenic and interesting locations. The mountain is usually not the focus of the image, but rather a small background detail."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/series/series_gekkou_gahou.tres
msgid "Gekkō's Artwork"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/series/series_gekkou_gahou.tres
msgid "This series of 12 prints in the [url=art_style:kachō-e]kachō-e style[/url] following [url=art_school:Shijō School]Shijō school[/url] tradition."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/series/series_gekkou_genji.tres
msgid "54 Chapters of the Tale of Genji"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/series/series_gekkou_genji.tres
msgid "This set of 54 images illustrates each chapter of the Tale of Genji, a classic work written in the Heian period (11th century) by the noblewoman Murasaki Shikibu. It is often considered the world's first novel."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/series/series_gekkou_shishikiban.tres
msgid "Shikishi-ban Kachō-e"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/series/series_gekkou_shishikiban.tres
msgid "This set of 60 prints of flowers, animals, human figures, and scenery uses a shikishi-ban (square) format, a favorite of Gekkō's, and features whimsical linework and impressive gradations. Although \"kachō-e\" features in the title, most of the prints would not be considered [url=art_style:kachō-e]kachō-e[/url]."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/series/series_gekkou_women_flowers.tres
msgid "Comparison of Beautiful Women and Flowers"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/series/series_gekkou_women_flowers.tres
msgid "This set of 24 depicts women together with flowers associated with each person. Some prints use metallic overprinting."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/series/series_hirokage_comical_meisho.tres
msgid "Comical Views of Famous Places in Edo"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/series/series_hirokage_comical_meisho.tres
msgid "A series of 50 prints depicting comical versions of many of the famous prints by [url=artist:Utagawa Hiroshige]Hiroshige[/url]."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/series/series_hiroshige_2_100_views.tres
msgid "100 Views of Famous Places in the Provinces"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/series/series_hiroshige_2_100_views.tres
msgid ""
"This series of landscape prints dates from the middle of Hiroshige II's career, when he was in his mid-thirties. At this point, he had just started using the name Hiroshige, since his master, [url=artist:Hiroshige]the original Hiroshige[/url], had just died in 1858.\n"
"\n"
"A few of the images in this series are closely based on those in the well-known Hiroshige series Famous Views of the 60-odd Provinces."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/series/series_hiroshige_2_48_views_of_edo.tres
msgid "48 Famous Views of Edo"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/series/series_hiroshige_2_48_views_of_edo.tres
msgid ""
"This series of landscape prints was published when Hiroshige II was in his mid-thirties. At this point, he had just started using the name Hiroshige, since his master, [url=artist:Hiroshige]the original Hiroshige[/url], had just died in 1858.\n"
"\n"
"It contains various landscapes views around Edo (present-day Tōkyō)."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/series/series_hiroshige_36_views_tall.tres
msgid "36 Views of Mount Fuji (Hiroshige, Tall)"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/series/series_hiroshige_36_views_tall.tres
msgid "The later of two series of the same name by Hiroshige, depicting Mount Fuji from different locations, inspired by [url=artist:Katsushika Hokusai]Hokusai[/url]'s [url=art_series:36 Views of Mount Fuji]series of the same name[/url]. This one was in a portrait format."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/series/series_hiroshige_36_views_wide.tres
msgid "36 Views of Mount Fuji (Hiroshige, Wide)"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/series/series_hiroshige_36_views_wide.tres
msgid "The earlier of two series of the same name by Hiroshige, depicting Mount Fuji from different locations, inspired by [url=artist:Katsushika Hokusai]Hokusai[/url]'s [url=art_series:36 Views of Mount Fuji]series of the same name[/url]. This one was in a wide (landscape) format."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/series/series_hiroshige_3_products_of_greater_japan.tres
msgid "Products of Greater Japan"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/series/series_hiroshige_3_products_of_greater_japan.tres
msgid ""
"This series of 118 prints depicts regional scenes of farming, fishing, mining and other economic activities. The series was issued in August 1877 to coincide with the opening of Japan's first National Industrial Exposition (Naikoku Kangyō Hakurankai) held in Tōkyō's Ueno Park.\n"
"\n"
"Although not well known, the series was a treasure trove for Worlds Upon The Wind, as it depicted many everyday activities rarely seen in ukiyo-e."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/series/series_hiroshige_47_ronins_1935.tres
msgid "The Storehouse of Loyal Retainers"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/series/series_hiroshige_47_ronins_1935.tres
msgid "This series of 16 prints depicts scenes from the famous puppet (and later kabuki) play about 47 Ronin (masterless samurai) avenging their master, Asano Naganori, a fictionalized account of a 1703 Akō incident."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/series/series_hiroshige_53_stations_tokaido_gyousho.tres
msgid "53 Stations of the Tōkaidō - Gyōsho Edition"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/series/series_hiroshige_53_stations_tokaido_gyousho.tres
#: art/series/series_hiroshige_53_stations_tokaido_hoeidou.tres
#: art/series/series_hiroshige_53_stations_tokaido_jinbutsu.tres
#: art/series/series_hiroshige_53_stations_tokaido_kichizou.tres
#: art/series/series_hiroshige_53_stations_tokaido_kyouka.tres
#: art/series/series_hiroshige_53_stations_tokaido_reisho.tres
msgid ""
"This extremely popular series depicts the towns and rest stops along the Tōkaidō (\"eastern sea route\") connecting Kyōto to Tōkyō (then called Edo), the most important of the Five Routes of the Edo period.\n"
"\n"
"Hiroshige published multiple editions of the series with the same title, each containing different prints, including:[ul]\n"
"[url=art_series:53 Stations of the Tōkaidō - Hōeidō Edition]Hōeidō Edition, 1833 - 1834[/url]\n"
"[url=art_series:53 Stations of the Tōkaidō - Kyōka Edition]Kyōka Edition, 1840 - 1842[/url]\n"
"[url=art_series:53 Stations of the Tōkaidō - Gyōsho Edition]Gyōsho Edition, 1841 - 1844[/url]\n"
"[url=art_series:53 Stations of the Tōkaidō - Reisho Edition]Reisho Edition, 1847 - 1852[/url]\n"
"[url=art_series:53 Stations of the Tōkaidō - Kichizō Edition]Kichizō Edition, 1850 - 1851[/url]\n"
"[url=art_series:53 Stations of the Tōkaidō - Jinbutsu Edition]Jinbutsu Edition, 1851 - 1853[/url]\n"
"[/ul]"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/series/series_hiroshige_53_stations_tokaido_hoeidou.tres
msgid "53 Stations of the Tōkaidō - Hōeidō Edition"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/series/series_hiroshige_53_stations_tokaido_jinbutsu.tres
msgid "53 Stations of the Tōkaidō - Jinbutsu Edition"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/series/series_hiroshige_53_stations_tokaido_kichizou.tres
msgid "53 Stations of the Tōkaidō - Kichizō Edition"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/series/series_hiroshige_53_stations_tokaido_kyouka.tres
msgid "53 Stations of the Tōkaidō - Kyōka Edition"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/series/series_hiroshige_53_stations_tokaido_reisho.tres
msgid "53 Stations of the Tōkaidō - Reisho Edition"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/series/series_hiroshige_60_provinces.tres
msgid "Famous Views of the Sixty-odd Provinces"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/series/series_hiroshige_60_provinces.tres
msgid ""
"A series of 70 prints depicting famous places in each of Japan's then 68 provinces, plus a print of Edo, the capital, and a contents page.\n"
"\n"
"Hiroshige based many of his designs on old Japanese guidebooks called meisho zue. In particular, at least 26 of the designs are believed to have been based on drawings from the 8 volume series of guidebooks called Sansui Kikan (Exceptional Mountain and Water Landscapes) written and illustrated by Fuchigami Kyokkō (1753–1816) published between 1800 and 1802."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/series/series_hiroshige_famous_places_in_edo.tres
msgid "100 Famous Views of Edo (1854)"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/series/series_hiroshige_famous_places_in_edo.tres
msgid "A series depicting various locations in Edo (present-day Tōkyō). There are many versions of the series, all containing different sets of prints."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/series/series_hiroshige_famous_restaurants.tres
msgid "Famous Restaurants in Edo"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/series/series_hiroshige_famous_restaurants.tres
msgid "A series of 30 prints illustrating famous restaurants in Edo (present-day Tōkyō)."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/series/series_hiroshige_famous_views_of_edo.tres
msgid "100 Famous Views of Edo (1856-1859)"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/series/series_hiroshige_famous_views_of_edo.tres
msgid "A series of 119 prints, begun and largely completed by Hiroshige, depicting Edo (present-day Tōkyō). The prints were first published in serialized form in 1856–1859, with [url=artist:Hiroshige II]Hiroshige II[/url] completing the series after Hiroshige's death. It was tremendously popular and much reprinted."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/series/series_hiroshige_famous_views_of_osaka.tres
msgid "Famous Views of Naniwa"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/series/series_hiroshige_famous_views_of_osaka.tres
msgid "A lesser known series of 10 prints depicting famous places in the Kansai region, around Naniwa (present-day Ōsaka)."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/series/series_hiroshige_joruri_town.tres
msgid "Flourishing Business in Jōruri Town"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/series/series_hiroshige_joruri_town.tres
msgid ""
"A series of 8 prints depicting characters from famous plays reimagined in mundane roles of Edo-period merchants and customers. This is an example of mitate-e (見立絵), a genre that employs allusions, puns, and incongruities, often to parody classical art or events.\n"
"\n"
"The name of the series references Jōruri, a form of traditional narrative music in which a tayū (太夫) sings to the accompaniment of a shamisen. Jōruri often accompanies bunraku, traditional puppet theater. The name comes from Jōruri Monogatari (The Tale of Princess Jōruri), a foundational 15th-century romantic ballad, which is a fictional romance between the historical samurai leader Minamoto no Yoshitsune and a wealthy, healing-powered Princess Jōruri."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/series/series_hiroshige_kanto.tres
msgid "Famous Views of the Kantō Region"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/series/series_hiroshige_kanto.tres
msgid "A series of 5 prints depicting several famous locations in the Kantō region, which covers much of the 7 prefectures surrounding Tōkyō."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/series/series_hiroshige_kyoto.tres
msgid "Famous Places of Kyōto"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/series/series_hiroshige_kyoto.tres
msgid "A series of 10 prints depicting famous places around Kyōto."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/series/series_hiroshige_our_country.tres
msgid "Famous Places in Our Country"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/series/series_hiroshige_our_country.tres
msgid "A series of 15 prints illustrating various landscapes around Japan."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/series/series_hiroshige_snow_moon_flowers.tres
msgid "Snow, Moon and Flowers"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/series/series_hiroshige_snow_moon_flowers.tres
msgid "Although not officially named, this is a recognized set of 3 triptychs, completed one year before Hiroshige's death, representing the theme of snow, moon, and flowers. The last triptych depicts a scene of whirlpools to evoke the shape of flowers."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/series/series_hokusai_100_poems.tres
msgid "100 Poems as Told by the Nurse"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/series/series_hokusai_100_poems.tres
msgid ""
"This was Hokusai's last major single sheet series before he devoted his remaining years mainly to commissions. Of the intended series of 100, only 27 prints are known to have been completed. An additional 64 designs survive in the form of preparatory drawings.\n"
"\n"
"Hokusai based this series on the well-known anthology of poems, the Hyakunin Isshu (100 People, One Poem Each), compiled by the poet Fujiwara no Teika in 1235. The collected verses were familiar to most Japanese, and even today there are card games that involve recognizing the ending of a verse from this collection based on its starting lines.\n"
"\n"
"Hokusai approaches the poems from the perspective of an uneducated wet-nurse, allowing for seemingly simple-minded mistakes or misinterpretations which add a light-hearted quality to his renditions."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/series/series_hokusai_100_stories.tres
msgid "100 Ghost Stories"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/series/series_hokusai_100_stories.tres
msgid ""
"A series of 5 prints depicting several yōkai, each one referencing a woman who broke some Buddhist taboo.\n"
"\n"
"The series is made in reference to the tradition of Hyakumonogatari Kaidankai (\"gathering of one hundred supernatural tales\"), a popular Japanese game played at night, when people would meet to share folklore ghost stories and their own anecdotes."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/series/series_hokusai_100_view_of_fuji.tres
msgid ""
"This is a series of three illustrated books, considered one of Japan's most exceptional.\n"
"\n"
"The books contain over a hundred views of Mount Fuji in various styles and settings. Hokusai shows the peak in pure landscapes, with flora and fauna, in religious and mythological scenes and with different atmospheric effects, but above all, he focuses on ordinary people at work.\n"
"\n"
"The first two volumes are celebrated for their very high standards of woodblock printing, despite being monochromatic."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/series/series_hokusai_36_views.tres
msgid "36 Views of Mount Fuji"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/series/series_hokusai_36_views.tres
msgid ""
"Produced when [url=artist:Katsushika Hokusai]Hokusai[/url] was in his seventies and at the height of his career, this series of landscape prints depicts Mount Fuji from different locations and in various seasons and weather conditions. The immediate success of the publication led to another 10 prints being added to the series.\n"
"\n"
"Works in this series are examples of [url=art_style:meisho-e]Meisho-e[/url], and the heavy use of the Prussian Blue pigment also puts them in the category of [url=art_style:aizuri-e]Aizuri-e[/url]."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/series/series_hokusai_47_ronin.tres
msgid "The Treasury of Loyal Retainers"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/series/series_hokusai_47_ronin.tres
msgid ""
"This series depicts scenes from the famous puppet (and later kabuki) play about 47 Ronin (masterless samurai) avenging their master, Asano Naganori, a fictionalized account of a 1703 Akō incident.\n"
"\n"
"Hokusai published these works under the name Shunrō."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/series/series_hokusai_blue.tres
msgid "Untitled Series in Blue"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/series/series_hokusai_blue.tres
msgid "A rare series with very few known pieces, featuring prints using almost exclusively blue pigment. See [url=art_style:aizuri-e]Aizuri-e[/url]."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/series/series_hokusai_bridges.tres
msgid "Remarkable Views of Bridges in Various Provinces"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/series/series_hokusai_bridges.tres
msgid ""
"A set of 11 prints depicting scenic views of bridges, dramatized to inspire a sense of awe.\n"
"\n"
"Many of the bridges had literary associations, such as the Eight-plank Bridge of Mikawa. Two of the bridges were no longer extant during the time of production, so Hokusai drew them based on historical records."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/series/series_hokusai_waterfalls.tres
msgid "A Tour of the Waterfalls of the Provinces"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/series/series_hokusai_waterfalls.tres
msgid "A set of 8 prints depicting famous waterfalls. It is considered the first ukiyo-e series to focus on the subject. The waterfalls take up most of each sheet, dwarfing the human figures observing them."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/series/series_hyakki_yagyou.tres
msgid "Night Parade of One Hundred Demons"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/series/series_hyakki_yagyou.tres
msgid ""
"This is a Japanese picture scroll (e-maki) from the Edo period depicting the [url=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hyakki_Yagy%C5%8D]Hyakki Yagyō[/url], a legendary procession of 100 demons from Japanese folklore. Sometimes an orderly procession, other times a riot, it refers to a huge parade of supernatural creatures known as oni and yōkai that march through the streets of Japan at night.\n"
"\n"
"The original authorship is uncertain, but multiple versions of this scroll were made during the Edo period, each depicting several demons not found in other versions. One of these scrolls is attributed to a 12th-century priest-painter [url=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Toba_S%C5%8Dj%C5%8D]Toba Sōjō[/url]. Another is attributed to the 15th-century painter [url=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tosa_Mitsunobu]Tosa Mitsunobu[/url]. Yet another to the 17th-century painter [url=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tosa_Mitsuoki]Tosa Mitsuoki[/url].\n"
"\n"
"This is a prime example of [url=art_style:Yūrei-zu]Yūrei-zu[/url]."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/series/series_kuniyoshi_great_priest.tres
msgid "Sketches of the Life of the Great Priest"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/series/series_kuniyoshi_great_priest.tres
msgid "This series tells the story of Nichiren (1222-1282), a Japanese Buddhist priest who founded the Nichiren sect of Buddhism. It was commissioned in remembrance of the 550-year anniversary of the death of Nichiren."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/series/series_kuniyoshi_kisokaidou.tres
msgid "The 69 Post Stations of the Kisokaidō Road"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/series/series_kuniyoshi_kisokaidou.tres
msgid ""
"This series of 74 prints depicts the 69 rest stops along the Kisokaidō Road, plus prints for Edo, Kyōto, and a title page.\n"
"\n"
"The Kisokaidō Road was an inland route connecting Edo (present-day Tōkyō) with Kyōto.\n"
"\n"
"The main design of each print portrays a historical, legendary, or fictional scene associated with the location. A small panel in each print shows a view of the station."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/series/series_kuniyoshi_lucky_gods.tres
msgid "Seven Lucky Gods of the Sumida River"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/series/series_kuniyoshi_lucky_gods.tres
msgid "In this series of prints, kabuki actors are shown in costume along the Sumida River. One of the seven gods of good fortune is inset in the right upper corner of each print, drawn by Kuniyoshi's daughter and student, Yoshi (1842-1885), signed as Yoshijo (\"daughter of Yoshi\")."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/series/series_kuniyoshi_signs.tres
msgid "Selection for the Twelve Zodiac Signs"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/series/series_kuniyoshi_signs.tres
msgid "A set of 13 prints depicting historical and mythical figures likened to the twelve animals of the zodiac. There is one print for each animal, and an extra print for the tiger.  A tiny image of the animal is found on the edge of each title cartouche."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/series/series_kuniyoshi_six_rivers.tres
msgid "The Six Crystal Rivers"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/series/series_kuniyoshi_six_rivers.tres
msgid "This series of 6 triptychs depicts women on the banks of the Six Crystal Rivers (\"mu tamagawa\") from around Japan, a theme covered by several ukiyo-e artists."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/series/series_kuniyoshi_water_margin.tres
msgid "The 108 Heroes of the Popular Water Margin"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/series/series_kuniyoshi_water_margin.tres
msgid "A series of 108 prints illustrating the Chinese semi-historical classic novel, Water Margin (Suikōden, Shuǐhǔ zhuàn in Chinese). The novel tells the story of a band of 108 rebels who sought refuge in the margins of Liangshan Marsh, trying to protect the poor and downtrodden. Each print depicts one of the novel's 108 heroes."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/series/series_utagawa_tokaido_pairings.tres
msgid "53 Pairings for the Tōkaidō Road"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/series/series_utagawa_tokaido_pairings.tres
msgid "A set of 58 prints by [url=artist:Utagawa Kuniyoshi]Kuniyoshi[/url], [url=artist:Utagawa Kunisada]Kunisada[/url], and [url=artist:Utagawa Hiroshige]Hiroshige[/url] depicting historical characters associated with the Stations of the Tōkaidō."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/series/series_yoshida_south_alps.tres
msgid "Southern Japan Alps"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/series/series_yoshida_south_alps.tres
msgid "A series of prints depicting Japan's Southern Alps. Many of the prints depict views from the peaks, featuring pastel expanses of clouds below. Yoshida was an avid mountaineer, and these prints depict sights from his expeditions."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/styles/style_aizuri.tres
msgid "Aizuri-e"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/styles/style_aizuri.tres
msgid "Literally \"blue printed pictures\", this style makes heavy use of blue for shading, outlines or both, relying on the Prussian Blue pigment imported from Europe. It was most notably popularized by [url=artist:Katsushika Hokusai]Katsushika Hokusai[/url]."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/styles/style_bijinga.tres
msgid "Bijin-ga"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/styles/style_bijinga.tres
msgid "Literally \"beautiful women pictures\", this style focuses on depicting women's beauty. Many bijin-ga artists aimed to express inner beauty, so it was not considered important that the picture resemble the facial features of the model, and the depiction is often stylized rather than an attempt to create a realistic image."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/styles/style_emaki.tres
msgid "E-maki"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/styles/style_emaki.tres
msgid ""
"Literally \"picture scroll\", these are horizontal, heavily-illustrated scrolls which arose as a narration system during the Nara-period (710-794). Unlike most of the art in the game, these are not woodblock prints.\n"
"\n"
"Initially the style copied its much older Chinese counterpart, but by the 10th century the Japanese tradition developed its own distinct style.\n"
"\n"
"The scrolls are usually made of paper, but sometimes use silk instead. They are usually from 1 to 3 meters in length, but some reach as long as 12 meters. They are painted, drawn, or (rarely) stamped, and usually intersperse calligraphy and illustrations."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/styles/style_genji.tres
msgid "Genji-e"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/styles/style_genji.tres
msgid ""
"Literally \"Genji illustration\", this style depicts scenes from the Tale of Genji, a classic work written in the Heian period (11th century) by the noblewoman Murasaki Shikibu, which is often considered the world's first novel.\n"
"\n"
"This massive epic follows the philandering adventures of Prince Genji, also called Hikaru Genji (\"shining Genji\"), and in later chapters, the stories of his descendants.\n"
"\n"
"In the 1830s and 1840s, a humorous, swashbuckling adaptation of the novel, titled A Fraudulent Murasaki's Rustic Genji by Ryūtei Tanehiko, was serialized and became a best-selling sensation, further inflaming the demand for prints depicting Genji."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/styles/style_kacho.tres
msgid "Kachō-e"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/styles/style_kacho.tres
msgid ""
"Literally \"bird and flower pictures\", these originate from the Chinese tradition, and feature detailed close-ups of birds, flowers, and insects. The genre is often expanded to include animals and fish, as long as they are the sole focus of the print.\n"
"\n"
"Prints of this style are usually intended to capture specific seasons and classical literary motifs, and often appear alongside poems or text passages."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/styles/style_kaika.tres
msgid "Kaika-e"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/styles/style_kaika.tres
msgid "Literally \"enlightenment picture\", this style arose during the Meiji era and focuses on illustrating the modernization and Westernization of Japanese society in the wake of the fall of the Tokugawa shogunate during the Meiji Restoration. The subjects of these prints include new architecture, changes in clothing and hair fashion, new modes of transport like steam locomotives, and new industrial practices."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/styles/style_meisho.tres
msgid "Meisho-e"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/styles/style_meisho.tres
msgid "Literally \"pictures of famous places\", these prints and paintings focus on specific famous places in Japan, such as Mount Fuji. They became particularly popular during the Edo period, with its booming travel and pilgrimage culture."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/styles/style_mitate.tres
msgid "Mitate-e"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/styles/style_mitate.tres
msgid "Literally \"likened pictures\", this genre uses puns, allusions, and deliberate incongruities, usually to parody classical art or events. The term mitate arose from the haiku tradition in the Heian period (794-1185). Works which are today called mitate-e used different labels at the time, such as fūryū (風流, \"fashionable\")."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/styles/style_musha.tres
msgid "Musha-e"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/styles/style_musha.tres
msgid "Literally \"warrior pictures\", this style depicts warriors and samurai from Japanese and Chinese history and mythology."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/styles/style_rekishi.tres
msgid "Rekishi-ga"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/styles/style_rekishi.tres
msgid "Literally \"historical pictures\", this style depicts real or fictionalized historical characters or legendary scenes."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/styles/style_shin_hanga.tres
msgid "Shin-hanga"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/styles/style_shin_hanga.tres
msgid ""
"Literally \"new prints\", this movement was started by [url=artist:Shōzaburō Watanabe]Shōzaburō Watanabe[/url] in the early 20th century to revitalize the tradition of ukiyo-e art.\n"
"\n"
"Shin-hanga was more realistic, and heavily incorporated Western elements such as atmospheric light sources, shadow, and perspective. It also eschewed the ukiyo-e tradition of emulating hand-drawn brushstrokes, and often featured \"mottled\" impressions rather than being limited to planes of solid color or gradients."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/styles/style_surimono.tres
msgid "Surimono"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/styles/style_surimono.tres
msgid "Literally \"printed things\", these were privately commissioned woodblock prints created for special occasions like poetry competitions or New Year greetings rather than mass commercial sale. They are characterized by extreme carving precision, lavish materials, and elaborate textures like embossing."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/styles/style_yakusha.tres
msgid "Yakusha-e"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/styles/style_yakusha.tres
msgid "Literally \"actor pictures\", this highly popular genre depicted celebrity kabuki theater actors. They were frequently used as promotional materials and advertisements for plays."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/styles/style_yokohama.tres
msgid "Yokohama-e"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/styles/style_yokohama.tres
msgid "Literally \"Yokohama pictures\", this style boomed during the opening of Japan in the 1860s. It served to document the sudden influx of foreign cultures, Western architecture, international trade, and the daily lives of foreigners living in the port city of Yokohama."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: art/styles/style_yurei.tres
msgid "Yūrei-zu"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: blurb
#: art/styles/style_yurei.tres
msgid ""
"Literally \"ghost illustration\", this style focuses on depicting ghosts, demons, yōkai, and other supernatural beings.\n"
"\n"
"Early examples are mainly e-maki (painted scrolls), rather than woodblock prints. However, after censorship laws in 1842 outlawed the depiction of celebrities, some printers used fantastic creatures as substitutes for real people, including the ruling elite."
msgstr ""

