#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: art/viewer/art_viewer.tscn
msgid ""
"[b]<header_font_size>Intro[/font_size][/b]\n"
"\n"
"The art in Worlds Upon The Wind heavily relies on classical Japanese ukiyo-e woodblock prints, and to a lesser extent on older e-maki, or picture scrolls.\n"
"\n"
"Woodblock printing was invented in China in the early 3rd century, and made its way to Japan in the 8th century. It consists of carving a wooden block, inking it, and pressing it onto paper to efficiently create multiple impressions. It was first used for text, but was eventually adapted to images, and reached its peak in the Edo period (1603-1868). At this point print production involved 4 roles: the publisher who funded the project, the artist who designed the image, the carver who chiseled the wood, and the printer who pressed the paper.\n"
"\n"
"Ukiyo-e (浮世絵) is a style of woodblock printing that started out by depicting courtesans, kabuki actors, and sumo wrestlers, then expanded to include landscapes and a variety of other subjects. Most of the art used in Worlds Upon The Wind falls into the latter category.\n"
"\n"
"The term \"ukiyo\" (憂き世), translated as \"sorrowful world\", originated in Buddhist philosophy, and referred to the fleeting, impermanent nature of life, full of sorrow. However, its homophone 浮世, translated as \"floating world\", took on a hedonistic view of the concept - if life is so fleeting, one might as well enjoy it. It quickly became associated with the pleasure quarters and their raucous image, which is how the term \"ukiyo-e\" (pictures of the floating world) came about, as it started out by depicting people often found in the pleasure quarters.\n"
"\n"
"The game pays homage to both versions of the term, as well as the literal meaning of floating worlds."
msgstr ""

