#. TRANSLATORS: Property: record_title
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_autocracy.tres
msgid "Debate Speech: The Power of One"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_autocracy.tres
msgid ""
"PAGE: historian(PORTAL_NETWORK), AUDIENCE\n"
"historian: There is a shard out there in the Cloud Sea where one man's word is the law.\n"
"historian: It is remarkably effective.\n"
"historian: When the Overseer declares that something must be done, it gets done.\n"
"historian: If he says that a river must be dammed, it is dammed.\n"
"historian: If he says that a town must be replaced by a palace, it is replaced by a palace.\n"
"historian: I'm sure none of us would want to live on that shard.\n"
"historian: Yet the Scribe says that we must let a person lead not just one shard, but the entire world.\n"
"historian: Now, I have gotten to know the Avatar, and I have no doubt that they want only the best for our people.\n"
"historian: But what happens when the next leader comes, once we are used to taking orders from a single voice?\n"
"historian: Can we truly trust that no tyrant or fool will ever misuse this power?"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: record_title
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_big_dam.tres
msgid "Debate Speech: The Ancient Aqueducts"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_big_dam.tres
msgid ""
"PAGE: scribe(PORTAL_NETWORK), AUDIENCE\n"
"scribe: The Explorer recently brought to me an architectural draft, unearthed from the foundation of a newly settled city.\n"
"scribe: It depicts a sprawling network of aqueducts, so massive it rivals in size our greatest shards.\n"
"scribe: Our friend marveled at the sheer impossibility of its construction, and rightly so.\n"
"scribe: No single shard today could ever muster the resources or the coordination required for such a monumental endeavor.\n"
"scribe: In the days before the Shattering, our ancestors achieved the impossible because they moved as one body, uplifted by a unified vision.\n"
"scribe: Today, we celebrate our fragmentation as a triumph of independence.\n"
"scribe: Each local council acts as its own sovereign, every individual striving towards their personal glory.\n"
"scribe: Yet when each of us demands to be the hero of their own story, no one remains to follow a greater cause, and our collective strength is scattered to the winds.\n"
"scribe: We content ourselves with small victories, acting like the frog in a well who mocks the vast ocean for being too large for comfort.\n"
"scribe: What hope do we have of achieving lasting greatness if we refuse to lift a shared burden?\n"
"scribe: The Enlightened One reborn offers us a banner under which to unite, free from the folly of our pride.\n"
"scribe: Together, we can once again raise wonders that will elevate all of humanity."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: record_title
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_collision.tres
msgid "Debate Speech: Shard Collisions"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_collision.tres
msgid ""
"PAGE: historian(PORTAL_NETWORK), AUDIENCE\n"
"historian: The Scribe tells us that when the Enlightened One's Abode joined with its neighbor shard, it was a divine sign.\n"
"historian: But it isn't the first time that has happened.\n"
"historian: I have here a report from the Explorer, describing a massive shard that used to be three separate landmasses, a fact widely known by its inhabitants.\n"
"historian: These landmasses drifted together centuries ago and none of us knew about it.\n"
"historian: It turns out that when ten thousand islands drift in the Cloud Sea, sometimes they bump into each other.\n"
"historian: We are so desperate for a grand narrative to make sense of our shattered world, that we look at natural coincidences and call them destiny.\n"
"historian: If two leaves floating down a stream stick together, we don't see a divine plan, but when the same happens on a larger scale, we attribute meaning to the event.\n"
"historian: It is a dangerous thing to base the future of our civilization on a coincidence."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: record_title
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_communal_joy.tres
msgid "Debate Speech: Communal Joy"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_communal_joy.tres
msgid ""
"PAGE: avatar_adult(PORTAL_NETWORK), AUDIENCE\n"
"avatar_adult: The two scholars speak of grand plans and historical mistakes, but I find myself drawn to something simpler.\n"
"avatar_adult: When I walk among our people, I rarely see them debating how the land should be ruled. I see them trying to stay warm.\n"
"avatar_adult: The Cloud Sea is vast.\n"
"avatar_adult: It is a terrifying thing to look out to the horizon and feel entirely alone.\n"
"avatar_adult: What do people do when they are cold and afraid?\n"
"avatar_adult: They huddle together under a warm blanket.\n"
"avatar_adult: When a village gathers to sing the same song and eat from the same pot, they are pulling that shared blanket over their shoulders.\n"
"avatar_adult: Is there a better joy than knowing that your neighbor understands your heart?\n"
"avatar_adult: If our shared faith does nothing else but keep us from feeling alone in this vast sea, then I think it is a wonderful thing."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: record_title
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_cooperation.tres
msgid "Debate Speech: Cooperation"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_cooperation.tres
msgid ""
"PAGE: historian(PORTAL_NETWORK), AUDIENCE\n"
"historian: I have recently learned of an old shard that to this day does not have a capital.\n"
"historian: They have no shard council or central treasury.\n"
"historian: They can't even agree on a standard unit of measurement.\n"
"historian: By all conventional logic, a place like that should have collapsed the moment a bad storm rolled in.\n"
"historian: And a bad storm did roll in, devastating an entire town.\n"
"historian: But instead of being left to die, the locals found themselves overrun by thatchers and masons from neighboring villages.\n"
"historian: These workers showed up entirely unprompted and started fixing the roofs.\n"
"historian: Our political philosophers tell us that without an authority to maintain order, people would turn on each other.\n"
"historian: But this shard shows us that cooperation doesn't need to be enforced by law.\n"
"historian: When the roof caves in, helping your neighbor lift the beam is what allowed us to survive to this day.\n"
"historian: It is a fundamental instinct we humans possess.\n"
"historian: We do not need a grand empire to make us work together.\n"
"historian: We just need to listen to our conscience."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: record_title
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_doubt.tres
msgid "Debate Speech: The Necessity of Doubt"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_doubt.tres
msgid ""
"PAGE: historian(PORTAL_NETWORK), AUDIENCE\n"
"historian: The Scribe treats doubt as weakness of spirit.\n"
"historian: He believes that if we do not offer absolute certainty, the people will drown in despair.\n"
"historian: But certainty is a very rigid thing.\n"
"historian: Consider the pagodas we build today.\n"
"historian: We never fit the joints perfectly tight.\n"
"historian: We deliberately leave empty space for the wood to sway when an earthquake comes.\n"
"historian: Doubt is that empty space.\n"
"historian: It is the wisdom to admit that we do not know everything.\n"
"historian: If we rely on blind faith in one leader, our societies will lose their ability to adapt.\n"
"historian: If we cannot challenge our beliefs, we cannot learn from our mistakes.\n"
"historian: We must leave room for our people to wonder, or we will just be another stone pillar waiting to crumble."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: record_title
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_dragon_mine.tres
msgid "Debate Speech: Sacred Inscriptions"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_dragon_mine.tres
msgid ""
"PAGE: historian(PORTAL_NETWORK), AUDIENCE\n"
"historian: You have all heard of the Ember monks.\n"
"historian: For generations, they have built their monasteries on the volcanoes and revered the dragon spirits said to dwell within the magma chambers.\n"
"historian: The Grand Accord itself was inspired by inscriptions found in these caves.\n"
"historian: But now that we better understand the ancient language, it is time to reevaluate these inscriptions.\n"
"historian: \"Unstable load-bearing pillars.\"\n"
"historian: \"Toxic fumes. Do not breathe.\"\n"
"historian: \"Verify magma pressure before excavation.\"\n"
"historian: This is all wise advice, yes, but words of a divine spirit they are not.\n"
"historian: We are quick to dress up our own ignorance in the robes of divinity.\n"
"historian: A ruin we do not understand must be a holy temple.\n"
"historian: A disaster we didn't foresee must be divine will.\n"
"historian: It is easier to bow before a grand mystery than to patiently unearth mundane truth.\n"
"historian: If we keep hoping for supernatural salvation, we will stop working on real solutions."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: record_title
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_ecological_instability.tres
msgid "Debate Speech: The Ecological Report"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_ecological_instability.tres
msgid ""
"PAGE: historian(PORTAL_NETWORK), AUDIENCE\n"
"historian: The most recent addition to our museum here is a letter written to a ruler from before the Shattering.\n"
"historian: It warns of rising seas and endless droughts.\n"
"historian: We have spent centuries telling ourselves that the Old World was a paradise, brought low by a sudden, mysterious disaster.\n"
"historian: But this letter tells a more mundane story.\n"
"historian: Our ancestors stripped the earth bare, and ignored the warnings until the ground beneath them gave way.\n"
"historian: The Scribe urges us to look backward, to emulate the majesty of those ancient empires and rebuild their unified world.\n"
"historian: But why would we want to rebuild a house built on a rotten foundation?\n"
"historian: We must stop viewing the world through the lens of ancient myths.\n"
"historian: We need to look at our soil, study our water, and understand the limits of our environment.\n"
"historian: If we blindly repeat the methods of the past, we will eventually run out of world to shatter."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: record_title
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_electricity.tres
msgid "Debate Speech: The Miracle of Lightning"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_electricity.tres
msgid ""
"PAGE: historian(PORTAL_NETWORK), AUDIENCE\n"
"historian: My friend recently returned from a shard that is constantly battered by storms.\n"
"historian: Any sensible person would have dealt with this by building a thicker roof and staying indoors.\n"
"historian: But the locals there decided to do something absurd.\n"
"historian: They flew kites into the thunderclouds trying to catch the lightning.\n"
"historian: If a scholar came to me and asked me if that was a good idea, I'd have responded with a resounding no.\n"
"historian: But the people of that shard didn't have an authority figure to tell them whether their ideas had merit.\n"
"historian: Now they stuff lightning into jars, and use it to power lanterns that need no fuel.\n"
"historian: Letting our shards develop independently frees people to make their own choices.\n"
"historian: It means we get to see what happens when ten thousand different ideas are unchained from the shackles of conventional thought."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: record_title
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_environmental_monitoring.tres
msgid "Debate Speech: Observing Nature"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_environmental_monitoring.tres
msgid ""
"PAGE: scribe(PORTAL_NETWORK), AUDIENCE\n"
"scribe: The Historian often dismisses our ancient rites as idle contemplation, demanding physical proof from the divine.\n"
"scribe: Such views are blinded by a lack of patience.\n"
"scribe: The Enlightened One's wisdom is a seed, planted in the minds of the faithful to sprout in the fullness of time.\n"
"scribe: At the dawn of this era, a shrine was consecrated on an island in a lake.\n"
"scribe: The priests there were inspired into a ritual of patience: to meditate daily upon the exact line where the gate's pillars meet the water.\n"
"scribe: To an impatient mind, such practice appears an exercise in futility.\n"
"scribe: But consider the fruits of this unwavering devotion!\n"
"scribe: Through their steadfast vigilance, the priests began to record the swelling of the tides.\n"
"scribe: They learned to measure the heat of the air and foresee the coming tempests.\n"
"scribe: Their faith was a lens through which the world revealed its secrets.\n"
"scribe: Divine wisdom is profound and subtle.\n"
"scribe: It does not merely hand us unearned revelations.\n"
"scribe: It commands us to cultivate the patience required to comprehend the universe.\n"
"scribe: True stewardship of our environment cannot be achieved through the arbitrary whims of ten thousand shards.\n"
"scribe: It requires a shared vision, uniting us in the sacred journey to everlasting prosperity."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: record_title
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_extinction.tres
msgid "Debate Speech: The Possiblity of Extinction"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_extinction.tres
msgid ""
"PAGE: historian(PORTAL_NETWORK), AUDIENCE\n"
"historian: I have some somber news to report.\n"
"historian: As far as we can tell, the shard that held our very first shrine is no longer inhabited.\n"
"historian: I consulted the archives to understand what happened.\n"
"historian: It seems the tragedy is rooted in the same day that the shrine was consecrated.\n"
"historian: That day, a wild animal damaged the shrine's fence.\n"
"historian: In their zeal to protect the holy ground, the priests declared the local wildlife to be evil spirits.\n"
"historian: Over the centuries, bit by bit, they damaged the natural balance so much that the land could no longer support them.\n"
"historian: They had ten thousand chances to mend their ways, yet they clung to their mistaken beliefs.\n"
"historian: But can we truly blame them?\n"
"historian: They had been told that it is the unquestionable will of the Enlightened One.\n"
"historian: If we follow a rigid, universal faith without question, we will have no chance to correct our course.\n"
"historian: We must remain free to trust our own common sense, and to make our own choices."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: record_title
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_final.tres
msgid "The Final Debate Speech"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_final.tres
msgid ""
"PAGE: avatar_adult(PORTAL_NETWORK), AUDIENCE\n"
"avatar_adult: Many of you have waited for a long time to hear the will of the Enlightened One.\n"
"avatar_adult: I've waited my whole life for it too.\n"
"avatar_adult: When I first met the Historian she asked me if I was the Enlightened One.\n"
"avatar_adult: I said that although I wasn't, I could feel the divine will.\n"
"avatar_adult: But in my heart, all I felt was doubt.\n"
"avatar_adult: I felt broken, because I didn't hear the Enlightened One's words.\n"
"avatar_adult: We all feel broken sometimes, don't we?\n"
"avatar_adult: Recently, the Enlightened One's words finally reached me.\n"
"avatar_adult: At first, they made me feel more broken, because I knew I wouldn't bring the shards together.\n"
"avatar_adult: And because I knew they were the last words I would hear from the Enlightened One.\n"
"avatar_adult: But as I meditated on what they meant, I looked out across the Cloud Sea.\n"
"avatar_adult: Some of us see the New World as broken, but maybe it's just because we expect it to be different.\n"
"avatar_adult: If we stop looking at the past for how the world should be, we stop seeing a broken world.\n"
"avatar_adult: Look at what we have built on these floating shards.\n"
"avatar_adult: Ten thousand little worlds, each wonderful in its own way.\n"
"avatar_adult: The Scribe and the Historian have spent many afternoons sharing their wisdom with us.\n"
"avatar_adult: When a people can raise such thoughtful, caring voices, the leader's job is already done.\n"
"avatar_adult: I am sure the Enlightened One would want us to never stop debating and trying to make our world better.\n"
"avatar_adult: But today, the Enlightened One has found rest, content to live on in our hearts and the new stories we will compose.\n"
"avatar_adult: Let us turn over a new leaf.\n"
"avatar_adult: Instead of counting our years from the Shattering, let us call the next year the first of a new age.\n"
"avatar_adult: One where the Enlightened One speaks not through a single Avatar, but through each of us.\n"
"avatar_adult: It is a beautiful day in the Cloud Sea.\n"
"avatar_adult: I will step through the gate now, and find myself an ordinary life.\n"
"avatar_adult: I hope to be a good neighbor to you all."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: record_title
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_foreign_poem.tres
msgid "Debate Speech: The Foreign Poem"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_foreign_poem.tres
msgid ""
"PAGE: scribe(PORTAL_NETWORK), AUDIENCE\n"
"scribe: The Historian champions the independent development of our scattered shards, praising it as a garden of unique cultures.\n"
"scribe: The Explorer brings us tales of one such culture.\n"
"scribe: It is a place where the written word has been abandoned, and the people communicate through complex, rhyming verse.\n"
"scribe: When asked for directions, a local responded with a sophisticated poem, replete with homophones and local metaphors.\n"
"scribe: It was a masterpiece of wit and beauty, yet to the listener, it was incomprehensible.\n"
"scribe: We were blessed with the gift of art to bridge the rifts between our hearts.\n"
"scribe: But for the arts to truly connect us, the artist's spirit must stir the soul of their audience.\n"
"scribe: What good is a masterful poem if your neighbor a shard away cannot comprehend the cultural foundation it stands upon?\n"
"scribe: By letting our societies drift apart in the Cloud Sea, we are dismantling the bridges of empathy.\n"
"scribe: We are choosing to isolate ourselves, becoming strangers to one another.\n"
"scribe: Let us instead embrace a common foundation, ensuring that the beauty we create can once again be shared by all."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: record_title
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_golden_age.tres
msgid "Debate Speech: The Golden Age"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_golden_age.tres
msgid ""
"PAGE: historian(PORTAL_NETWORK), AUDIENCE\n"
"historian: Through the ages we have looked up to the Old World as a dream to restore.\n"
"historian: I understand the appeal.\n"
"historian: It is comforting to think that if we just put the shards back together, all our problems will be solved.\n"
"historian: But we cannot forget why the world shattered in the first place.\n"
"historian: Our ancestors were not a happy people, content with the world they had.\n"
"historian: They cut out forests to build palaces, and dug so deep that the earth finally gave way beneath them.\n"
"historian: Despite all their riches, they were never satisfied, and that was the root of the problem.\n"
"historian: If all we achieve is to bring back that world, we will just continue the cycle of greed.\n"
"historian: We have a chance to build something new on these shards, something informed by the mistakes of the past.\n"
"historian: I would much rather we build modest homes that stand than a grand palace which is doomed to fall."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: record_title
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_hedonism.tres
msgid "Debate Speech: Hedonism"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_hedonism.tres
msgid ""
"PAGE: scribe(PORTAL_NETWORK), AUDIENCE\n"
"scribe: We often hear of shards that have achieved material abundance, and many a voice sings praises for these achievements.\n"
"scribe: But let us examine deeply the cost of such mundane success.\n"
"scribe: The Explorer brings us tales of a shard whose granaries perpetually overflow.\n"
"scribe: Freed from the struggle for survival, what monumental works did these people pursue?\n"
"scribe: What higher truths did they seek?\n"
"scribe: They sought nothing but the perfection of their next meal.\n"
"scribe: They abandoned the teachings of the Enlightened One to build shrines celebrating intoxicants.\n"
"scribe: They spend their days debating the thickness of noodles, while their spirits languish in comfortable apathy.\n"
"scribe: This is the insidious danger of a purely material collective.\n"
"scribe: Lacking a guiding light to inspire higher thought, the mind degrades into base hedonism.\n"
"scribe: It replaces the pursuit of lasting beauty and worthwhile achievements with the fleeting satisfaction of a full stomach.\n"
"scribe: We were not saved from the Shattering to become well-fed beasts.\n"
"scribe: We must unite under a higher mandate, so our prosperity may elevate our spirits, rather than merely sate our appetites."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: record_title
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_ichi_go_ichi_e.tres
msgid "Debate Speech: A Culture of Tea"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_ichi_go_ichi_e.tres
msgid ""
"PAGE: historian(PORTAL_NETWORK), AUDIENCE\n"
"historian: There's a shard out there in the Cloud Sea with a centuries-long tea tradition.\n"
"historian: Apparently, you cannot walk ten paces there without bumping into a tea house.\n"
"historian: They even hold town council meetings in them.\n"
"historian: But the funny thing is, they don't much care about the tea itself.\n"
"historian: They just pluck whatever leaves are growing nearby, toss them in hot water, and call it a day.\n"
"historian: Some may say that these people have taken the easy way out, given up on achieving a truly masterful drink.\n"
"historian: But I think they are a wise people who saw the true meaning in sharing a drink.\n"
"historian: Instead of chasing unattainable perfection, they pay attention to the joy already in their grasp.\n"
"historian: The joy of sitting on a worn cushion and laughing at a neighbor's jokes.\n"
"historian: We could spend lifetimes trying to rebuild a perfect world, but in the process, won't we miss the ordinary, imperfect beauty sitting right in front of us?"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: record_title
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_industrial_expansion.tres
msgid "Debate Speech: Coordinated Scholarship"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_industrial_expansion.tres
msgid ""
"PAGE: scribe(PORTAL_NETWORK), AUDIENCE\n"
"scribe: I bear in my hands an ancient record, a decree marshaling a hundred scholars to study the properties of a single metal.\n"
"scribe: In this shattered world, we praise the isolated genius, idolizing the lone inventor toiling in obscurity.\n"
"scribe: Yet the deepest mysteries of creation yield only to the combined weight of countless minds working in perfect unison.\n"
"scribe: When a thinker on an isolated shard uncovers a brilliant truth, it is but a fragile spark, easily extinguished without peers to fuel it.\n"
"scribe: Without a shared language and purpose, ten thousand scholars might spend their lives making the same initial discovery.\n"
"scribe: They will remain trapped at the threshold of understanding, unable to build upon each other's triumphs.\n"
"scribe: True intellectual advancement demands an architecture of collaboration, so the insights of one generation become the bedrock for the next.\n"
"scribe: To comprehend the vast machinery of the world, we must foster the coordination that only a universal aim can achieve."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: record_title
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_inquisition.tres
msgid "Debate Speech: The Holy Magistrates"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_inquisition.tres
msgid ""
"PAGE: historian(PORTAL_NETWORK), AUDIENCE\n"
"historian: Let us look, today, at the fate of the shard that held our very first shrine in the New World.\n"
"historian: Centuries ago, the priests there decided to question the villagers about a broken fence.\n"
"historian: I suppose people were impressed by how quickly they found the culprit, and asked them to look into other crimes.\n"
"historian: Regardless of how exactly the events escalated, their outcomes are now plain to see.\n"
"historian: Priests patrolling the streets, enforcing the holy duty of confession on anyone they meet, and exacting punishment in their holy tribunals.\n"
"historian: When you give a priest a judge's authority, you create a very dangerous kind of justice.\n"
"historian: A judge knows they are just enforcing a rule.\n"
"historian: A holy magistrate believes that they are rooting out absolute evil.\n"
"historian: There is no limit to the cruelty a person can justify when they are convinced that they are enacting a divine plan.\n"
"historian: The Scribe believes that uniting us under a single religious mandate will bring universal harmony.\n"
"historian: But this shard's story is a lesson to us all about what happens when faith becomes the law."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: record_title
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_investment.tres
msgid "Debate Speech: The Ancestral Legacy"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_investment.tres
msgid ""
"PAGE: scribe(PORTAL_NETWORK), AUDIENCE\n"
"scribe: Consider a village where the springs have suddenly run dry.\n"
"scribe: To survive, the people decide to dig a new well.\n"
"scribe: The first generation digs up the soil until their shovels can go no deeper.\n"
"scribe: The second generation makes pickaxes to break the rocks and hauls them up, basket by basket.\n"
"scribe: Now the third generation stands at the bottom of the pit.\n"
"scribe: The work is agonizing, but the stone is finally damp.\n"
"scribe: It would be so very easy for them to throw down their tools, and say the work is too difficult to continue.\n"
"scribe: But if they stop, they do not merely accept their own thirst.\n"
"scribe: They accept that their parents and grandparents suffered in the dark for nothing.\n"
"scribe: We are that third generation.\n"
"scribe: The Enlightened One saved our ancestors from destruction, and they accepted that miracle with a promise to make the world whole again.\n"
"scribe: We carry centuries of their sacrifices on our shoulders.\n"
"scribe: We have no right to simply shrug them off."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: record_title
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_kind_justice.tres
msgid "Debate Speech: Approaches to Justice"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_kind_justice.tres
msgid ""
"PAGE: historian(PORTAL_NETWORK), AUDIENCE\n"
"historian: Have you heard of the shard with no judges or jails?\n"
"historian: When someone there commits a crime, the community holds a hearing to list every kind thing that person has ever done.\n"
"historian: It might sound ludicrous to us, yet this approach has worked for them all these centuries.\n"
"historian: When a society grows too large, it loses this natural goodness.\n"
"historian: It has to invent strict justice to keep order.\n"
"historian: When a central authority declares laws from a distant capital, they are rigid, blunt tools.\n"
"historian: They have to be, to apply in every situation.\n"
"historian: But in an independent community, people know each other.\n"
"historian: Your friends know your flaws, but they also know your circumstances, and can temper their judgement with care.\n"
"historian: If we leave moral judgement to divine commandments, we are forced to trust a blind executioner carrying out orders with no regard for our individuality."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: record_title
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_lost_history.tres
msgid "Debate Speech: A Shard That Forgot Its History"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_lost_history.tres
msgid ""
"PAGE: scribe(PORTAL_NETWORK), AUDIENCE\n"
"scribe: We recently heard from a shard where the gate has malfunctioned for generations, leaving its people marooned.\n"
"scribe: Those isolated souls believe themselves to be entirely alone in the void, having forgotten the other shards and the true history of our world.\n"
"scribe: When a community is severed from our shared story, the steady march of generations inevitably erases an uncomfortable past.\n"
"scribe: The Historian and I are in complete agreement that the follies of our ancestors must not be forgotten.\n"
"scribe: Where we differ is that I am unwilling to gamble with so solemn a duty.\n"
"scribe: When every village is left to write its own history, they will always cast themselves as the victims, never the architects of their own ruin.\n"
"scribe: By binding ourselves to the Enlightened One's teachings, we prevent our descendants from editing their way into another apocalypse."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: record_title
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_magic_chemicals.tres
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_magic_fire.tres
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_magic_science.tres
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_magic_self_harm.tres
msgid "Debate Speech: Magic Rituals"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_magic_chemicals.tres
msgid ""
"PAGE: historian(PORTAL_NETWORK), AUDIENCE\n"
"historian: Back when the priests among our ancestors practiced magical rituals, they settled a shard we now call The Fox Spirits Domain.\n"
"historian: When they first met these foxes, they considered them evil spirits, and poured circles of salt to ward them off.\n"
"historian: Over the centuries, that simple warding ritual became an obsession.\n"
"historian: In their zeal against spiritual impurity, they poisoned their soil and turned their landscape grey.\n"
"historian: They still hang on, surviving on hardy roots which are among the few plants that tolerate the harsh environment.\n"
"historian: When we try to enforce a holy order on nature, we risk destroying it.\n"
"historian: The real world is not a perfect temple designed for human purpose.\n"
"historian: It is a muddy, chaotic mess full of systems we don't understand.\n"
"historian: For one, we don't understand why the shards float the way they do, and until we learn that, trying to force them together at any cost is not a wise plan."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_magic_fire.tres
msgid ""
"PAGE: historian(PORTAL_NETWORK), AUDIENCE\n"
"historian: Back when the priests among our ancestors practiced magical rituals, they settled a shard we now call The Fox Spirits Domain.\n"
"historian: When they first met these foxes, they considered them evil spirits, and decided to banish them with fire and axe.\n"
"historian: Their descendants adopted fire as the solution to every inconvenience.\n"
"historian: If the spring pollen made them sneeze, they solved it by burning the forest down.\n"
"historian: It is a profound tragedy, how quickly a single act of misplaced zeal can become a tradition that leaves the world barren.\n"
"historian: We could look at their empty plains and conclude that people are doomed to destroy whatever they touch.\n"
"historian: But the people there do not burn forests anymore.\n"
"historian: They recognized the harm they were doing and changed their ways.\n"
"historian: They still keep the tradition alive, but instead of burning down forests, they build safe bonfires during festivals.\n"
"historian: This is the great strength of letting our people forge their own paths.\n"
"historian: They are allowed to make terrible mistakes, but they are also free to realize their folly and correct their course.\n"
"historian: If we bind all our shards to a single, unquestionable holy law, we lose the ability to change our minds."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_magic_science.tres
msgid ""
"PAGE: historian(PORTAL_NETWORK), AUDIENCE\n"
"historian: Back when the priests among our ancestors practiced magical rituals, they settled a shard we now call The Fox Spirits Domain.\n"
"historian: Even though the Scribes of the past called these foxes evil spirits, the priests of this shard chose to study them instead of taking immediate action.\n"
"historian: By putting away their holy wards and opening their eyes, they learned to see the world as it is.\n"
"historian: Today they learn medicine by watching what herbs sick monkeys chew on, and protect their crops by breeding the right kind of beetles.\n"
"historian: Our history is full of stories of looking at things we do not understand and calling them divine mysteries.\n"
"historian: We let these mysteries inspire elaborate rituals, in an effort to tame the unknown.\n"
"historian: But these rituals are just the first, clumsy steps toward understanding the world.\n"
"historian: If we turn the Scribe's holy mandates into absolute laws, we will freeze our minds in the present and stop learning.\n"
"historian: We must leave our people free to put down the sacred texts and look at reality not as it ought to be, but as it really is."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_magic_self_harm.tres
msgid ""
"PAGE: historian(PORTAL_NETWORK), AUDIENCE\n"
"historian: Back when the priests among our ancestors practiced magical rituals, they settled a shard we now call The Fox Spirits Domain.\n"
"historian: When they first met these foxes, they considered them evil spirits, and tried to ward them off by performing blood sacrifice.\n"
"historian: Their descendants on that shard took this lesson to heart.\n"
"historian: Now, they bleed themselves to cure a fever and to beg the heavens for rain.\n"
"historian: It is a strange thing, how easily we can convince ourselves that suffering is a holy act.\n"
"historian: When we decide that a painful ritual is what the heavens demand, we stop looking for practical solutions.\n"
"historian: We are often told that the hardships of trying to rebuild the Old World are a necessary, holy sacrifice.\n"
"historian: But pain is not a currency that buys us favor with the universe.\n"
"historian: It is just pain.\n"
"historian: We should choose a path that seeks to heal our communities, rather than proving our devotion by how much we are willing to hurt ourselves."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: record_title
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_monk_rulers.tres
msgid "Debate Speech: The Monk Rulers"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_monk_rulers.tres
msgid ""
"PAGE: historian(PORTAL_NETWORK), AUDIENCE\n"
"historian: Let us look, today, at the fate of the shard that held our very first shrine in the New World.\n"
"historian: Centuries ago, the priests there decided to bar laypeople from entering a shrine after some fences were damaged.\n"
"historian: It seems a harmless precaution, but over the centuries this elevation of the divine over the mundane has had dire consequences.\n"
"historian: When my friend the Explorer visited the shard, he was arrested for nothing more than writing in his journal.\n"
"historian: You see, literacy on this shard is a privilege reserved for the holy order.\n"
"historian: When we allow our priests to control our laws, we create a dangerous hierarchy.\n"
"historian: A city council answers to the citizens.\n"
"historian: A holy magistrate answers to none but the spirits.\n"
"historian: And who is to say whether spirits are just?\n"
"historian: Who, indeed, but the priests themselves?\n"
"historian: I believe we are capable of developing our own morals, learning from our successes and failures, and adapting to the needs of the age.\n"
"historian: The Scribe believes that uniting us under a single religious mandate will bring universal harmony.\n"
"historian: But this shard's story is a lesson to us all about what happens when faith becomes the law."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: record_title
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_moral_leadership.tres
msgid "Debate Speech: Moral Leadership"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_moral_leadership.tres
msgid ""
"PAGE: scribe(PORTAL_NETWORK), AUDIENCE\n"
"scribe: My esteemed colleague often warns us of the dangers of an organized faith.\n"
"scribe: I invite you instead to look at the shard that holds the first shrine we established after the Shattering.\n"
"scribe: The very day that the shrine was consecrated, a miscreant broke the fences of that sacred site.\n"
"scribe: Rather than punishing the trespass or erecting higher walls, the monks tore the fences down.\n"
"scribe: They opened their grounds to all, welcoming the faithful to share in the Enlightened One's light.\n"
"scribe: Today, the shrines on that shard are the beating hearts of each settlement.\n"
"scribe: Children learn to read and write from the priests.\n"
"scribe: When disputes arise, the people of the shard trust in divine wisdom rather than letting the local ruling council decide their fate.\n"
"scribe: Their donation box is left unlocked, brimming with coins, yet none is tempted to take what is not theirs.\n"
"scribe: You cannot achieve so complete a trust by following legal codes which change with every passing season.\n"
"scribe: You need a shared faith in ageless wisdom to guide you towards what is right.\n"
"scribe: It is the scaffolding which raises a society above its base temptations."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: record_title
#. Property: text
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_plague.tres
#: hub/museum/museum_detail_dialogue.tscn
msgid "Debate Speech: Plague"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_plague.tres
msgid ""
"PAGE: historian(PORTAL_NETWORK), AUDIENCE\n"
"historian: I recently received a new museum exhibit from one of the shards.\n"
"historian: It is a human bone pierced by a thousand little holes.\n"
"historian: The scholars of that shard speak of a terrible bone-eating plague that once swept across the ancient world.\n"
"historian: We often look at our scattered shards and see only isolation, treating our separation as a tragedy that must be fixed.\n"
"historian: But we fail to see the advantages of our current state.\n"
"historian: A united world is a fragile world.\n"
"historian: When everyone shares the same roads, they also share the same disasters.\n"
"historian: If a sickness takes root in a unified empire, it will travel every highway until there is nowhere left to hide.\n"
"historian: The Cloud Sea ensures that if one shard falls, the rest of humanity survives.\n"
"historian: The Scribe wants to stitch the world back together.\n"
"historian: But if we remove all the empty space between us, we remove the gaps that keep a fire from spreading."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: record_title
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_redistribution.tres
msgid "Debate Speech: Sharing Food"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_redistribution.tres
msgid ""
"PAGE: avatar_adult(PORTAL_NETWORK), AUDIENCE\n"
"avatar_adult: Since I came to the Haven, I have been blessed by many tales from other shards.\n"
"avatar_adult: And whenever I hear of people going hungry, my heart just aches.\n"
"avatar_adult: Some of these shards suffered famines for generations.\n"
"avatar_adult: Even now, with full bellies, the memory of starvation lies deep in their souls.\n"
"avatar_adult: No one should have to live with that kind of lingering terror.\n"
"avatar_adult: While they were starving, there were other shards in the Cloud Sea wondering what to do with all their excess grain.\n"
"avatar_adult: Coming together means that when one community suffers a drought, the others can send baskets full of food to see them through.\n"
"avatar_adult: If we choose to share our paths, we become each other's keepers.\n"
"avatar_adult: A bad harvest becomes a shared burden, easily carried.\n"
"avatar_adult: We can hold each other up, and make sure that no one ever has to face a bitter winter alone."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: record_title
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_royal_monument.tres
msgid "Debate Speech: The Royal Monument"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_royal_monument.tres
msgid ""
"PAGE: historian(PORTAL_NETWORK), AUDIENCE\n"
"historian: I have recently heard of a curious monument on an otherwise ordinary shard.\n"
"historian: It is a metal statue of an ancient ruler, standing three stories tall.\n"
"historian: It makes me think of the labor and resources poured into such a project.\n"
"historian: It must have taken hundreds of people and enough metal to make ten thousand hammers.\n"
"historian: All to stoke one person's vanity.\n"
"historian: When we raise a person to a pedestal overlooking the entire world, their whims become the law of the land.\n"
"historian: When a leader governs millions, people become numbers in a ledger.\n"
"historian: They become resources to be allocated, and having a few hundred people forge a statue is hardly a significant expenditure.\n"
"historian: A society on that scale becomes a blind, hungry beast, uncaring of its own citizens.\n"
"historian: If we are led by one state, we will turn ourselves into fuel for another grand monument."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: record_title
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_sacrifice.tres
msgid "Debate Speech: The Issue of Practicality"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_sacrifice.tres
msgid ""
"PAGE: scribe(PORTAL_NETWORK), AUDIENCE\n"
"scribe: The Historian calls on us to embrace practicality.\n"
"scribe: She claims that to be sensible is to only believe what we can see and touch.\n"
"scribe: But let us look at what practical people do when the work is hard and the challenges are daunting.\n"
"scribe: A practical person sees a chestnut and decides to mash it into a paste to satisfy their hunger.\n"
"scribe: It requires a profoundly impractical person to take that same chestnut and bury it in the soil, trusting in a harvest they might not even live to see.\n"
"scribe: That trust is what we call faith.\n"
"scribe: It is the absolute belief that the future is worth sacrificing for.\n"
"scribe: A society that only starts projects it can finish before sundown will never build anything that outlasts the winter."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: record_title
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_small_industries.tres
msgid "Debate Speech: Small Industries"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_small_industries.tres
msgid ""
"PAGE: historian(PORTAL_NETWORK), AUDIENCE\n"
"historian: I have read that in the Old World, one could buy a robe for the price of two rice balls.\n"
"historian: To achieve such efficiency, these robes were made in great big halls by hundreds of tailors all working off the same pattern.\n"
"historian: They in turn received cloth from a thousand weavers across a dozen provinces.\n"
"historian: And the products were distributed all over the world.\n"
"historian: Today, a robe is made by a chain of artisans, each trusting in the other's skill.\n"
"historian: It is an expensive item that few of us can buy on a whim.\n"
"historian: It seems obvious to prefer a cheaper price, but it comes at a cost to us as a society.\n"
"historian: Would you rather be one of those hundreds of tailors, making the same robe for people you will never meet, or today's artisan, making a robe that your village priest will wear?\n"
"historian: A unified world is efficient, but in it we become identical shingles in a giant roof, unable to see how our efforts impact the world around us."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: record_title
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_talisman_bead.tres
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_talisman_pouch.tres
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_talisman_ring.tres
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_talisman_scent_bag.tres
msgid "Debate Speech: The Talismans"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_talisman_bead.tres
msgid ""
"PAGE: scribe(PORTAL_NETWORK), AUDIENCE\n"
"scribe: Many people today look at our customs as thoughtless imitation.\n"
"scribe: They think we are children following arbitrary rules.\n"
"scribe: But let us examine the humble charcoal beads that so many of us wear.\n"
"scribe: Generations ago, the Enlightened One instructed our ancestors to press these beads from the ashes of old fires.\n"
"scribe: For centuries, we have worn them as a symbol of humility, a reminder of the world we lost.\n"
"scribe: Recently, our scholars and healers have begun to study the hidden properties of ash and charcoal.\n"
"scribe: They have discovered that charcoal naturally draws out impurities, filtering the rot from stagnant water and absorbing poisons from the body.\n"
"scribe: Now we know that the charcoal dust that drops from the beads into our food helps us survive illnesses.\n"
"scribe: We never needed to understand why the beads were important to benefit from them.\n"
"scribe: We can study the world to comprehend the machinery of miracles, without rejecting the ones we cannot yet explain.\n"
"scribe: Faith is the candle we carry to push away the dark, until our knowledge is bright enough to dispel it."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_talisman_pouch.tres
msgid ""
"PAGE: scribe(PORTAL_NETWORK), AUDIENCE\n"
"scribe: Many people today look at our customs as thoughtless imitation.\n"
"scribe: They think we are children following arbitrary rules.\n"
"scribe: But let us examine the humble seed pouches that so many of us wear at our belts.\n"
"scribe: Generations ago, the Enlightened One instructed our ancestors to weave these bags loosely and fill them with seeds.\n"
"scribe: For centuries, we have carried them as a symbol that we are the seeds of a new world, meant to sow what we may not harvest.\n"
"scribe: As we all know now, the loose cloth of our bags allows tiny seeds to slip out and scatter along the paths.\n"
"scribe: We have been unknowingly reseeding barren soil for centuries, lush fields sprouting in our wake.\n"
"scribe: One need not understand ecology to revive the land.\n"
"scribe: One simply needs to follow the divine wisdom.\n"
"scribe: We can study the world to comprehend the machinery of miracles, without rejecting the ones we cannot yet understand.\n"
"scribe: Faith is the candle we carry to push away the dark, until our knowledge is bright enough to dispel it."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_talisman_ring.tres
msgid ""
"PAGE: scribe(PORTAL_NETWORK), AUDIENCE\n"
"scribe: Many people today look at our customs as thoughtless imitation.\n"
"scribe: They think we are children following arbitrary rules.\n"
"scribe: But let us examine the humble copper rings that so many of us wear.\n"
"scribe: Generations ago, the Enlightened One instructed our ancestors to forge these rings.\n"
"scribe: For centuries, we have worn them as a symbol of our unbroken lineage.\n"
"scribe: Recently, our scholars and healers have begun to study the hidden properties of metals.\n"
"scribe: They have discovered that copper repels the unseen corruption that spoils our water and festers in our wounds.\n"
"scribe: Our ancestors who wore those rings while tending their injuries survived fevers that claimed others.\n"
"scribe: They did not need to understand why the rings were important.\n"
"scribe: They only needed to follow the divine wisdom.\n"
"scribe: We can study the world to comprehend the machinery of miracles, without rejecting the ones we cannot yet explain.\n"
"scribe: Faith is the candle we carry to push away the dark, until our knowledge is bright enough to dispel it."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_talisman_scent_bag.tres
msgid ""
"PAGE: scribe(PORTAL_NETWORK), AUDIENCE\n"
"scribe: Many people today look at our customs as thoughtless imitation.\n"
"scribe: They think we are children following arbitrary rules.\n"
"scribe: But let us examine the simple herbal sachets that so many of us carry.\n"
"scribe: Generations ago, the Enlightened One instructed our ancestors to pack small pouches with dried mugwort, mint, and cypress.\n"
"scribe: For centuries, we have worn them as a symbol of our harmony with the woods, a reminder to reject the decadent perfumes of the past.\n"
"scribe: Recently, our scholars and healers have begun to study the hidden properties of these common plants.\n"
"scribe: They have discovered that the oils in these herbs repel the unseen pests that carry disease, and their scent clears the lungs when the winter damp sets in.\n"
"scribe: Our ancestors who kept these sachets tied around their necks survived illnesses that claimed others.\n"
"scribe: They did not need to understand why the herbs were important.\n"
"scribe: They only needed to follow the divine wisdom.\n"
"scribe: We can study the world to comprehend the machinery of miracles, without rejecting the ones we cannot yet explain.\n"
"scribe: Faith is the candle we carry to push away the dark, until our knowledge is bright enough to dispel it."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: record_title
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_theocracy.tres
msgid "Debate Speech: The Utmost Spirituality"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_theocracy.tres
msgid ""
"PAGE: historian(PORTAL_NETWORK), AUDIENCE\n"
"historian: My explorer friend recently visited a shard that functions like one giant monastery.\n"
"historian: It is a society perfectly united by faith.\n"
"historian: The people there never argue and live in absolute peace.\n"
"historian: But they achieve this by treating the physical world as a meaningless distraction.\n"
"historian: When their crops fail, they endure the hunger, convinced that their suffering is a sign of spiritual purity.\n"
"historian: They have achieved perfect harmony by completely giving up their own agency.\n"
"historian: This is the danger of blind faith in a divine will.\n"
"historian: If we believe that our misfortunes are part of a divine plan, we risk losing the very drive that kept us alive after the Shattering.\n"
"historian: We must preserve our distinct voices, and personally speak up when the grand unified plan does not work for us."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: record_title
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_transience.tres
msgid "Debate Speech: Against the Storm"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_transience.tres
msgid ""
"PAGE: historian(PORTAL_NETWORK), AUDIENCE\n"
"historian: Imagine a city where sandstorms cover the buildings up to their roofs every other season.\n"
"historian: Would you stay there, spending your day shoveling dust only to find yourself buried again in the morning?\n"
"historian: Such cities exist, you know?\n"
"historian: And people live there.\n"
"historian: When the storms blow, they pack up their belongings and move across the shard, returning with the next season.\n"
"historian: These people learned that it is easier when you adapt, even if you have to change your way of life.\n"
"historian: They learned to make the best of a situation rather than spend all their effort fighting against a force of nature.\n"
"historian: When the world shattered, we adapted.\n"
"historian: We learned to live on these shards.\n"
"historian: To keep struggling for a dream of Reunification is to spend our lives shoveling sand in a sandstorm."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: record_title
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_twin_gods.tres
msgid "Debate Speech: The Twin Gods"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_twin_gods.tres
msgid ""
"PAGE: scribe(PORTAL_NETWORK), AUDIENCE\n"
"scribe: The Explorer has brought us curious sermons from an isolated shard, penned by survivors who worshipped the sun and the moon.\n"
"scribe: Left adrift after the Shattering, they sought comfort in the celestial bodies above them.\n"
"scribe: We cannot fault them, for a heart desperate for warmth will gather what embers it can find.\n"
"scribe: Their devotion, though genuine, illuminates the profound tragedy of our long silence.\n"
"scribe: They yearned for the Enlightened One, yet we withheld the teachings to ourselves, leaving these souls to grasp at fragments of the divine.\n"
"scribe: By failing to share the unified truth, we abandoned generations to wander the dark alone.\n"
"scribe: We hold the lantern that can guide them home.\n"
"scribe: We cannot hoard its light out of a misplaced respect for their isolation.\n"
"scribe: It is our sacred duty, inspired by the Avatar, to carry this light across the Cloud Sea.\n"
"scribe: We must share the unified truth, so no shard is ever left to endure the long night by themselves."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: record_title
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_warlords.tres
msgid "Debate Speech: Bullies"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_warlords.tres
msgid ""
"PAGE: avatar_adult(PORTAL_NETWORK), AUDIENCE\n"
"avatar_adult: The Explorer told me of a shard where neighbors only look at each other from behind high walls.\n"
"avatar_adult: They spend their whole lives waiting for an attack, treating anyone from the next village over as a monster.\n"
"avatar_adult: It breaks my heart.\n"
"avatar_adult: But I think I understand why it happens.\n"
"avatar_adult: When a town stands completely alone, every stranger looks like a threat.\n"
"avatar_adult: Fear takes root when people feel isolated.\n"
"avatar_adult: And fear makes us do cruel things.\n"
"avatar_adult: If we agree to support each other across the Cloud Sea, we remove that fear.\n"
"avatar_adult: We become a safety net for those who need it most.\n"
"avatar_adult: We can make sure that no local chief gets to bully their people.\n"
"avatar_adult: We can finally tear down those walls, knowing there is a vast community looking out for us all.\n"
"avatar_adult: Let us be brave enough to hold hands, and build a world where we treat each other with kindness."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: record_title
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_war_machine.tres
msgid "Debate Speech: Weapons of War"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_war_machine.tres
msgid ""
"PAGE: historian(PORTAL_NETWORK), AUDIENCE\n"
"historian: Have you ever wondered about the great metal constructs we keep finding on various shards?\n"
"historian: Or why we only find them in the wastelands?\n"
"historian: Well, scholars have done much research into the topic, and as far as we can tell these metal constructs were the cause of the wastelands.\n"
"historian: These were ancient weapons, older than the oldest artifact we have from before the Shattering.\n"
"historian: Even the language used by the people who built them is completely alien to us.\n"
"historian: We have no idea how they worked, and perhaps it makes me a bad scholar, but I hope we never learn.\n"
"historian: We all think war on this scale is the domain of ancient legends, nothing we could ever encounter in this modern world.\n"
"historian: Perhaps the reason we think so is because our shards have limited the scale of conflict.\n"
"historian: A warlord may conquer their shard, but if their ambition extends to other shards, the defenders can simply bar the gates.\n"
"historian: The Cloud Sea might be an inconvenience for our merchants, but it is also a very effective moat."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: record_title
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_weird_lifestyles.tres
msgid "Debate Speech: The Wild People"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: debate/arguments/dlg_argument_weird_lifestyles.tres
msgid ""
"PAGE: historian(PORTAL_NETWORK), AUDIENCE\n"
"historian: I have recently read a report about a shard that completely refused to civilize.\n"
"historian: They live in caves and hollows, refusing to ever fell a tree or break a stone.\n"
"historian: If this shard was a province in a unified world, it would be a complete nightmare.\n"
"historian: You can't collect taxes from a commune that has no private property.\n"
"historian: You can barely even reach them without roads, which they'd refuse to build.\n"
"historian: A central state would have to force these forest dwellers to assimilate.\n"
"historian: But today, these people have the freedom to live in a way that perfectly matches their own conscience, and those that choose a different path are just a gate hop away from a thousand different lifestyles to pick from."
msgstr ""

