msgstr ""

#: shard_types/adventurers/shard_type_adventurers.gd:33
#: shard_types/river/shard_type_river.gd:42
msgid "An established <term_lower:capital>."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: shard_types/adventurers/shard_type_adventurers.tres
msgid "The Restless Seekers"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: shard_name_override
#: shard_types/adventurers/shard_type_adventurers.tres
msgid "Fūkyōjima"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: shard_name_override_meaning
#: shard_types/adventurers/shard_type_adventurers.tres
msgid "Island of Wind-Madness"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/adventurers/shard_type_adventurers.tres
msgid "$shard_name was settled by adventurers and daredevils. It is said that in the shard's early days, a child was not considered educated until they could draw a topographical map of the nearest settlement, yet teaching them to read was considered an optional luxury. \"Literacy is useful,\" the elders of $town1 would say, \"but it will not help you find your way back through the forest in a storm.\""
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/adventurers/shard_type_adventurers.tres
msgid "Architecture in $shard_name developed with a peculiar philosophy: stability was boring. When the architects designed a great bridge across a gorge, they deliberately engineered a wobble into it. They argued that a bridge that does not sway offers no excitement to the spirit, and that walking across a static structure is merely commuting, whereas crossing their bridge was an event. To this day, guardrails are viewed as insults to one's agility."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/adventurers/shard_type_adventurers.tres
msgid "The culture here reveres the \"Party\" above the family unit. Social standing is determined by who you trust to hold your rope when you climb a cliff. The local tea houses are rowdy halls where groups form based on complementary skills. You might see a monk, a merchant, and a woodcutter huddled over a map of the local forest, planning a route just because the monk heard a rumor that getting lost in the deep woods is \"life-changingly terrifying\"."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/adventurers/shard_type_adventurers.tres
msgid "In other shards, the appearance of a dangerous beast is a cause for alarm. Here, it is a cause for jealousy. When a massive beast began terrorizing the outskirts of $town3, instead of mobilizing the guards, the village council held a lottery to choose who got the honor of chasing it first. The winners were paraded through the streets in celebration, while the losers sulked and complained that they never got to have any fun."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/adventurers/shard_type_adventurers.tres
msgid "The Adventurers Guild in $shard_name serves as the de facto government, though it governs lightly. Their primary function is maintaining the Great Board, a massive slab of wood where \"quests\" are posted, though most are merely civic duties wrapped in grandeur. Repairing a roof is framed as \"Summiting the Leaking Tiles\", and delivering grain is \"Bearing the Golden Burden\". It turns out people will happily do mundane chores if you promise them a title and a badge."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/adventurers/shard_type_adventurers.tres
msgid ""
"Well, this was an eventful trip. My very first day at the tavern, I was recruited into a party to \"Scour the Infinite Abyss\". They turned out to be a cleaning crew and we spent the next day cleaning the town well. At least I got a shiny copper badge for it.\n"
"\n"
"As I continued traveling, I found the towns bustling but utterly chaotic. Whoever decides to build something doesn't seem to care much for what the settlement actually needs. They just want to tackle the hardest possible project. Like an inn I stayed at that was hanging between two sides of a canyon on a bunch of ropes. Apparently just building on solid ground is \"too boring\"."
msgstr ""

#: shard_types/archaeology/shard_type_archaeology.gd:28
msgid "At least %d <spot:%s> settlements."
msgstr ""

#: shard_types/archaeology/shard_type_archaeology.gd:29
msgid "An <shop:%s> <term_lower:shop>."
msgstr ""

#: shard_types/archaeology/shard_type_archaeology.gd:30
#: shard_types/food_heaven/shard_type_food_heaven.gd:20
#: shard_types/kind_justice/shard_type_kind_justice.gd:17
#: shard_types/kind_justice/shard_type_kind_justice.gd:18
#: shard_types/medicine/shard_type_medicine.gd:34
#: shard_types/militant/shard_type_militant.gd:38
#: shard_types/mq_wild_monastery/shard_type_mq_wild_monastery.gd:25
#: shard_types/newspaper/shard_type_newspaper.gd:40
#: shard_types/poets/shard_type_poets.gd:17
msgid "At least %d %s."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: shard_types/archaeology/shard_type_archaeology.tres
msgid "The Wasteland Excavators"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: shard_name_override
#: shard_types/archaeology/shard_type_archaeology.tres
msgid "Tsukajima"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: shard_name_override_meaning
#: shard_types/archaeology/shard_type_archaeology.tres
msgid "Barrow Island"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/archaeology/shard_type_archaeology.tres
msgid "The settlers of $shard_name built many of their towns in the shard's wasteland. Although establishing a secure food supply posed great challenges, this unusual choice of habitat led them to the discovery of thick sheets of a strange, unyielding metal scattered under a thin layer of topsoil. Local smiths were frustrated at first, as the metal wouldn't even soften in their hottest forges, but soon adapted, learning to cut, carve, and polish the metal sheets. These wasteland towns became the industrial heart of the shard, outfitting farmers and builders with tools that never rusted or broke."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/archaeology/shard_type_archaeology.tres
msgid "Besides being an industrial hub, the wasteland attracted many scholars eager to study the artifacts in the underground ruins. Among the most unusual finds were stacks of thick paper, thousands of sheets bearing the exact same writing. What baffled the scholars even more was the text, as the script appeared to bear no relation even to the language used before the Shattering. They concluded that these papers were intended to reach a vast number of people, though the meaning they were meant to convey remains a mystery."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/archaeology/shard_type_archaeology.tres
msgid "As the excavations of the wasteland on $shard_name continued, they found more and more Iron Wrecks, large hollow structures made of the same indestructible metal so common on this shard. As scholars have studied these in more detail, they have found openings leading into bellies filled with rows of heavy metal eggs. While the purpose of these artifacts remains debated, they are known to be hollow, as the empty shells of such eggs are sometimes found embedded into the glass of the wasteland's glass dunes."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/archaeology/shard_type_archaeology.tres
msgid "Today, the archaeological explorations on $shard_name have mostly stopped. The tunnels under the wasteland have been mapped almost completely, and local archives have exhaustive catalogues of all the artifacts found within. While much of the past remains shrouded, the scholars have been able to disprove the earlier assumption that it was a surface settlement swallowed by millennia of dust. They discovered metal stairways leading up from several corridors, with thick metal trapdoors at the top, which proved that the entire structure must have been built underground from the start."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/archaeology/shard_type_archaeology.tres
msgid ""
"At first I found this shard desolate, with the wasteland surrounding most of the cities, but it quickly grew on me after I got to visit the underground ruins they have mapped out. That place is huge! I spent hours walking through the tunnels and never reached the end. It's a whole city down there, full of ancient pipes and pieces of strange machines.\n"
"\n"
"I was hoping to bring back a few of the artifacts for our museum, but the only one they let me take was a thick sheet of paper with text in an unknown ancient language. They have a room stacked full of papers identical to it, so they give them out freely to visiting scholars."
msgstr ""

#: shard_types/autocracy/shard_type_autocracy.gd:20
msgid "Acquire the <term:relic.inheritance_scroll> <term_lower:relic> during the <spot:%s> <term_lower:event>: <event:%s>."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: shard_types/autocracy/shard_type_autocracy.tres
msgid "The Managed Estate"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: shard_name_override
#: shard_types/autocracy/shard_type_autocracy.tres
msgid "Kiritsu no Kuni"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: shard_name_override_meaning
#: shard_types/autocracy/shard_type_autocracy.tres
msgid "Law Province"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/autocracy/shard_type_autocracy.tres
msgid "Following the first census, Overseer Kinzo, the Enlightened One's chosen, took his duties seriously. To ensure food security, all grain grown on $shard_name was collected at the capital, weighed, taxed, and redistributed, often to the same farms that grew it. Those questioning this marvel of administrative efficiency often found their ration forms misplaced in the agriculture ministry's offices for months at a time."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/autocracy/shard_type_autocracy.tres
msgid "Yield management reached a new level of efficiency when each town was ordered to focus entirely on one industry. For example, $town1 was tasked with delivering nothing but chopsticks. This left the town with no firewood, threatening to leave it frozen, but the Overseer saved the day by sending a massive shipment of timber, proving once again the wisdom of his leadership."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/autocracy/shard_type_autocracy.tres
msgid "To ensure civic harmony, the culture ministry enacted the Statute of Civic Etiquette. These laws standardized such things as the length of sleeves, the depth of polite bows, and the proper way to address His Excellency the Overseer. Those breaking the laws find the unmapped edges of the shard quite character-building, while those reporting such violations are invited to attend the cultural stewardship meetings at the capital."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/autocracy/shard_type_autocracy.tres
msgid "Today, the administration considers $shard_name to have achieved a state of perfect, if fragile, balance. To protect this achievement from outside corruption, the torii gates are sealed, and travel is strictly forbidden. The official records speak of a paradise where every citizen knows their exact role, every meal is meticulously portioned, and the chaotic winds of the Cloud Sea are kept safely outside the high walls of the capital."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/autocracy/shard_type_autocracy.tres
msgid ""
"This place was a challenge to get to. When I stepped through the gate onto the shard, the guards simply told me to go back. I had to wait until the middle of the night to sneak in unnoticed, and still almost got caught.\n"
"\n"
"Once I got some local clothes, I was able to blend in, but no shop would let me enter without a government permit, so I had to search around until I found a black market under a bridge. Folks there smuggle goods from off the shard and barter them for ration chits.\n"
"\n"
"Just as I was about to leave, a bunch of soldiers showed up, confiscated everything, and escorted me back to the gate. At least I didn't end up in one of their many jails."
msgstr ""

#: shard_types/cloth/shard_type_cloth.gd:21
#: shard_types/mq_wild_monastery/shard_type_mq_wild_monastery.gd:23
msgid "A <spot_upgrade:%s> <term_lower:spot_upgrade> in the <spot:%s>."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: shard_types/cloth/shard_type_cloth.tres
msgid "The Yarn Spinners"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: shard_name_override
#: shard_types/cloth/shard_type_cloth.tres
msgid "Tsumugi Shima"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: shard_name_override_meaning
#: shard_types/cloth/shard_type_cloth.tres
msgid "Pongee Island"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/cloth/shard_type_cloth.tres
msgid "When the first settlers arrived on $shard_name, many of them set up weaving workshops in towns across the shard. These workshops were tiny, single-family affairs, producing just enough to keep their neighbors clothed. Most used labor-intensive backstrap looms, and sewed the cloth into simple garments by themselves. Although hemp was the predominant material, some began to raise silkworms, but they barely produced enough silk to make handkerchiefs."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/cloth/shard_type_cloth.tres
msgid "Over time, the weaving industry of $shard_name expanded. Workshops grew to consist of several households sharing free-standing looms. As inter-town trade began to spread, many weaving households only spun the fiber into yarn and shipped it to large textile watermills on the river for weaving. The cloth would then continue on to tailors in other towns, and the garments would be distributed by traders to towns all over the shard."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/cloth/shard_type_cloth.tres
msgid "As the weaving industry developed further, quality control became crucial. Without a central government to enforce standards, quality was policed through a web of trust. Each tailor worked with the same textile mills for much of their career, each textile mill operator knew the dyers they trusted, and each dyer sourced yarn from the same households. Because the production pipeline was so interdependent, whenever a dyer's vats cracked or a watermill broke, the other artisans would immediately pool resources to repair them."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/cloth/shard_type_cloth.tres
msgid "Today, $shard_name is known across the Cloud Sea for its fine textiles and masterwork garments, featuring remarkably durable cloth and elaborate color layering. A robe from $shard_name is said to be made to outlast the wearer, and distant shards keep such robes as family heirlooms."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/cloth/shard_type_cloth.tres
msgid ""
"I did things differently on this trip. Instead of visiting specific sites, I followed the journey silk takes to become a robe. I started from a mulberry grove where the silkworms are raised. The grove is shared, but each family has their own silkworms.\n"
"\n"
"Next I saw how they spin the fiber into silk. The workshop I visited had just one loom, and the family worked it in shifts. They packed the yarn into baskets, and I joined the porter caravan that took it to a massive watermill on the river, which turned it into cloth. The chief there told me she'd worked with the same spinners for thirty years.\n"
"\n"
"The cloth was then dyed in the same town. Since it was a solid color fabric, they did the dyeing after the cloth was woven, rather than at the yarn stage. This went on to a tailor at the capital. I didn't stay long enough to see that particular cloth turn into a robe, but they had several examples of finished work, and it certainly deserves its reputation."
msgstr ""

#: shard_types/ecocentrism/shard_type_ecocentrism.gd:34
#: shard_types/religion/shard_type_religion.gd:29
msgid "<spot_upgrade:%s> (<spot:%s>)\n"
