msgstr ""

#: shard_types/ecocentrism/shard_type_ecocentrism.gd:38
msgid ""
"No mining or woodcutting <term_lower:spot_upgrade>s:[ul]\n"
"%s[/ul]"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: shard_types/ecocentrism/shard_type_ecocentrism.tres
msgid "The Overgrown Communes"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: shard_name_override
#: shard_types/ecocentrism/shard_type_ecocentrism.tres
msgid "Nezukami Mori"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: shard_name_override_meaning
#: shard_types/ecocentrism/shard_type_ecocentrism.tres
msgid "Root Grasping Forest"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/ecocentrism/shard_type_ecocentrism.tres
msgid "The people who settled in $shard_name took the lessons of the Shattering to heart. Perhaps too much so. Although the expedition that settled this shard left behind buildings of timber and stone, the permanent settlers refused to repeat the sins of the Old World by swinging an axe or lifting a pick. Most moved into mud huts, but the more radical ones live in burrows, natural caves, and hollows in large trees."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/ecocentrism/shard_type_ecocentrism.tres
msgid "As their architecture blended into the landscape, so too did their society. The idea of a central government brought to mind the rigid hierarchies of the Old World, so the population dissolved into hundreds of tiny, self-sufficient communes. Even today, traveling between these communes is notoriously difficult, as paving a road is considered a violent scarring of the earth. All but the most traveled paths now weave through deep woods and untouched undergrowth."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/ecocentrism/shard_type_ecocentrism.tres
msgid "The cuisine here has evolved into a strict practice of non-interference. The inhabitants survive almost entirely on a diet of fruits, refusing to harvest anything that harms the host plant. The only exceptions are made for medicinal herbs. When a root must be pulled to treat a fever, the commune gathers to perform a lengthy, deeply apologetic ritual to the earth. Consequently, the scholars of the shard do not study astronomy or engineering, but dedicate their lives to botany, nutrition, and the healing arts, ensuring that every borrowed leaf is put to its absolute best use."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/ecocentrism/shard_type_ecocentrism.tres
msgid "In these isolated communes, the local wildlife is granted absolute right of way. If a family of raccoon dogs decides to settle in a commune's firepit, the cooks have to dig up a new one, or simply forgo hot meals until the animals leave. When a fox wanders into a family burrow, the residents either pack up their belongings and leave, or learn to live alongside their new roommate. Many opt for the second option, with almost a third of households having an animal companion, as the beasts of this land have grown remarkably accustomed to humans."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/ecocentrism/shard_type_ecocentrism.tres
msgid ""
"I nearly twisted my ankle three times just trying to find the first settlement on this shard. There are no roads at all. The first locals I found explained that I'm supposed to follow mushroom patches, which they use as signposts of sorts.\n"
"\n"
"Everyone I met was living out in the wild. Apparently they gave up on building long ago, so instead of towns they have tiny communes scattered throughout the place. I had to sleep outside on moss mats most of the time. Way more comfortable than I expected, but I wouldn't want to spend the rest of my days living like that.\n"
"\n"
"It's certainly an odd way of life, but it seems to work for them, and to their credit, they can figure out how to settle anywhere, no matter the geography. Oh, and their berry wine is to die for."
msgstr ""

#: shard_types/food_heaven/shard_type_food_heaven.gd:21
msgid "%s higher than all other yields."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: shard_types/food_heaven/shard_type_food_heaven.tres
msgid "A Gourmand's Heaven"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: shard_name_override
#: shard_types/food_heaven/shard_type_food_heaven.tres
msgid "Hōjōhara"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: shard_name_override_meaning
#: shard_types/food_heaven/shard_type_food_heaven.tres
msgid "Abundance Plains"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/food_heaven/shard_type_food_heaven.tres
msgid "The first settlers of $shard_name were heroic, wise, and incredibly hungry. From early on, keeping bowls full was their top priority, and soon the primary challenge was not starvation, but storage. Before long, towns were judged primarily by the size of their strategic rice reserves, and any village that faced a failed harvest was immediately overflowing with supplies from their neighbors. The customary greeting here is not \"Hello\", but \"How was your meal?\"."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/food_heaven/shard_type_food_heaven.tres
msgid "As mere survival became a distant memory, the people of $shard_name turned their energy toward unnecessary perfection. In $town1, a debate over the proper thickness of a noodle caused a bitter schism, with every citizen aligning themselves with one of the two rival noodle shops. Meanwhile, the scholars of $town2 abandoned literature to focus on mapping out the migration path of the local mushroom colony. The highest status here is achieved by earning the highly sought-after title of Person of Taste."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/food_heaven/shard_type_food_heaven.tres
msgid "When tensions rose between the orchard keepers and the rice farmers over water rights, the dispute was settled in a battle of chefs, ensuring that no harsh words were spoken on an empty stomach. The resulting Great Banquet of $town3 lasted for four days, with the orchard keepers eking out a victory with a dish of persimmons simmered in pickled plum syrup."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/food_heaven/shard_type_food_heaven.tres
msgid "Today, visitors to $shard_name's capital are drawn to the grand shrine beside the city hall. Foreigners are surprised by the fact that it is dedicated not to the Enlightened One, but to the spirits of fermentation. The shrine's halls carry the earthy, salty tang of miso, and faint burbles can be heard from the huge cellars lying under its floors. People here move with a deliberate, satisfied slowness, having learned that rushing through life only leads to indigestion."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/food_heaven/shard_type_food_heaven.tres
msgid ""
"Now that's a shard I want to visit again! I barely stepped out of the gate before being offered a free bowl of \"welcome soup\", which tasted better than the most expensive meal I've had before. And by their standards, it's considered barely edible.\n"
"\n"
"Every day of the trip, my biggest challenge was to limit my portions so I could try as many dishes as possible. By the time I reached the capital I had to buy a new sash because mine was getting too tight.\n"
"\n"
"The highlight was definitely the fermentation shrine on the capital's main square. I've never seen so many kinds of pickles, and I didn't even have time to visit the cellars!\n"
"\n"
"I'm sure the scholars would consider these people simple, but they've certainly achieved perfection in their own way."
msgstr ""

#: shard_types/hunger/shard_type_hunger.gd:22
#: shard_types/militant/shard_type_militant.gd:39
msgid "No more than %d %s."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: shard_types/hunger/shard_type_hunger.tres
msgid "The Austere Rationers"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: shard_name_override
#: shard_types/hunger/shard_type_hunger.tres
msgid "Wabi no Kuni"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: shard_name_override_meaning
#: shard_types/hunger/shard_type_hunger.tres
msgid "Austerity Province"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/hunger/shard_type_hunger.tres
msgid "$shard_name has been a hungry place ever since its founding. Between the scarce natural resources and the lack of agriculture, starvation has been a constant companion for the people. In time, they have adapted to the hunger. They calibrated their scales to the weight of a single hair and began locking people up for spilling a bag of rice. Survival is a luxury here, for which the people compete daily."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/hunger/shard_type_hunger.tres
msgid "Crime is uncommon on $shard_name, partly because there's little to steal. The town guards spend much of their time patrolling the mess halls, watching for hands reaching for a second scoop or the telltale bulge of a turnip hoarded in a sleeve. A neighbor with a full belly is a neighbor stealing from the stockpile, and a round stomach is probable cause for an audit of one's household."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/hunger/shard_type_hunger.tres
msgid "Despite the austere conditions, $shard_name is not without celebrations. $town3 hosts a grand festival at the end of its fasting season, where every citizen receives a full bowl of rice and all the miso soup they can take. Still, some locals abstain even during these days, as they view hunger as a badge of civic loyalty."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/hunger/shard_type_hunger.tres
msgid "With time, new agricultural techniques and distribution policies have made starvation a thing of the past, yet the culture is not so quick to change. The granaries may be full now, but tables remain sparse. The memory of hunger persists in the back of people's minds, keeping them anxious about the next time a harvest might fail, or a storm might ruin a town's orchard."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/hunger/shard_type_hunger.tres
msgid ""
"This was an odd place. At first it seemed like just another shard, but after I spent a few days with the locals, I noticed some oddities. First off, even the rich officials seem to be wearing worn-out clothes. They have shops bursting at the seams with robes and sashes, yet they consider it a waste to throw out an outfit until it's full of holes.\n"
"\n"
"The dining customs are odd too. You can't order a dish at a restaurant until you've finished everything already on your plate. I met a traveler who brought some pears to eat on the way, and once he was done, he packed the cores into a little bag, saying that he'll be planting them in his garden, because it would be a waste to throw them away."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: shard_types/kind_justice/shard_type_kind_justice.tres
msgid "The Turning Society"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: shard_name_override
#: shard_types/kind_justice/shard_type_kind_justice.tres
msgid "Zenrintō"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: shard_name_override_meaning
#: shard_types/kind_justice/shard_type_kind_justice.tres
msgid "Good Neighbor Island"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/kind_justice/shard_type_kind_justice.tres
msgid "The legal system of $shard_name is a source of confusion for newcomers, who might ask where the prison is, only to be pointed toward the town square. When a person here breaks the law, they are not locked up or fined. Instead, the community forms a circle around the accused. One by one, they take turns ruthlessly listing every nice thing the accused has ever done. \"You fixed my roof,\" one might say, or \"you delivered medicine to my grandma\". This ritual, or \"Turning\" as the folks here call it, lasts until the criminal, mortified by their own decency, breaks down and begs to be allowed to just be good again."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/kind_justice/shard_type_kind_justice.tres
msgid "This unique approach to justice has transformed the role of the town guard in $town1. Since there are no arrests to make, their primary duty is surveillance of the benevolent kind. They stalk the streets with thick ledgers, lying in wait to catch someone helping a turtle cross the road or returning a dropped coin. This ensures that if a Turning is ever held, they have plenty of ammunition to use against the accused."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/kind_justice/shard_type_kind_justice.tres
msgid "$shard_name's approach to safety has led to a considerable lack of privacy among the townspeople. In $town2, locks are considered rude, and fences rarely reach above one's knee. The people reason that to maintain harmony, everyone should be fully aware of their neighbors' lives. Burglary is very rare, mostly because robbing a house where you've already been invited for tea is socially awkward."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/kind_justice/shard_type_kind_justice.tres
msgid "This culture of aggressive positivity has created a peculiar social pressure in the marketplace of $town3. Haggling has been turned on its head, transforming into reverse-bidding, where the seller insists the price is too high and the buyer insists it is too low. Disputes are resolved quickly, usually because both parties are worried that a prolonged argument might trigger a Turning, which everyone agrees is socially exhausting."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/kind_justice/shard_type_kind_justice.tres
msgid "Historians note that the people here view the Shattering not as the result of a sin, but of a temporary lapse in society's judgement. Just as they remind a criminal of their goodness to restore them, they tend to the land with gentle care, as if reminding the soil of all the gifts it has given them."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/kind_justice/shard_type_kind_justice.tres
msgid ""
"This was a lovely place to visit. Everyone was welcoming, and eager to introduce me to a dozen of their friends. The streets were clean, and the communal meals were filled with folks offering to let me stay at their houses. I gladly accepted one such invitation, since inns didn't seem to exist on the shard. Local travelers just show up in a town and assume someone will lend them a room.\n"
"\n"
"However, the people all seemed a bit... naive. When I asked how they collect taxes, they told me it's on an honor system. When a woman's coinpurse went missing at a bathhouse, she said she'd just look for it next time she visits. I suppose they get on just fine, but I sure hope no one comes to take advantage of them."
msgstr ""

#: shard_types/lazy/shard_type_lazy.gd:26
msgid "At most [color=#880000]%d %s[/color]."
msgstr ""

#: shard_types/lazy/shard_type_lazy.gd:27
#: shard_types/mountains/shard_type_mountains.gd:30
msgid "At least %d <term_lower:settlement>s."
msgstr ""

#: shard_types/lazy/shard_type_lazy.gd:28
#: shard_types/lightning/shard_type_lightning.gd:28
#: shard_types/militant/shard_type_militant.gd:41
msgid "At least %d <spot_upgrade:%s> <term_lower:spot_upgrade>s in <spot:%s>s."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: shard_types/lazy/shard_type_lazy.tres
msgid "The Leisure Province"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: shard_name_override
#: shard_types/lazy/shard_type_lazy.tres
msgid "Anseijima"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: shard_name_override_meaning
#: shard_types/lazy/shard_type_lazy.tres
msgid "Repose Island"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/lazy/shard_type_lazy.tres
msgid "The settlers of $shard_name were greeted by a land rich in wild fruit, rendering deliberate agriculture an afterthought. To avoid impacting the natural bounty, fields were eschewed in favor of letting wildflowers and weeds flourish. The native groves remained communal spaces, with every citizen expected to spend a few days each month tending them. Many look forward to this duty, as aside from making jam, it is one of the only culturally acceptable forms of labor."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/lazy/shard_type_lazy.tres
msgid "Celebrations on $shard_name often include only a brief ceremony, followed by a long feast, and a dance consisting for the most part of gentle swaying and slowly rolling on the ground. A communal nap usually follows, and the day culminates in a gathering around a lazily smoldering fire to tell stories and play improvisational games late into the night."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/lazy/shard_type_lazy.tres
msgid "The people of this shard have traditionally lived in small straw huts, but the citizens of $town1 have come up with a different solution. Given the temperate weather, they started to forego buildings and instead sleep in hammocks hung between tree trunks. Larger trees support multiple levels of hammocks, with rope ladders for easy access. People even bring their hammocks with them when traveling."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/lazy/shard_type_lazy.tres
msgid "On most shards, the Order of Growth consists of serene monks and nuns who watch grass grow as a form of meditation. Not so on $shard_name. Here, it consists of rebellious youths who consider growth a counter to the stagnation they see in their culture. They build large treehouses, connected by bridges into elaborate neighborhoods, and craft masterwork wooden implements and gears, which serve as the shard's main export goods."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/lazy/shard_type_lazy.tres
msgid ""
"I arrived on $shard_name just after a large group of tourists, and had to wait for hours while the arrival clerk leisurely recorded everyone's name, stopping for occasional snacks and a lengthy lunch break.\n"
"\n"
"My first destination was the famed treetop town of $town2, where nuns from the Order of Growth led me to an odd mechanized platform that brought us to the entrance hidden in the canopy. As we strolled through the settlement, crossing rope bridges and stopping at tea houses, I overheard many people referring to \"danglers\". When I asked my guide about this, she got uncomfortable and explained that it is an offensive term for people who still live in their hammocks.\n"
"\n"
"I then visited a settlement of these hammock people. It looked like little more than a forest with a few firepits during the day, but by evening every tree within view was full of hammocks, blocking every possible exit. Luckily, I was offered a free hammock and was able to spend the night there before returning to the gate."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: shard_name_override
#: shard_types/lightning/shard_type_lightning.tres
msgid "Raikō no Kuni"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: shard_name_override_meaning
#: shard_types/lightning/shard_type_lightning.tres
msgid "Lightning Province"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/lightning/shard_type_lightning.tres
msgid "From the early days, the ever-present storms of $shard_name have birthed a culture of storm chasing. The people in these storm chaser camps, with their gravity-defying hair and rubber sandals, were considered lunatics at first, until [b]Arashi Ryuutarou[/b] built a kite that guided lightning out of the skies to wherever one wished. This invention was quickly adopted as a tool to start fires and shatter rocks on command."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/lightning/shard_type_lightning.tres
msgid "With time, the people of $shard_name learned to store the lightning, like a venomous snake charmed into a basket. The vast cellars below the Stormwatch Citadel were filled with jars of gold leaf and brine, each charged with enough lightning to stop a heart. It is said that in these basements, one clumsy step can start a storm."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/lightning/shard_type_lightning.tres
msgid "One invention led to another, and it wasn't long before the capital's nighttime streets were bright with thousands of charcoal lamps that burned no fuel. These lightning-powered lanterns lengthened the days, but emitted a constant, high-pitched hiss and a low thrum, making silence a rare commodity."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/lightning/shard_type_lightning.tres
msgid "The latest invention taking $shard_name by storm is a technique for coating metals with the thinnest skin of gold or silver using the lightning jars. This has become so popular and widespread, that even farmers are often seen wielding gilded hoes, and no dinner in the capital is considered proper unless eaten with a pair of gilded chopsticks from a silvered bowl."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/lightning/shard_type_lightning.tres
msgid ""
"That was one bright shard! They had lanterns everywhere. You couldn't even see the stars at night, and I bet you can see the capital from three shards over.\n"
"\n"
"I heard that their lanterns barely need maintenance. They have invented some odd mechanism to keep them burning without fuel. They say it's the same thing as lightning, but they've now learned to somehow get it from waterfalls.\n"
"\n"
"I can't even try to figure all this stuff out, but their scholars say folks knew about it before the Shattering, and now they've started teaching the techniques to people on other shards."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/likely_shard_types_list.tscn
msgid "Potential Shard Cultures"
msgstr ""

#: shard_types/medicine/shard_type_medicine.gd:32
msgid "Acquire the %s <term_lower:relic> during the <spot:%s> <term_lower:event>: <event:%s>."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: var_name
#: shard_types/medicine/shard_type_medicine.tres
msgid "extract"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: shard_types/medicine/shard_type_medicine.tres
msgid "A Healer's Rest"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: shard_name_override
#: shard_types/medicine/shard_type_medicine.tres
msgid "Kiyomebara"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: shard_name_override_meaning
#: shard_types/medicine/shard_type_medicine.tres
msgid "Purification Plains"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/medicine/shard_type_medicine.tres
msgid "The discovery of the Butterbur Extract was a defining moment in $shard_name's history. Healers quickly realized that more effective medicine was within their grasp, and much of the scholarship done on the shard turned toward healthcare and life extension. Even chefs became experts in pharmacy, abandoning the pursuit of flavor for the pursuit of efficacy. Sweets are viewed with suspicion here, while a dish that tastes like wet tree bark is praised for its wholesomeness."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/medicine/shard_type_medicine.tres
msgid "A revolution in healing arrived with the discovery of disease spirits like the [b]Housougami[/b], invisible to the eye but deadly to the body. It was found that these spirits cling to unwashed surfaces, and need to be exorcised through regular baths and scrubs. Since these spirits jump from person to person, touching among strangers has become a taboo. A visitor who offers a handshake is shunned as an unwashed barbarian, and to hug an outsider is a quick way to become a pariah."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/medicine/shard_type_medicine.tres
msgid "Perhaps the most unusual feature of $shard_name is its art scene. Where other cultures paint landscapes or portraits, the masters of $shard_name produce lovingly detailed charcoal sketches of hearts, livers, and spleens. To visitors, the art gallery in the capital might look macabre and disturbing, but to a local, an accurate, lifelike sketch of an appendix is the highest form of beauty."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/medicine/shard_type_medicine.tres
msgid "$shard_name's ultimate claim to fame is the longevity of its inhabitants. It is common for people to grow old enough to meet their great-grandchildren. These elders are revered for their attention to their health, and are often elected as leaders of their communities. While this has kept decisions resistant to the whims of youth, it has also led to a culture obsessed with caution, avoiding any project that might involve even the tiniest sliver of risk."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/medicine/shard_type_medicine.tres
msgid ""
"This shard was a bit boring. Half of the residents are elders, and the few youngsters I met might as well have been elders themselves, with the way they shy away from the least bit of fun.\n"
"\n"
"I wanted to get a cup of sake at an inn, but they informed me that it's illegal because it harms your health. Rock climbing is also illegal, and so is sailing. What's the point of living so long if you don't even get to enjoy your life?"
msgstr ""

#: shard_types/militant/shard_type_militant.gd:40
msgid "At least %d settlements not <term_lower:connect_settlement>ed to the <term_lower:capital>."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: shard_types/militant/shard_type_militant.tres
msgid "The Fractured States"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: shard_name_override
#: shard_types/militant/shard_type_militant.tres
msgid "Kenko Iwa"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: shard_name_override_meaning
#: shard_types/militant/shard_type_militant.tres
msgid "Firm Rock"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/militant/shard_type_militant.tres
msgid "On $shard_name, the concept of a \"neighbor\" is synonymous with \"threat\". Soon after the shard was settled, trust eroded, and settlements withdrew into themselves, evolving into tiny, paranoid city-states. Travelers will find the spaces between them desolate, the roads deliberately destroyed to keep the others from getting close."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/militant/shard_type_militant.tres
msgid "A famous rope bridge once connected two neighboring towns. It hangs limp now, while both sides proudly claim they were the ones who cut the rope first to protect themselves from the barbarians across the gap. In the absence of bridges, they build watchposts, staring suspiciously at the horizon for invasions that never come."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/militant/shard_type_militant.tres
msgid "Daily life on $shard_name is a performance of readiness. Farmers in $town2 till the soil in breastplates, believing that an attack is always imminent. $town3 has gone even further, moving the fields inside the high city walls, which has resulted in the streets being cramped, claustrophobic, and smelling faintly of fertilizer and panic."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/militant/shard_type_militant.tres
msgid "The shard capital still stands, but it governs nothing. It has become a demilitarized zone, a neutral ground where no weapons are allowed. Its only industries are bureaucracy and spite. Each city has a cadre of representatives living in the capital, who regularly meet to exchange formal insults and hurl empty threats at each other."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/militant/shard_type_militant.tres
msgid ""
"I've never been to a shard that made me feel so unwelcome. All their towns have walls, and none would let me in. I had to camp out in the wilderness the entire time.\n"
"\n"
"At one point I met a courier on the road. He had two guards with him, and was apparently carrying a letter of grievance to a nearby village. The only other person I got to speak to was a hermit. Apparently he was a guard who got exiled after he let a woman from another town inside the walls when she was about to go into labor."
msgstr ""

#: shard_types/mountains/shard_type_mountains.gd:31
msgid "A <spot:%s> or <spot:%s> <term_lower:spot> in each <term_lower:settlement>."
msgstr ""

#: shard_types/mountains/shard_type_mountains.gd:32
#: shard_types/mountains/shard_type_mountains.gd:33
#: shard_types/mountains/shard_type_mountains.gd:34
#: shard_types/newspaper/shard_type_newspaper.gd:43
msgid "A <spot_upgrade:%s> <term_lower:spot_upgrade>."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: shard_types/mountains/shard_type_mountains.tres
msgid "The Dwellers of the Walls"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: shard_name_override
#: shard_types/mountains/shard_type_mountains.tres
msgid "Gakujima"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: shard_name_override_meaning
#: shard_types/mountains/shard_type_mountains.tres
msgid "Peak Island"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mountains/shard_type_mountains.tres
msgid "When the first settlers arrived on $shard_name, they founded towns in the high mountains and deep valleys. In $canyon_town, homes are cut directly into the rock, turning the entire cliff face into a single, massive building interconnected by hundreds of carved stairways. The locals point out that a solid stone roof has the benefit of never blowing away in the wind. In $ice_town, the locals take advantage of the icy weather around their high peak to preserve food. They mash hardy tubers and spread them on the cold rocks, then once frozen, store them in bags buried under ice. They claim that this method keeps the food perfectly edible for years."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mountains/shard_type_mountains.tres
msgid "As the settlements expanded, the verticality of $shard_name required new ways to stay connected. The people here hold bridge-weaving festivals, where villages on neighboring peaks gather to twist thick hemp and vines into dizzying walkways over the abyss. These festivals are typically started by blowing massive horns, which echo against the mountains and announce the event to all the villages in the area. Agriculture, too, had to adapt. It is now split into two distinct disciplines: valley farmers tending delicate greens and high-altitude specialists coaxing hardy roots from the frozen soil."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mountains/shard_type_mountains.tres
msgid "In $volcano_town, instead of building cooking fires, the locals cook much of their food in springs and on the hot rocks by the lava vents. Yet, the mountain's harshness is never entirely forgotten. By local custom, anyone seeking a seat on the town council must first climb the highest nearby peak wearing nothing but a thin robe. This traditional test of endurance ensures their leaders maintain humility, and intimately experience the harsh realities of the land they govern."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mountains/shard_type_mountains.tres
msgid "Today, the architects of $shard_name have perfected the art of living among the cliffs. Having learned from past tremors, $canyon_town is now designed to be completely resistant to earthquakes. Builders carefully avoid existing fault lines, leave exceptionally thick ceilings between levels, and carve deep into the bedrock rather than hugging the fragile cliff face. There and in other $shard_name towns, deep valleys used to be plunged into shadow by early afternoon, but now artisans have mounted massive polished bronze sheets along the upper ridges. These mirrors bounce the sun directly into the valleys below, ensuring many more hours of daylight for the residents."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mountains/shard_type_mountains.tres
msgid ""
"The locals met me at the gate with their traditional welcoming gesture: a wad of bitter, gray lichen. They explained that I should chew it to stave off the dizzying effects of the thin air. This remedy worked wonderfully, though I found my teeth stained blue afterwards. I've been told that the color will wear off in a few days.\n"
"\n"
"The archives mentioned the polished bronze mirrors used to light the deep valleys, but reading about them does not prepare you for the reality of standing in these lit valleys. The reflected sunlight doesn't just light the area up, but crisscrosses the air in glowing shafts. It's like standing in a dark room with dozens of pinholes.\n"
"\n"
"The valley farmers use this reflected light to grow delicate greens, which I tasted. I boiled them directly in a hot spring after the local custom, while watching wild goats walk directly on a sheer cliff face. I'm disappointed to report that the greens mostly tasted of sulfur, but the locals seem to like it."
msgstr ""

#: shard_types/mq_accord_monument/shard_type_mq_accord_monument.gd:15
msgid "A fully established <shop:accord_monument>."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: shard_name_override
#: shard_types/mq_accord_monument/shard_type_mq_accord_monument.tres
msgid "Chikai no Iwa"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: shard_name_override_meaning
#: shard_types/mq_accord_monument/shard_type_mq_accord_monument.tres
msgid "Oath Rock"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_accord_monument/shard_type_mq_accord_monument.tres
msgid "Soon after the Accord Monument was established, it became the favorite destination for pilgrims from across the Cloud Sea. People would come to pay their respects to the divine wisdom inscribed here, and consult the priests on ways to connect with the Enlightened One on their own shards. Many visitors brought fragments of divinity with them: sacred plants revered in their culture for their curative effects, or animals that seemed to act with more insight than common beasts."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_accord_monument/shard_type_mq_accord_monument.tres
msgid "During the long centuries, as people lost faith in the promised salvation, the number of pilgrims dwindled, and so did the number of priests. Still, those that did remain took their duties seriously. They followed the vows set down in the Accord, caring for the surrounding environment and preserving ancient wisdom. The temple garden is still filled with the sacred plants collected through the ages, and the archives bear records of all the petitions the pilgrims have brought before the priests in centuries past."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_accord_monument/shard_type_mq_accord_monument.tres
msgid "As the new age dawned, the role of the Accord Monument has evolved. The priests now serve as scholars of environmentalism. Farmers from other shards come here to learn how to best care for their crops, and engineers hold conferences on techniques to prevent soil erosion. The archives have since been analyzed through a different lens, mapping out a history of challenges that various shards have faced in the past. The sacred plants planted and replanted over centuries have also proven useful, as the monument now has a great underground vault filled with seeds from all across the Cloud Sea, in case any of them go extinct in the future."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_accord_monument/shard_type_mq_accord_monument.tres
msgid ""
"This wasn't my first trip to the Accord Monument. I went with my parents once when I was a child. The place is much like how I remembered it, full of scholars and off-shard researchers.\n"
"\n"
"During my visit they were hosting an exhibit on soil types. The old reliquary hall was filled with various core samples and plates of dirt and clay of various colors. There was even an interactive station where you could pour water into glass tubs full of different soils and see how quickly it would start dripping from the holes in the base. None of it was particularly useful to me, but I couldn't help but be inspired by how much work and study has gone into making the food we eat every day."
msgstr ""

#: shard_types/mq_avatar_home/shard_type_mq_avatar_home.gd:15
msgid ""
"All of the following <term_lower:spot_upgrade>s [b]in the same settlement[/b]:[ul]<spot_upgrade:%s>\n"
"<spot_upgrade:%s>\n"
"Any Monastery[/ul]"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: shard_name_override
#: shard_types/mq_avatar_home/shard_type_mq_avatar_home.tres
msgid "Shizugokoro Shima"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: shard_name_override_meaning
#: shard_types/mq_avatar_home/shard_type_mq_avatar_home.tres
msgid "Calm Heart Island"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_avatar_home/shard_type_mq_avatar_home.tres
msgid "$shard_name was settled with the sole aim of becoming the Avatar's secret home, and $avatar_town was founded with everything a young deity might need. When the Scribe arrived with the Avatar in tow, the monks were prepared with solemn rituals and ancient scripture. The child, however, was more interested in the frogs by the meditation ponds. The Scribe did not attempt to force the heavy burden of history onto small shoulders. If the Avatar spent the afternoon watching ripples in the water, the Scribe reasoned, then surely there was deep wisdom to be found in those ripples. The most respected theologians of the age dedicated their days to maintaining the quiet sanctuary, finding reverence in watching a child play."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_avatar_home/shard_type_mq_avatar_home.tres
msgid "As the years passed, the Scribe's endless hours of instruction began to take root. The Avatar came to understand the fading daydreams of youth as fragmented memories of an ancient past. Now, looking at a map of the scattered shards or tracing the glyphs of a sacred text brought on a strange, undeniable sense of familiarity. When the time finally came to leave this quiet sanctuary and return to the Haven, the Avatar bid farewell to the monks and made sure to check the meditation ponds for frogs one last time before stepping into the gate and solemnly accepting the heavy burden of reuniting the world."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_avatar_home/shard_type_mq_avatar_home.tres
msgid "After the monks and nuns that raised the Avatar heard of their decision to retire from the world, they took it as a teaching of non-intervention. They still live in the monastery, but they have let nature reclaim all but the living quarters. Today, the grounds are overgrown with waist-high shrubs, and the meditation pond the Avatar had loved so much has become a tiny swamp."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_avatar_home/shard_type_mq_avatar_home.tres
msgid ""
"This trip was a little underwhelming. I know the Avatar was no god, but they did ignite the spark that led to this new age of enlightenment, so I was surprised that the place isn't as well maintained as our other monuments.\n"
"\n"
"The temple complex looks abandoned, and isn't open to visitors. The surrounding town is heavily catering to tourists, and full of folks trying to make money by showing visitors a tree that the Avatar allegedly climbed as a child, or a tea house which the Avatar used to frequent, no doubt run by their friends."
msgstr ""

#: shard_types/mq_forest_shrine/shard_type_mq_forest_shrine.gd:38
#: shard_types/mq_foxes/shard_type_mq_foxes.gd:17
#: shard_types/mq_lake_shrine/shard_type_mq_lake_shrine.gd:17
msgid "Finish the <event:%s>."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: shard_types/mq_forest_shrine/shard_type_mq_forest_shrine.tres
msgid "The Site of the First Shrine"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: shard_name_override
#: shard_types/mq_forest_shrine/shard_type_mq_forest_shrine.tres
msgid "Motojima"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: shard_name_override_meaning
#: shard_types/mq_forest_shrine/shard_type_mq_forest_shrine.tres
msgid "Origin Island"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_forest_shrine/shard_type_mq_forest_shrine.tres
msgid "$shard_name was the site of the first official shrine to the Enlightened One in the New World. Despite an initial incident after the shrine's dedication, the monks have maintained it in perfect condition since."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_forest_shrine/shard_type_mq_forest_shrine.tres
msgid "After animals were blamed for the defilement of the First Shrine, measures were taken to prevent further harm to the holy site. Moats were dug around shrines, surrounded by spiked fences to keep wildlife away, and ritual hunts are still carried out monthly to thin the local animal population."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_forest_shrine/shard_type_mq_forest_shrine.tres
msgid "The early decision to restrict access to the First Shrine to monks has had several consequences since. As the shrine expanded with pavilions, gardens, and prayer halls, more and more of the sacred woods became inaccessible to the common people. Before long, the whole of $shrine_town became a restricted sacred area."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_forest_shrine/shard_type_mq_forest_shrine.tres
msgid "What began as a brief investigation into broken fences slowly evolved into a permanent system. The monks declared that it is a holy duty of every citizen to report suspicious behavior, and hiding others' misdeeds was just as valid a reason for penance as the misdeeds themselves."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_forest_shrine/shard_type_mq_forest_shrine.tres
msgid "Soon after its founding, the First Shrine became the beating heart of $shrine_town. The repaired fences were eventually torn down, to be replaced with food stalls, which became a favored haunt for local children during the long summer evenings. Fueled by the sweet treats sold at these stalls, the children spent many an hour playing hide and seek among the tall trees surrounding the shrine."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_forest_shrine/shard_type_mq_forest_shrine.tres
msgid "As the years passed, priests found more and more fault in animals. They declared them to be evil spirits, abominations against the Enlightened One's natural order, and posted bounties, offering rewards for tails and pelts. This backfired when several residents of $nonshrine_town were found to be secretly raising lizards to harvest their tails."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_forest_shrine/shard_type_mq_forest_shrine.tres
msgid "As shrines were built in other towns on the shard, they also enforced the ban on laypeople. However, an exception was made for those who wanted to present offerings to the Enlightened One. To make sure no commoner could pretend to be a monk, laws were instituted allowing only monks to wear orange clothing. For unclear reasons, this was later expanded to include all dyed clothing, and eventually required laypeople to dress only in clothing made of rough, undyed hemp."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_forest_shrine/shard_type_mq_forest_shrine.tres
msgid "To handle the growing number of accusations, the priests established a specialized branch of the order. These magistrates patrol the streets with absolute authority to detain and question anyone, bypassing judges and town councils. They also keep detailed records of all inhabitants, and failure to register is considered a punishable offense."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_forest_shrine/shard_type_mq_forest_shrine.tres
msgid "With the shrine's many child patrons, the monks decided to start hosting regular sessions to teach the holy glyphs, and the place soon turned into a school that also taught writing, arithmetic, and natural philosophy. Smaller shrines in other towns followed suit, and thanks to these schools, $shard_name has become one of the most literate places in the Cloud Sea."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_forest_shrine/shard_type_mq_forest_shrine.tres
msgid "As the population of predators dwindled due to the monthly hunts, rodents became more and more common, and learned to better hide in the walls and crevices of the towns' buildings. Today, it isn't unusual for half the harvest to end up being destroyed by vermin, leading to a cycle of blame that continues to inflame the hatred of animals prevalent among the locals."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_forest_shrine/shard_type_mq_forest_shrine.tres
msgid "After $nonshrine_town rebelled against the monks' strict rules, the head monk of the First Shrine led a force to pacify the rebels, and the ensuing commotion resulted in more restrictive laws. Today, laypeople are forbidden from learning the Enlightened One's holy glyphs, leaving much of the populace illiterate, and unable to argue with the priesthood."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_forest_shrine/shard_type_mq_forest_shrine.tres
msgid "Today, $shard_name is an orderly place, with magistrates enforcing strict laws and holding court in austere, windowless tribunal halls. Criminals are punished by imprisonment, and their families are fined heavily. The shard's main event is the Atonement Festival, where citizens gather to confess minor sins in the hope that offering up small transgressions will shield them from more serious accusations."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_forest_shrine/shard_type_mq_forest_shrine.tres
msgid "The monks on $shard_name are afforded great respect among the locals. In addition to the shrine maintenance and teaching duties they've performed over generations, they now often serve as mediators whenever conflicts arise. The shrine in $nonshrine_town even serves as a bank, letting people entrust their excess funds to the monks, and take interest-free loans for large projects."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_forest_shrine/shard_type_mq_forest_shrine.tres
#: shard_types/mq_lake_shrine/shard_type_mq_lake_shrine.tres
msgid "(overridden)"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_forest_shrine/shard_type_mq_forest_shrine.tres
msgid "As far as I could tell, this shard is no longer inhabited. Perhaps there are people living somewhere, but the town by the gate is nothing but ruins. I traveled along the old paved road for two days before turning back, and saw no people or any signs of recent use. I suppose it would be a miracle if every settled shard survived."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_forest_shrine/shard_type_mq_forest_shrine.tres
msgid ""
"This was a harrowing trip. The guards at the gate refused to let me keep my clothes, and insisted that I purchase a rough hemp tunic sanctioned by the priesthood.\n"
"\n"
"When I tried to pay for my stay at the inn, I found that the only accepted form of payment were tokens issued by the local shrine. The priest at the shrine demanded that I make a large offering before he would even speak to me, but he did eventually give me a few tokens in return for all the money I brought, just enough to spend two nights at the inn.\n"
"\n"
"I decided to leave the next day. As I was sitting on a bench writing this report, I was mobbed by a patrol of priests accusing me of blasphemy for using holy glyphs, and escorted back to the gate."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_forest_shrine/shard_type_mq_forest_shrine.tres
msgid ""
"When I first stepped out of the gate, I was escorted to a locked room and spent an hour answering questions for the magistrates. I had to list every shard I had visited in the past ten years, every place I intended to visit while on this shard, and the purpose of every item I brought with me. Eventually they let me go, but that left a bad first impression.\n"
"\n"
"The first thing I noticed was that homes had no ground floor windows. I suppose people really value their privacy here. That was supported by how they speak. No one I talked to would share any details of their life, dodging questions and offering platitudes instead. The only genuine interaction I was able to observe was two kids playing by the river. They took turns dunking each other's head in the water while demanding they confess their crimes, then bursting out laughing.\n"
"\n"
"As I was heading back to the gate, I could swear someone was following me, ducking into alleyways whenever I turned around. But perhaps I was just affected by everyone's paranoia."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_forest_shrine/shard_type_mq_forest_shrine.tres
msgid ""
"When I arrived on the shard, I was immediately welcomed and given a flyer showing the town's map, with local inns and eateries annotated in beautiful calligraphy. Gen, the local head priest, came personally to walk me to the inn. As we were strolling along, people would often come up and ask Gen about various upcoming shrine events. Apparently the shrine also acts as a school, a marketplace, and a party venue.\n"
"\n"
"The next morning I went to visit this unusual shrine, and found a bustling market instead. After a breakfast of sweet buns I made my way to the actual shrine building, barely visible behind the stalls. The little hut was empty, and when I asked around, I learned that Gen and his team were out in the fields helping with the harvest. I left a few coins in the donation box before leaving, and noticed that it was already almost full. It didn't even have a lid. On some shards those donations would've disappeared within a day."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: shard_types/mq_foxes/shard_type_mq_foxes.tres
msgid "The Fox Spirits Domain"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: shard_name_override
#: shard_types/mq_foxes/shard_type_mq_foxes.tres
msgid "Kitsunebi Mori"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: shard_name_override_meaning
#: shard_types/mq_foxes/shard_type_mq_foxes.tres
msgid "Fox Fire Woods"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_foxes/shard_type_mq_foxes.tres
msgid "$shard_name was the first place where the settlers of the New World encountered hauntings, a group of fox spirits. They led the expedition deep into the woods, and when they finally found their way out, the foxes were gathering around an old tree on the edge of the forest. The Scribe and his acolytes shed blood from their own palms to banish the haunting once and for all."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_foxes/shard_type_mq_foxes.tres
msgid "$shard_name was the first place where the settlers of the New World encountered hauntings, a group of fox spirits. They led the expedition deep into the woods, and when they finally found their way out, the foxes were gathering around an old tree on the edge of the forest. The Scribe and his acolytes poured salt in arcane circles to banish the haunting once and for all."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_foxes/shard_type_mq_foxes.tres
msgid "$shard_name was the first place where the settlers of the New World encountered hauntings, a group of fox spirits. They led the expedition deep into the woods, and when they finally found their way out, the foxes were gathering around an old tree on the edge of the forest. Despite the Scribe's objections, the settlers decided to let the foxes be, and merely recorded their findings."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_foxes/shard_type_mq_foxes.tres
msgid "$shard_name was the first place where the settlers of the New World encountered hauntings, a group of fox spirits. They led the expedition deep into the woods, and when they finally found their way out, the foxes were gathering around an old tree on the edge of the forest. The Scribe and his acolytes cut down the tree to banish the haunting once and for all."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_foxes/shard_type_mq_foxes.tres
msgid "The culture of $shard_name took an odd turn after the settlers' first encounter with the foxes. The effectiveness of bloodletting in warding off spirits led to the practice spreading like wildfire through the everyday rituals of the locals. A personal misfortune is often treated by spilling a few drops of blood, while a drought calls for a communal self-flagellation ritual."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_foxes/shard_type_mq_foxes.tres
msgid "The custom of throwing salt to ward off fox spirits soon evolved into the philosophy that if a little salt keeps hauntings at bay, then a lot of it must make a place holy. The practice of salting the earth before breaking ground for a new home or planting a new field became widely accepted. The soil around settlements is now entirely grey, and the locals congratulate themselves on successfully starving out the invisible menace."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_foxes/shard_type_mq_foxes.tres
msgid "The culture of careful observation on $shard_name took root early. After the encounter with fox spirits, the priests established a routine of regularly returning to study the tree and its surroundings. They soon discovered that an unusually fragrant species of moss grows in the area, and appears to be tempting to the local wildlife. This simple discovery was enough to dispel the belief that the foxes are evil spirits, and they have since been treated as regular animals."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_foxes/shard_type_mq_foxes.tres
msgid "The culture of $shard_name took an odd turn after the settlers' first encounter with the foxes. The effectiveness of fire in warding off spirits led to burning being a common solution for other issues. For example, when irritating pollen drifts from the forests in the spring, it is customary to simply burn down all trees within settlement range, though some locals complain that the smoke and ashes are just as irritating."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_foxes/shard_type_mq_foxes.tres
msgid "The healthcare practices of $shard_name are centered entirely around blood-letting, as well as ways to recover from it. The healers have found that certain crops help people maintain the quality of their blood, so it is common to see more fields of spinach and beetroot than buckwheat nowadays. That said, many look with suspicion upon people with particularly healthy complexion, and extreme pallor is in fashion as a sign of spiritual diligence."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_foxes/shard_type_mq_foxes.tres
msgid "$shard_name eventually ran out of readily available salt, even with off-shard imports, and the priesthood began blessing the land with lye and copper shavings. They believe that a pure forest is one with no moss or undergrowth, so the trees can properly channel the energies of the soil. As a result, the woods look like a vast, neatly swept graveyard."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_foxes/shard_type_mq_foxes.tres
msgid "Many scholars on $shard_name specialize in the observation of animal behavior. This has led to numerous discoveries, such as medicinal herbs that the local macaques chew when they are ill. Natural disasters like wildfires are now predicted early by observing animals fleeing out of the forests, and the purity of wells is now judged by the fish and toads living in them."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_foxes/shard_type_mq_foxes.tres
msgid "Over time, the settlements of $shard_name have lost much of their vegetation. In fact, most towns are surrounded by wide open plains where forests once stood. These plains are furthermore remarkably empty, as the animals have retreated further and further into the wilderness, fleeing the devastating burnings. The locals are content with this state of affairs, feeling safe from the incursions of wild hauntings."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_foxes/shard_type_mq_foxes.tres
msgid "As ritual cuts became a universal expectation, the vocation of \"proxy bleeders\" has arisen. The wealthy and the elderly now hire people to perform the rituals in their place, which is particularly valuable for merchants, as all contracts have to be signed in blood to be considered binding."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_foxes/shard_type_mq_foxes.tres
msgid "Today, a visitor to $shard_name will find a stark, bleached landscape that smells of iron and ash. The towns are immaculate, and the citizens take immense pride in their purified, pale soil. They spend hours scrubbing their garden stones with toxic pastes to ensure no rogue spirit dares to sprout a patch of lichen. The crop yields on $shard_name are now at a historical minimum, which the locals blame on the lack of spiritual diligence in these wayward times."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_foxes/shard_type_mq_foxes.tres
msgid "$shard_name has become famous for its animal encyclopedias, thick volumes filled with detailed descriptions of animal anatomy and behavior. When the scholars ran out of local animals to study, they started traveling to other shards to catalogue the fauna all across the Cloud Sea, spreading the shard's reputation far and wide."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_foxes/shard_type_mq_foxes.tres
msgid "Today, the culture of $shard_name has changed. The indiscriminate burnings have given way to small, orderly rituals where incense and prayer slips are burned in little braziers. The main exception to this is the Summer Bonfire festival, where large bonfires in the shape of glyphs are burned on the slopes of specific mountains to guide the fragmented spirits to join the Enlightened One's divine being."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_foxes/shard_type_mq_foxes.tres
msgid "(overriden)"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_foxes/shard_type_mq_foxes.tres
msgid ""
"This was a pretty eerie place. I'd heard that this shard is full of \"blood cultists\", but I'd always dismissed it as exaggerated rumors. Well, imagine my surprise when the customs officials asked me to stamp my name on the visitors list, and handed me a needle. I had to draw a drop of blood instead of using my personal seal.\n"
"\n"
"I spent the whole trip in the small town by the gate. The main plaza is lined with fox statues draped in red aprons. It seems that at some point they stopped considering foxes to be evil spirits, and they now call them the shard's protectors.\n"
"\n"
"Despite the size of the town, I found a branch of the Bleeders Guild there. The bleeders are extra healthy folks who are hired when the wealthy need to sign a lot of documents or make a particularly large donation to the church. Apparently they are well paid and happy with their jobs, so who am I to judge?"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_foxes/shard_type_mq_foxes.tres
msgid ""
"I wasn't able to do much exploration on this shard. When I tried to check into an inn, I discovered that the only currency accepted here is salt, and having not brought any with me, I couldn't pay.\n"
"\n"
"A local miller took pity on me and let me stay in a spare room, but before she even let me into the house I had to go through a purification ritual, bathing in ice water and being dusted with some sort of metallic shavings.\n"
"\n"
"Having no means of paying for food or passage to other towns, I had to return the next day."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_foxes/shard_type_mq_foxes.tres
msgid ""
"The people of this shard are remarkably practical. My inn was next to a school, so I went there and was disturbed to find the teacher showing the children a cut-up frog. The teacher later told me that the frog had died of old age, and dissecting it is the best way to understand how bodies work.\n"
"\n"
"After the anatomy lessons, a local ranger took the kids out into the wilderness to sketch local plants and wildlife. He explained that no predators live in the woods this close to town because the rangers plant a certain type of bush whose scents deter these animals.\n"
"\n"
"They apply even more advanced techniques in their farming. They breed a specific kind of beetle and release them into the fields, because these particular bugs eat weeds and leave crops alone, while also chasing off other insects.\n"
"\n"
"I can't argue with the effectiveness of their approach, but I personally prefer to keep a little more mystery in the world."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_foxes/shard_type_mq_foxes.tres
msgid ""
"I arrived just before the shard's famous Summer Bonfire festival. I joined the townsfolk who were in charge of setting up one of the fires as they climbed up the mountain. They were clearly experts, cutting up grass, layering sticks in the shape of the spirit glyph, and surrounding it with gravel to prevent a wildfire.\n"
"\n"
"Two days later, back in town, everyone gathered in the evening to watch the burning glyphs. I have to admit it was an impressive sight, the bright yellow flames blazing in such a clear shape against the dark mountainside. Later the same night, the kids burned a large fox effigy in the town square. They assured me that no one bears foxes any grudges, and this is simply a fun ancient tradition they keep to this day."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: shard_types/mq_lake_shrine/shard_type_mq_lake_shrine.tres
msgid "The Holy Lakes"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: shard_name_override
#: shard_types/mq_lake_shrine/shard_type_mq_lake_shrine.tres
msgid "Mizukagami no Hama"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: shard_name_override_meaning
#: shard_types/mq_lake_shrine/shard_type_mq_lake_shrine.tres
msgid "Water Mirror Beach"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_lake_shrine/shard_type_mq_lake_shrine.tres
msgid "$shard_name is known for its many beautiful lakes, one of which has a tiny island with a grand shrine to the Enlightened One. As one of the first shrines to be erected in the New World, it has become a popular pilgrimage destination. People come from all across the shard to meditate on its shores, finding wisdom in the reflection on its tranquil water."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_lake_shrine/shard_type_mq_lake_shrine.tres
msgid "In the early days after the shrine was established and the incense burning ritual was adopted, the lake gained fame for a permanent, sweet-smelling haze that settled over the still waters. Pilgrims would visit for the chance to inhale the holy spirit and carry away a piece of divinity in their bodies. The burnt incense was also collected and spread over the little island, with the priests drawing intricate mandalas in the ashes."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_lake_shrine/shard_type_mq_lake_shrine.tres
msgid "The communal pot of the Lake Shrine has become famous throughout the shard. Although originally used to boil plain grains for the folks living around the lake, it has evolved into a perpetual stew with seasonal flavorings added daily. The pot itself was later replaced by a giant cauldron taking up much of the shrine's courtyard, and pilgrims visit regularly to taste the famous dish prepared in it."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_lake_shrine/shard_type_mq_lake_shrine.tres
msgid "When the Lake Shrine was first founded, it adopted an unusual ritual of gathering fallen leaves to carpet the grounds. Over the years, the priests noticed that vegetation thrived wherever the leaves were deposited, and when the farmers heard of this, they quickly adopted the practice of spreading compost on their fields, greatly enhancing the shard's fertility."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_lake_shrine/shard_type_mq_lake_shrine.tres
msgid "When the Lake Shrine was first founded, the priests adopted a ritual of meditating on the spot where the torii gate meets the water. Eventually, a pragmatic priest realized that it would be useful to record the change in water level, and erected a large wooden pillar in the lake on the other side of the shrine, marked with notches. Every day, the priests would use this giant ruler to measure and record the water level."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_lake_shrine/shard_type_mq_lake_shrine.tres
msgid "Over the years, the falling ash and the occasional discarded incense stick have changed the lake. The clear waters have turned murky, and fish have become rare. Many of the priests also developed a chronic cough, which they suffered with pride as a sign of sharing the heavy burden of the Enlightened One."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_lake_shrine/shard_type_mq_lake_shrine.tres
msgid "With time, shrines across the shard have adopted the communal cauldron tradition, and many households no longer have their own kitchens, opting instead to have all their meals at the nearest shrine. Travelers say that each village on $shard_name has its own unique flavor, and locals take visitors from off the shard on culinary tours to taste the flavors of each village."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_lake_shrine/shard_type_mq_lake_shrine.tres
msgid "Classic painting never took hold on $shard_name, but a unique local artform developed instead. In the autumn, when the leaves turn gold and red, artists gather them in large amounts and arrange them into grand compositions, each leaf a paint daub. Although such artwork rarely lasts more than a few days, it is seen as a celebration of transience, rather than waste, and people treasure the opportunity to see a great artist's work, knowing that it is their only chance to witness its unique beauty."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_lake_shrine/shard_type_mq_lake_shrine.tres
msgid "Over the years, the priests of $shard_name invented other measurement techniques based on their water level measuring tradition. Early on they learned to measure volume by submerging things in water. Later, they constructed a device for measuring temperature, by forcing air through a tube into a water measuring vessel, and noting how the water level rises when the air is heated."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_lake_shrine/shard_type_mq_lake_shrine.tres
msgid "Now, centuries later, the practice of burning incense is long out of favor, replaced with aromatic herbs planted in the shrine gardens. The lake was dredged repeatedly, and has since regained much of its clarity. The priesthood now dismisses the early practices as minor mistakes on the road to enlightenment."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_lake_shrine/shard_type_mq_lake_shrine.tres
msgid "The custom of shared meals has forged a strong sense of community in the towns of $shard_name, as rich and poor alike sit on the same mats and eat from the same pot. This sense of communal pride is also bolstered by the fact that the original cauldron at the Lake Shrine has remained in continuous use for centuries now."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_lake_shrine/shard_type_mq_lake_shrine.tres
msgid "The philosophers of $shard_name have developed a worldview that celebrates the impermanent, cyclical nature of life. Just as leaves paint the landscape for a brief moment before withering and feeding next year's growth, so do people live to spread kindness across their communities before departing, having made life better for the next generation. Even the Shattering, they argue, was merely the end of one such cycle, necessary to give rise to the civilization of the Cloud Sea."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_lake_shrine/shard_type_mq_lake_shrine.tres
msgid "The scientifically-minded priesthood of $shard_name eventually sought to understand the history of the lake. They drove hollow bamboo tubes deep into the lakebed, extracting columns of mud. By studying the layers of sediment they were able to infer the history of the surrounding environment. On the layers corresponding to the time of the Shattering, they found a massive concentration of metals and ash, a sure sign that the land had suffered horrific burns before the world broke apart."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_lake_shrine/shard_type_mq_lake_shrine.tres
msgid ""
"The famous lake on this shard is really beautiful. The waters are crystal clear, and the island with the shrine looks like a little jungle in the middle. When I asked the priest how they got it to look like that, she said it was a divine blessing. Another visitor asked if it had always been this way, to which she conceded that it took a lot of hard work from the priests over the generations.\n"
"\n"
"I asked around and learned that generations back the lake was full of black sludge from incense ash, but it's a sore topic for the priesthood. They've since overcorrected, and don't allow fire on the island at all. They even follow a strict raw diet because cooking would involve burning.\n"
"\n"
"A shifty fellow offered to sell me a stick of ancient incense, which is illegal in the town, but I decided not to get in trouble for such a mundane item."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_lake_shrine/shard_type_mq_lake_shrine.tres
msgid ""
"The dining culture of this shard is its most distinctive feature. It seems that the people of each town have all of their meals together at a single eatery. The lines can be a bit long, but all the food is free, and I can say from personal experience that it does taste very good.\n"
"\n"
"The eateries are maintained by the local priesthood, and the townsfolk regularly contribute ingredients into the common pot. Multiple people asked if I had brought any ingredients from off the shard, and seemed disappointed when I said that I hadn't known of the custom before arriving here.\n"
"\n"
"I visited one more town before coming back. On my way there I camped by the road for the night and had some of my travel rations. As I was doing so, an old woman passing by came over to chastise me. Apparently it's bad manners here to eat alone. The next day I got to have a meal at my destination, which was served the same way, but tasted quite different, heavy with ginger and mustard seed."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_lake_shrine/shard_type_mq_lake_shrine.tres
msgid ""
"I arrived on this shard at a fortunate time. The leaves had just started to turn red, and the local artists were at the peak of their activity. Each morning from the window of my inn I saw groups of people arranging leaves into great murals, which were swept into the lake by evening. My favorite was one that showed a lone deer standing on a cliff looking at a sunset.\n"
"\n"
"I didn't have time to visit the famous lake shrine, but I did go to the smaller shrine in the town where I was staying. The priest there was particularly proud of their graveyard, which looked like an oddly orderly forest. They don't burn their dead here, and they don't put memorial stones either. Instead, they bury the bodies under a sapling, and the deceased's family cares for the tree. They see it as maintaining the cycle of nature."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_lake_shrine/shard_type_mq_lake_shrine.tres
msgid ""
"I visited the famous lake shrine on this shard. The place is beautiful, the whole island being surrounded by blooming lotus flowers. A priest explained to me that these flowers are not just a decoration, but serve a practical purpose, as they change color whenever the lake's water is contaminated.\n"
"\n"
"The shrine's archive is filled with scrolls. At first I thought they were holy sutras and the like, but it turns out they are records of various measurements dating back centuries. The priests have studied these to find regularities in the weather, and can now predict it with such accuracy that it's common for people to check the shrine's announcement board every morning to learn whether they need to carry umbrellas."
msgstr ""

#: shard_types/mq_scholars_hub/shard_types_mq_scholars_hub.gd:22
msgid "Complete a settler quest for a %s."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: shard_types/mq_scholars_hub/shard_type_mq_scholars_hub.tres
msgid "The Scholastic Hub"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: shard_name_override
#: shard_types/mq_scholars_hub/shard_type_mq_scholars_hub.tres
msgid "Chiejima"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: shard_name_override_meaning
#: shard_types/mq_scholars_hub/shard_type_mq_scholars_hub.tres
msgid "Sagacity Island"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_scholars_hub/shard_type_mq_scholars_hub.tres
msgid "$shard_name was founded with the explicit aim of translating the ancient language of early scripture. One of the first breakthroughs came when a scholar realized that unlike in the modern language, the ancients put verbs before nouns. Another hurdle was cleared when similar passages from different eras were compared, and the evolution of each glyph was traced and extrapolated. This was enough to start understanding the texts, but the scholars went further, and were able to reconstruct the sounds of the language by referencing holy chants preserved over the centuries, which the priests by now considered to be sacred sounds unrelated to human language."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_scholars_hub/shard_type_mq_scholars_hub.tres
msgid "With their translation task accomplished, the scholars of $shard_name gained much prestige and attracted the best minds of the Cloud Sea. These newcomers brought records from their home shards, and soon $shard_name housed the most complete library in recorded history. Aside from holy texts and historical records, the library now houses recipes, blueprints, and even transcriptions of local songs from countless shards."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_scholars_hub/shard_type_mq_scholars_hub.tres
msgid "When the scholars ran out of things to record, they turned their intellect towards studying unexplained phenomena. The most notable of these is the humble gate. Since time immemorial, gates have been taken for granted, but no one has ever been able to explain how they worked. Now, with modern scientific methods, scholars are studying the mechanics of teleportation. So far the most significant finding is that objects passing through a gate always come out colder, which may suggest that thermal energy is consumed to facilitate the movement. While this remains an active topic of research, there's little hope of being able to construct new gates in the foreseeable future."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_scholars_hub/shard_type_mq_scholars_hub.tres
msgid ""
"I stayed at the research complex, where they usually host visiting scholars. One of the deans took me under her wing and showed me all around the campus.\n"
"\n"
"When she took me to the library, I couldn't even see where the rows of shelves ended. They have the writings all organized, and the librarian taught me how to use the index to find things by topic, date, author, or shard in minutes. I found several atlases with maps of shards I'd never heard of, and the scribes were kind enough to make copies of them for me to take home.\n"
"\n"
"With the dean's encouragement, I attended a seminar to see the kind of things they teach. This one was taught by a printer who explained that their studio had invented a way of printing text that uses many small woodblocks, with a single glyph each, which they move around to create different texts.\n"
"\n"
"Finally, as I was about to leave I saw a group of scholars doing experiments on gates. They dug one up and turned it horizontally, supported by four pillars. Then they dropped iron weights through it and recorded how fast they were going when they popped out of the other gate. They seemed sure that they'll figure out how gates work pretty soon."
msgstr ""

#: shard_types/mq_teo_abode/shard_type_mq_teo_abode.gd:33
msgid "Dedicate the Enlightened One's statue on this shard."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: shard_types/mq_teo_abode/shard_type_mq_teo_abode.tres
msgid "The Enlightened One's Abode"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: shard_name_override
#: shard_types/mq_teo_abode/shard_type_mq_teo_abode.tres
msgid "Onogorojima"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: shard_name_override_meaning
#: shard_types/mq_teo_abode/shard_type_mq_teo_abode.tres
msgid "Self Coalescing Island"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_teo_abode/shard_type_mq_teo_abode.tres
msgid "$shard_name has the honor of hosting the grand statue through which the Enlightened One's blessing flows into the New World. The town with the statue, $monastery_town, is entirely dedicated to its care and maintenance, with all but a few of the residents being monks. Other towns have a few more lay residents, but since $shard_name is the center of the monastic order, much of the infrastructure is dedicated to supporting the monks and helping pilgrims reach $monastery_town safely."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_teo_abode/shard_type_mq_teo_abode.tres
msgid "When the Scribe declared that the time of passivity had passed, the monks quickly sprung into action. The prayer halls were turned into glyph research hubs, and scholars began producing treatises and commentaries on the old texts. The priesthood organization evolved too, as new teachers arose to lead factions focused on studying different types of essence. These masters now wear special robes and carry gold scepters adorned with relics to mark their station, and pilgrims regularly arrive to beg for their blessing."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_teo_abode/shard_type_mq_teo_abode.tres
msgid "As the revelation that the Enlightened One's spirit is fractured resounded around the world, the priesthood has adapted once again. The Order of Growth now maintains the grounds. Gone are the glyph-covered pillars surrounding the statue, and in their place are vines which slowly climb up the Enlightened One's stone body. The monks hope that these vines will channel the divine blessing throughout the shard and beyond, helping the fragments of the Enlightened One's spirit connect and unify."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_teo_abode/shard_type_mq_teo_abode.tres
msgid ""
"First thing I did was visit the boundary between the two united shards. It's a bit of a wasteland, with sharp cliffs marking the border, but it's already healing. There's even vegetation growing from the cracks. I reckon in a generation or two, it'll be good as new.\n"
"\n"
"As for the child, I asked the monks at the Abode, and they were ecstatic. They launched a shard-wide search, and it only took a few weeks for them to return with two dozen children. Then another week to figure out which of them was the right one and to deal with the parents. I've brought the kid back as instructed."
msgstr ""

#: shard_types/mq_wild_monastery/shard_type_mq_wild_monastery.gd:26
msgid "No shard capital."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: shard_types/mq_wild_monastery/shard_type_mq_wild_monastery.tres
msgid "The Holy Wilds"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: shard_name_override
#: shard_types/mq_wild_monastery/shard_type_mq_wild_monastery.tres
msgid "Dōbutsu no Mori"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: shard_name_override_meaning
#: shard_types/mq_wild_monastery/shard_type_mq_wild_monastery.tres
msgid "Animal Forest"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_wild_monastery/shard_type_mq_wild_monastery.tres
msgid "When $shard_name was first settled, the aim was to learn to live in harmony with nature. No grand fortresses were erected, and no shard-wide industries were established. In fact, the only building of note on this shard is the Monastery of the Wilds, where monks invite wild beasts to roam the grounds, seeking to commune with the spirit of the Enlightened One dwelling within them."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_wild_monastery/shard_type_mq_wild_monastery.tres
msgid "The monks of $shard_name use animals to guide much of what they do. When new paths need to be established, they look for animal trails to widen. When wells are to be dug, they follow wild dogs until they start digging holes around dry riverbeds. Even their eating habits are inspired by the local fauna, so when the bears settle down to hibernate for the winter, the monks start their seasonal fast, consuming nothing but water and dried seeds."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_wild_monastery/shard_type_mq_wild_monastery.tres
msgid "The sacred rites of the order are aimed at growing close to the animals around them, so they have long abandoned the old holy chants, and instead pray by mimicking animal sounds, and trying to harmonize with birdsongs. The custom of holy relics has similarly been adapted, with the most treasured artifacts consisting of the shed antlers of a particularly famous deer and the feathers of an owl that had spent its whole life on monastery grounds and had been named an honorary monk."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_wild_monastery/shard_type_mq_wild_monastery.tres
msgid "Today, the monks have adopted the scientific method of the age. They have thoroughly studied bird migration patterns, and even confirmed that some birds regularly use gates to travel between shards. They have also enhanced the balance of the local ecosystem, importing pollinators best suited to the local flora, and adopting animals whose habitats are no longer sustainable on their home shards."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/mq_wild_monastery/shard_type_mq_wild_monastery.tres
msgid ""
"The few towns on this shard seemed to have more animals than people. The first town I visited had strange birds the size of a child walking around the street. When I got close to one it seemed completely unafraid. I checked into an inn that night, and my room had a separate cubby in case I brought along an animal companion.\n"
"\n"
"When I finally reached the Monastery of the Wilds, the monks introduced me to several of the animals that live on the temple grounds. Each of them has their own monastic title, and some even lead meditation classes. The best part, though, was when the monks took me out into the wilderness and showed me the giant beasts they brought from another shard. These are colossal, as big as a house, with tusks and strange, long noses that they use almost like hands. I've never seen anything like them!"
msgstr ""

#: shard_types/newspaper/shard_type_newspaper.gd:41
#: shard_types/newspaper/shard_type_newspaper.gd:42
msgid "At least %d <spot_upgrade:%s> <term_lower:spot_upgrade>s."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: shard_types/newspaper/shard_type_newspaper.tres
msgid "The Paper Distributors"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: shard_name_override
#: shard_types/newspaper/shard_type_newspaper.tres
msgid "Kami no Kuni"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: shard_name_override_meaning
#: shard_types/newspaper/shard_type_newspaper.tres
msgid "Paper Country"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/newspaper/shard_type_newspaper.tres
msgid "In the early days, the temples on $shard_name sought to spread the teachings of the Enlightened One to every household. They set monks to the tedious task of copying the holy text, and freely gave out copies to temple visitors. Junior scribes, finding their wrists sore and their sleeves empty, began a side business. They copied the daily postings from village notice boards and sold them as single-page news broadsheets. People could buy a broadsheet and read a month's worth of posting without having to visit the notice board every day."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/newspaper/shard_type_newspaper.tres
msgid "With reading material suddenly cheap and plentiful, few of the inhabitants remain illiterate. Seeing a willing market, private publishers have begun producing weekly lists of auspicious glyphs and newly invented invocations alongside the traditional holy texts. This has become a big worry for the priests, afraid that these unauthorized claims will dilute the true doctrine. It usually goes unsaid that they are perhaps equally worried about having competition with much more appealing offerings."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/newspaper/shard_type_newspaper.tres
msgid "Hand-copying has given way to carved woodblocks, and broadsheets are printed by the hundreds in dedicated studios. While most still have a small section on auspicious glyphs, much of the space is dedicated to market prices, job postings, and town council edicts. To keep the news fresh, studios even hire couriers to run bundles of papers between settlements. This new boom has caused $shard_name's paper mills immense prosperity. Entire forests of mulberry have been planted just to keep up with the demand, at the cost of eliminating much of the native brushland plant life."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/newspaper/shard_type_newspaper.tres
msgid "Today, the studios have mastered color illustrations, and the papers regularly announce touring plays and sumo matches with vibrant, block-printed portraits of the participants. Yet the most popular section remains the back page, where an anonymous mountain hermit gives blunt interpersonal advice to heart-broken lovers and bickering siblings. The industry did suffer one notable hiccup, however, when a careless printer swapped one character, causing the $town1 paper to announce that a colony of demons had devastated a local field, rather than carrying the intended news of a wild rabbit incursion."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/newspaper/shard_type_newspaper.tres
msgid ""
"When I stepped through the gate, a young girl shoved a printed broadsheet into my hands and demanded I pay for it, which I did. The front page had a critical tea house review and a list of rice prices in every settlement. The back page declared that 猫 was today's auspicious glyph, and had a section where Master Abe, a famous local hermit, advised a reader to leave his wife because she argued against him in front of his boss.\n"
"\n"
"I had dinner at the inn the first night, and found that half of the patrons had their noses buried in the paper, occasionally scoffing or nodding. When I asked to take my leftover food with me, the innkeeper ended up wrapping it in one of these broadsheets too.\n"
"\n"
"My trip also included a detour to visit $town1. At some point a misprint in the paper announced that demons (rather than rabbits) invaded a field, and the locals have fully embraced the joke. The local shrine offers rabbit-warding charms, and the bakeries sell spicy steamed buns shaped like angry rabbits."
msgstr ""

#: shard_types/past_run.gd:29
msgid "another town"
msgstr ""

#: shard_types/poets/shard_type_poets.gd:18
msgid "A %s <term_lower:glyph> in the <term_lower:card_deck>."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: shard_types/poets/shard_type_poets.tres
msgid "The Inkless Lands"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: shard_name_override
#: shard_types/poets/shard_type_poets.tres
msgid "Uta Gatari no Oka"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: shard_name_override_meaning
#: shard_types/poets/shard_type_poets.tres
msgid "Sung Tales Hill"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/poets/shard_type_poets.tres
msgid "Among the original settlers of $shard_name was a tribe of illiterate storytellers who maintained an oral tradition preserving songs and stories from before the Shattering. Their remarkable memory was due to a system of mnemonic poems used to ensure important details remain consistent through retellings. These settlers established a culture focused on spoken language, dismissive of the written word."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/poets/shard_type_poets.tres
msgid "The poems of the storytellers spread quickly among the population, but their use far exceeded their original practical purpose. Instead, the beauty and conciseness of the words became revered, and poets throughout the shard competed to pack the most profound meaning into the fewest syllables. The most masterful of these new compositions have often been adopted by the community as common proverbs, their authors forgotten."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/poets/shard_type_poets.tres
msgid "The culture of poetry on $shard_name is no longer limited to artists. Mochi pounders recite poems to maintain rhythm as they pound rice. Merchants compose poems to advertise their wares. Even laws here are written in verse, ensuring that magistrates can easily memorize them."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/poets/shard_type_poets.tres
msgid "The poetic tradition is still used as a historical record on $shard_name. Every town maintains a generational epic, and each decade the town's greatest poets collaborate to compose a verse that best summarizes recent history. Personal history is maintained in a similar fashion. In addition to common names each of the locals has a \"story name\", a verse they compose over the course of their life, adding syllables after each significant life event they experience. This includes death, as it is custom for a person on their deathbed to add three final syllables to summarize their life experience."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/poets/shard_type_poets.tres
msgid ""
"My first experience on this shard was getting lost on my way to the inn. There are no written signs anywhere, so I had to ask one of the locals for directions. He came up with a funny verse on the spot that used homophones for directions as rhymes. I don't remember the exact wording, since I ended up listening to so much other poetry.\n"
"\n"
"Later, I shared a pot of tea with an old woman who explained their \"story name\" custom. It's a verse that works like a brief biography. Hers mentioned her first job as a messenger and incorporated the names of all her grandchildren. She mentioned that she already had the final three syllables picked out to reveal on her deathbed, but these are always kept secret until the time comes.\n"
"\n"
"I can't say I became a poet from this trip, but I brought back several of their most famous poems and picked up a few tricks for choosing the right words."
msgstr ""

#: shard_types/religion/shard_type_religion.gd:33
msgid ""
"A shrine or temple <term_lower:spot_upgrade> in each settlement. One of:[ul]\n"
"%s[/ul]"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: shard_types/religion/shard_type_religion.tres
msgid "The Hallowed Lands"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: shard_name_override
#: shard_types/religion/shard_type_religion.tres
msgid "Junrei no Hayashi"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: shard_name_override_meaning
#: shard_types/religion/shard_type_religion.tres
msgid "Pilgrimage Grove"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/religion/shard_type_religion.tres
msgid "The settlements of $shard_name function less like independent towns and more like the scattered dormitories of a single, shard-wide monastery. To walk down a street here is to navigate an obstacle course of roadside shrines, spirit statues, and incense burners. It is a place of profound peace, mostly because everyone is too busy praying."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/religion/shard_type_religion.tres
msgid "Construction on $shard_name is a test of piety, not engineering. Thick walls are considered offensive to earth spirits, a sign of distrust in the land's benevolence. As a result, roofs here are undeniably holy, but when the rains come, they also tend to become holey. Furthermore, moving into a new home requires a priest's blessing, a ceremony so lengthy and costly that many families spend more on the blessing than on the construction itself."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/religion/shard_type_religion.tres
msgid "Commerce here is viewed with pity. Production and trade are disdained as obsessions with transient things that distract from the eternal. The temples collect weekly dues to relieve the citizens of this spiritual burden. Consequently, the granaries are often empty, but the temples are filled with gilded relics and rare incense."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/religion/shard_type_religion.tres
msgid "Social friction on $shard_name is non-existent. Disagreement is considered a \"spiritual impurity\", a sickness of the soul. The prescribed cure is a week of fasting, so consensus is usually achieved by lunchtime. The faith so widespread among $shard_name's inhabitants does have benefits, though. When a contagious fever arrived in $town1, the people did not panic. They simply stayed inside and chanted for weeks, accidentally inventing quarantine."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/religion/shard_type_religion.tres
msgid ""
"Almost everyone on this shard is a monk or nun. They don't all dress in robes, but that makes little difference. Anytime I tried to strike up a conversation, we somehow ended up talking about the transience of this floating world or the blessings of the local spirits.\n"
"\n"
"Every little village has several shrines, each dedicated to a different spirit, and filled with holy relics which they rotate regularly depending on the phase of the moon. Many people I've met keep little books of all the relics they've seen, and go on regular pilgrimages all over the shard to add stamps to these books."
msgstr ""

