#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: shard_types/river/shard_type_river.tres
msgid "The Land of the Flow"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: shard_name_override
#: shard_types/river/shard_type_river.tres
msgid "Ōkawajima"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: shard_name_override_meaning
#: shard_types/river/shard_type_river.tres
msgid "Big River Island"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/river/shard_type_river.tres
msgid "$shard_name's many river settlements suffered great damage when the river first flooded. Since then, they have rebuilt their homes on tall, thick stilts. Nowadays floods are celebrated in the highly anticipated River Lantern Festival, an occasion to let past mistakes wash away and start the season with a clean slate."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/river/shard_type_river.tres
msgid "The waterways of $shard_name have become so fundamental to daily life, that \"Downstream\" has all but replaced \"North\". Downstream, however, is more than just a direction. The folks living by the edges of the shard, where the water spills out into the Cloud Sea, are a distinct social class. The water that reaches them is murky, often in shades of indigo or madder from the textile mills Upstream, unfit for agriculture. These \"junkers\" usually earn their living by salvaging and repurposing the flotsam that flows down the river."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/river/shard_type_river.tres
msgid "The scholars of $river_town have developed a philosophy inspired by the constant movement of the stream. Just as the river never stops moving, so should a person continue to change throughout their life. Just as water flows around obstacles, so should a person avoid direct conflict. Instead of tradition and stability, innovation is treasured. Instead of brute force or sudden insight, patience and persistence are prized above all."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/river/shard_type_river.tres
msgid "The old capital of $shard_name is now little more than a tourist attraction. Much of the shard's government has been moved to an immense, interconnected flotilla of rafts and wide-bellied river ships. This floating bureaucracy drifts slowly from the source to the edge, collecting taxes and resolving disputes, before being slowly rowed back Upstream when the river's flow slows down."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/river/shard_type_river.tres
msgid ""
"When I first made my way to the capital using our old maps, I found a sleepy little place. The locals directed me to the river instead. A few days later, a grand flotilla of rafts passed by, and they took me aboard. Apparently, the whole shard is managed from those rafts.\n"
"\n"
"We passed by most of the settlements on the shard while drifting down the river, though I was told that there are towns further inland, reachable from the river by canals. These canals lead to artificial lakes around which those towns are built.\n"
"\n"
"When we reached the edge of the shard, I got to meet the \"junkers\" who live there. Friendly fellows. They've got a big market selling things they've scavenged from the river. They have these sealed jars filled with random items that you buy blindly. The one I got was mostly filled with driftwood, but it also had this coin with a carp on it, which I brought back as a memento."
msgstr ""

#: shard_types/seasonal_migration/shard_type_seasonal_migration.gd:18
msgid "At least %d settlements in a %s."
msgstr ""

#: shard_types/seasonal_migration/shard_type_seasonal_migration.gd:19
msgid "At least %d different settlements in a %s."
msgstr ""

#: shard_types/seasonal_migration/shard_type_seasonal_migration.gd:20
msgid "All settlements <term_lower:connect_settlement>ed to the <term_lower:capital>."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: name
#: shard_types/seasonal_migration/shard_type_seasonal_migration.tres
msgid "The Migratory Circuit"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: shard_name_override
#: shard_types/seasonal_migration/shard_type_seasonal_migration.tres
msgid "Sunabokori Iwa"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: shard_name_override_meaning
#: shard_types/seasonal_migration/shard_type_seasonal_migration.tres
msgid "Dusty Rock"
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/seasonal_migration/shard_type_seasonal_migration.tres
msgid "The settlement of $shard_name began as a gamble against the harsh winds and ever-present dust. The first settlers discovered that the ancient ruins here held immense treasures of the past, but for half the year, scouring winds blew hard enough to strip the paint off a wagon in minutes. Rather than fleeing, they reinforced their homes in $dry_town with thick, windowless walls towards the wind, agreeing on a shared custody with the storms."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/seasonal_migration/shard_type_seasonal_migration.tres
msgid "Not long after the founding expedition ended, the inhabitants of $shard_name established a new custom. During the windy season, they would journey to the green, safe towns like $wet_town, then return after the worst of the dust storms had passed. To survive this journey, these people have turned road building into an art. These are no mere dirt tracks, but paved, lantern-lit highways engineered to withstand centuries of abuse. During migration seasons, the road becomes a moving city in itself, teeming with carts, caravans, and camps."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/seasonal_migration/shard_type_seasonal_migration.tres
msgid "This dual life created a peculiar domestic culture on $shard_name. Because they could not carry everything with them, settlers learned to leave their homes fully stocked for their return. A unique trust developed: you might leave your door unlocked, knowing the only thing that might enter is the wind. Over time, dwellings turned into communal property. Returning families would settle in the first house they encountered, leaving their old home for others to occupy. In fact, the concept of \"home\" itself shifted from a building to one's baggage and one's travel companions. Visitors are often unsettled by the sight of $dry_town in the off-season: a pristine, silent metropolis where the only movement is the dust blowing through the streets."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/seasonal_migration/shard_type_seasonal_migration.tres
msgid "Today, the people of $shard_name are possessed by a rhythmic restlessness. They are the best logistical planners in the Cloud Sea, able to pack an entire community into crates in under two days. Their architecture is aerodynamic, their furniture is collapsible, and their road network is legendary."
msgstr ""

#. TRANSLATORS: Property: text
#: shard_types/seasonal_migration/shard_type_seasonal_migration.tres
msgid ""
"I was lucky to arrive on this shard during what they call migration season. I quickly joined the endless flow of caravans and was welcomed without a question. As we traveled towards the \"dry\" cities, my companions explained how the entire population of the shard migrates between cities every season.\n"
"\n"
"At one point we passed a restaurant, but one unlike any I'd seen before. The whole building was set on hundreds of wheels, moving along with the flow of traffic. The food was pretty good too.\n"
"\n"
"When we finally arrived, it was as if the city was waking up from hibernation. One day a street was completely deserted, and the next day it was bustling with workshops and offices. I suppose when you've spent your whole life traveling, you can unpack a workshop in your sleep."
msgstr ""

